dditional compliance documentation step triggered automatically.\n- However, if well-designed, it could REDUCE the manual burden on developers who currently have to remember to update docs before releases. The automation aspect is genuinely helpful for prototyping speed in the long run — you don't stop prototyping to hand-update a compliance document.\n- The risk: over-engineering at the start. \"Governance system\" language suggests bureaucracy. If this becomes a heavyweight framework, it will slow down prototyping rather than help it.\n- The dataset versioning + change tracking piece is genuinely useful for prototyping — being able to say \"this model was trained on dataset v3.2, here's what changed from v3.1\" is valuable even outside compliance.\n\n**Critical concerns:**\n- \"Real-time updating\" — in practice, documentation updates lag behind code by design (you need a review step). Claiming real-time is misleading and sets up an impossible engineering target.\n- \"Automated audits... synchronize these records with Recital 71 requirements\" — this is the weakest claim. Regulating what \"sufficient\" compliance looks like in automated form is extremely hard. You'd be building a rule engine that interprets legal text, which is brittle and requires constant maintenance as interpretation evolves.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (a) version control + change tracking for code/datasets (mature tech, easy), and (b) automated compliance validation against regulatory requirements (hard, novel, fragile).\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThe core engineering idea — CI/CD-triggered documentation updates with dataset versioning — is technically sound and I'd build it. But the proposal as framed overpromises on the \"automated audit\" and \"real-time\" aspects, which are where the real complexity and risk lie. The \"governance system\" framing suggests this might balloon into a bureaucracy project rather than staying lean engineering tooling.\n\nFor prototyping speed: if 