esn't specify a concrete prototype deliverable or timeline. It's framed as \"explore\" — which from my perspective means uncertain output and uncertain timeline.\n- Cryptographic proofs of execution is an extremely hard problem (related to zk-proofs, TEEs) with no clear path to production deployment in the near term for general AI system logging. This is a decade-long research area being applied to what is fundamentally an infrastructure plumbing problem.\n- The risk: we spend 2-3 years on elegant research while the actual compliance deadline (August 2026 for Annex III, August 2027 for Annex I) requires us to have working logging in production systems NOW.\n- \"Prohibitive storage costs\" — this is a real concern at scale but standard engineering solutions (tiered storage, log rotation, sampling, compression) address this without needing cryptographic proofs of execution.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my perspective as a development engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this proposal is misaligned with what Article 12 actually demands in the near term. The regulation calls for automatic recording of events — that's infrastructure work, not research. While the long-term research direction is intellectually interesting, committing scarce implementation resources to a \"research track\" on cryptographic proofs of execution when we need working logging pipelines deployed is a poor allocation. The actual engineering problem (structured event logging with appropriate retention, access controls, and queryability) can be prototyped in weeks, not years.\n\nHowever, there IS merit: the privacy-preserving angle (especially for Annex III biometric systems where input data includes sensitive personal information) is genuinely important, and \"compressed telemetry\" could yield practical tools faster than \"cryptographic proofs of execution.\" But bundling both into one vague research initiative dilutes the actionable parts.\n\nI think this should b