:**\n- The proposal doesn't propose a concrete prototype. It's exploratory research without a clear path to a shippable component.\n- \"We will develop new probe-based logging techniques\" — the word \"new\" suggests this isn't an adaptation of existing tooling but genuine R&D with uncertain timelines.\n- No mention of what system architecture it would integrate into, no mention of latency requirements, storage constraints, or how it interacts with the existing logging stack.\n- The scope is undefined: \"complex neural networks\" could mean anything from a small CNN to a massive transformer-based model with hundreds of billions of parameters.\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires (from my engineering perspective):**\n- Art 12(1): Technical capability for automatic event recording over system lifetime — this is an implementation requirement, not a research question. You need logging infrastructure that works.\n- Art 12(2): Logging must cover risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring — these are functional requirements with clear enough scope to prototype against.\n- Art 12(3) (for Annex III point 1(a) systems): Specific data points required (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, verification person IDs) — very concrete, immediately implementable.\n\nThe proposal doesn't address any of the concrete requirements in a way that leads to a prototype. It's positioned as fundamental research into what \"events\" mean in neural networks. While intellectually interesting, from my vantage point:\n- I cannot commit implementation resources to something whose output specification is still an open question.\n- There's no clear path from this research to a working logging module I can test and ship.\n- The practical engineering requirements of Art 12(3) don't require me to understand \"unstable latent space\" — they require me to log specific data points (timestamps, database references, input matches, verifier IDs). Those are straightforward engineering tasks.\n- Even for the more general Art 12(2)(a)-(c), I can prototype reasonable logging solutions based on current understanding without needing this fundamental research.\n\nThis proposal is interesting as long-term R&D but doesn't clear my bar for committing scarce implementation resources. It's a research question, not an engineering deliverable with a prototyp path.\n",
