lopment work to do audits, reducing headcount for prototyping.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Peer review of technical documentation does have merit for catching errors in log schemas, ensuring completeness of recorded fields (e.g., start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, person identification as required by Article 12(3)).\n- But the proposal is vague on implementation: what tools? What format? How do you \"review project logs against specific requirements\" - manually? With checklists? Automated validation scripts?\n- Without specifying the technical mechanism (e.g., automated log schema validators, CI/CD pipeline checks for logging completeness), this remains a bureaucratic process rather than an engineering solution.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability and prototyping speed/quality), this proposal is primarily a governance/culture initiative dressed up as a technical response. It doesn't propose any concrete engineering artifact or technical mechanism. The monthly audit cycle, while well-intentioned, would consume scarce engineering time on process rather than building the actual automatic logging infrastructure that Article 12(1) demands. The \"technical documentation review\" aspect is useful but generic - it's not specific to AI system development prototyping workflows.\n\nHowever, I should note that some peer review of documentation quality does help ensure that when we do build logging systems, they're documented properly for compliance. But the proposal as stated doesn't address the core technical challenge and would add overhead to our development cycle.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject or low-approve. It's not harmful per se, but from my specific vantage point of \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping,\" this is a process that adds friction without proposing any technical artifact. The monthly cadence could be reasonable as part of a broader compliance program, but standing alone