tion from training logs\" part needs rework, having a standardized template structure saves time on every project. The cost is low (schema design + tooling). The benefit compounds with each additional high-risk system we develop.\n\nBut the framing issue bothers me — it's positioned as solving Article 12 when it really addresses more of the Article 11 documentation side, and the Model Cards basis doesn't cleanly map to per-use event logging requirements. This could mislead resource allocation if leadership thinks \"we've handled Article 12\" when we haven't built the runtime logging infrastructure.\n\nI'll approve it but with a moderate promise score reflecting that it's useful but not as comprehensive or well-aligned as presented. Maybe around 55-62. It addresses a real efficiency need (standardized docs reduce per-project overhead) and is low-cost to implement, which aligns with my resource-allocation mandate. But the premise gaps keep it from being higher.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 58.\n",
