es the \"technical documentation\" aspect of Recital 71 but doesn't fully address the logging capability requirement of Article 12(1)-(3).\n- The proposal says it \"replaces static PDF reports with dynamic archives\" - regulators may still expect certain formats for submission. However, this is more about internal management than regulatory submission format.\n- The \"mandatory triggers for code deployment\" is a good control but could create operational friction that leads to workarounds if not properly integrated into existing CI/CD pipelines.\n- It addresses the documentation aspect well but doesn't mention the logging capabilities required by Article 12(2) (recording events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring).\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports compliance with Recital 71's documentation requirements and partially supports Article 12. It's a foundational infrastructure piece that would be necessary regardless of other proposals. From an institutional survival perspective, having proper version-controlled technical documentation is essential for:\n- Demonstrating compliance to national competent authorities during audits\n- Responding to post-market monitoring requests (Article 72)\n- Maintaining market reputation as a responsible AI developer\n- Enabling traceability when issues arise\n\nThe proposal is well-targeted at a specific regulatory requirement. It's not overreaching, it's implementable, and it creates an enforceable control (mandatory triggers). The Git-based approach is practical for an R&D institution.\n\nOne concern: the proposal frames this as replacing \"static PDF reports\" - I need to be careful that the institution still produces documentation in formats acceptable to regulators when requested. But as an internal system, a Git-based portal with export capabilities would be fine.\n\nAnother concern: The proposal says it \"guarantees\" documentation remains up to date. In comp