quiring new infrastructure.\n- The immutable database is a reasonable choice - likely you'd use existing secure storage or a modest addition to current data infrastructure.\n- The interceptor pattern means minimal disruption to existing API endpoints and workflows.\n\n**Regulatory fit with Article 12:**\n- Captures timestamps (relevant to Art 12(3)(a) for Annex III systems).\n- Input hashes relate to input data tracking (Art 12(3)(c)).\n- Model versions support traceability of the system's configuration.\n- However, it doesn't explicitly cover all of Art 12(3): reference database against which input was checked (b), identification of natural persons in verification (d). But as a middleware layer, some of these might be handled at different layers or might not apply to all systems.\n- The \"automatic recording\" requirement of Art 12(1) is directly addressed by the middleware approach.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Is the \"primary serving framework\" truly universal across all lab systems? If some research prototypes use different frameworks, this might not cover everything. But the proposal says \"wrapper around our existing API endpoints,\" which suggests it's designed to work at a common layer.\n- Resource commitment: building and maintaining this middleware is a non-trivial engineering effort, but it's a one-time investment that pays off across all projects. This is a classic infrastructure play that makes sense for an R&D institution.\n- The proposal is scoped reasonably - it doesn't overreach into trying to solve every logging requirement, just the core automatic event recording.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, efficient use of resources. It addresses a real regulatory need (automatic logging per Art 12(1)) in a way that maximizes reuse across the lab's R&D portfolio while minimizing disruption to scientific work. It leverages existing infrastructure rather than building new systems. The resource-allocation logic is sound: one mi