eriment tracking tools reduces implementation friction and adoption resistance.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal mentions \"input hashes\" but Article 12(3)(c) specifically requires \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" - hashing might not be sufficient; you may need the actual data or at least retrievable references. This could be a compliance gap.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention logging of events relevant for \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification\" (Art 12(2)(a)). While it captures timestamps and input hashes, the framework needs to be designed around *what* events are logged for risk identification.\n- \"Absolute regulatory compliance\" is an overstatement - no system guarantees absolute compliance. But from my perspective as a Compliance Officer, I'd note this as marketing language rather than a substantive flaw in the proposal itself.\n- The proposal doesn't mention log retention periods, access controls, or data protection considerations (GDPR intersection) for logging personal data (e.g., \"identification of the natural persons involved\" under Art 12(3)(d)).\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - the proposal needs to ensure logs persist and remain accessible throughout the entire operational life, not just during active development.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThis is a strong, directly relevant proposal that addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) and provides infrastructure for meeting Articles 12(2) and potentially 12(3). A unified, mandatory approach is exactly what compliance requires - you need consistent, auditable logging across all high-risk systems. The fact that it's \"mandatory middleware\" means no team can opt out or implement a sub-standard version. This directly serves institutional survival (avoiding regulatory penalties), regulatory compliance (meeting the \"shall\" requirements), and market reputation (demonstrating robust internal controls).\n\nHowever, I should note some gaps:\n- Input hashes vs. actual input data for matches (Art 12(3)(c))\n- No explicit mention of risk-event logging design (Art 12(2)(a))\n- No mention of log retention/accessibility over full system lifetime\n- No GDPR/data protection considerations for personal data in logs\n\nThese are implementation details that can be addressed during development, but the overall architecture and approach is sound and directly aligned with what Article 12 demands. The core insight - build a unified, mandatory logging layer rather than letting each team figure it out independently - is exactly right from a compliance standpoint.\n\nI would approve this proposal. It addresses the fundamental requirement of Article 12 in the most effective way possible: centralized, automated, mandatory, and integrated with existing tools. The gaps I identified are refinements that should be specified during implementation but don't undermine the core value of the proposal.\n\nPromise score: This is a high-value proposal from my perspective. It directly addresses the \"shall\" requirement in Article 12(1), provides the infrastructure for 12(2) and 12(3), reduces compliance risk through standardization, and is operationally feasible by integrating with existing tools. The gaps are real but addressable during implementation. I'd say around 85-90. Let me settle on 87 - it's very strong but has some specific gaps that need to be addressed in the implementation spec (input data vs hashes for matches, explicit risk-event logging design, retention policies).\n",
