road — \"anomalies indicating a substantial modification or emergent risk\" is vague and could balloon in scope. What constitutes a \"suspicious logging pattern\"? This needs tight scoping to avoid resource creep.\n- However, it does leverage existing infrastructure (the log pipeline) and serves a clear compliance purpose tied directly to Article 12(2). It's not a speculative scientific ambition — it has a defined regulatory driver.\n- The cost-benefit is reasonable: if we're already building the logging system (mandatory), adding an alerting layer on top is relatively incremental in resource terms, especially compared to the cost of a compliance breach.\n- From an infrastructure reuse standpoint, the dashboard could potentially be built on existing monitoring/observability tooling rather than requiring new infrastructure.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable, bounded engineering project that builds on mandatory infrastructure we'd build anyway. The main risk is scope creep on the ML anomaly detection component — if it becomes an open research problem, it could consume disproportionate resources. But even with just heuristic triggers (no ML), this provides meaningful value for compliance. The proposal as stated includes both heuristics AND ML detectors, which is where I have some reservation about resource commitment.\n\nHowever, looking at it more carefully: the core ask is an alerting dashboard on top of logs we're already generating. That's a well-understood engineering task. The ML component adds complexity but isn't unreasonable for a lab with R&D capability. The proposal doesn't require new infrastructure — it uses the logging pipeline Article 12(1) mandates.\n\nI think this clears my bar as a resource-efficient add-on to mandatory work, with manageable scope if we start with heuristics and layer in ML detectors later. It's not a speculative scientific bet; it has direct regulatory utility. I'd approve but note the scoping risk on the ML component.\n\nP