liant with Art 12(1).\n\n2. **Article 71/Recital requirements**: The recital emphasizes keeping records throughout the lifetime, technical documentation kept up to date, and specific data points (algorithms, training, testing, validation processes, risk-management system). A dashboard for real-time event monitoring doesn't address the comprehensive record-keeping requirement — it's more of a monitoring/alerting tool than a compliance record-keeping system.\n\n3. **\"Primary tool\" overclaim**: The proposal calls this the \"primary tool for post-market monitoring.\" Post-market monitoring under Article 72 is much broader than just watching a dashboard. It involves systematic review, incident reporting (Art 73), cooperation with market surveillance authorities, etc. Reducing it to a dashboard is reductive and could give false comfort.\n\n4. **Annex III point 1(a) specifics**: For biometric identification systems, Article 12(3) requires very specific logging: start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. A generic \"unified dashboard\" doesn't clearly address these specific requirements.\n\n5. **Institutional survival / resource commitment angle**: This is a significant engineering project (telemetry pipeline, aggregation infrastructure, alerting system). From a compliance officer's perspective, committing scarce resources to building this when it doesn't fully satisfy the regulatory requirement (which is about the system's inherent logging capability) is risky. It could create false confidence that compliance is achieved while the actual Article 12(1) technical requirement on the system architecture goes unaddressed.\n\n6. **Market reputation**: Having a monitoring dashboard is good practice and demonstrates diligence to regulators, but if it's presented as THE compliance mechanism for record-keeping, it's misleading. Regulators would expect the logging to be inherent in the system design per Art 12(1),