t architectures. A single API wrapper that works well across all of them without being too abstract is technically challenging. The abstraction layer itself could become a bottleneck or source of friction.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" is aspirational. In practice, integrating logging middleware into existing ML pipelines can be invasive, especially for research code that's rapidly changing. If the library adds overhead to prototyping (even small), it could slow down iteration speed - which contradicts its stated goal.\n- The scope is ambitious: building a production-grade immutable log store + multi-framework abstraction + standardized API is itself a significant engineering project. Before the first prototype benefits, you've committed substantial development resources.\n- However, for an R&D institution that develops multiple high-risk AI systems, the amortized cost makes sense IF the lab will indeed have multiple such projects.\n\n**From speed/quality of prototyping perspective:**\n- The upside is clear: if it works well, every new project gets compliance logging \"for free\" or near-free, saving days per project.\n- The downside risk: if the abstraction is too rigid or the integration is too complex for novel/experimental ML architectures (which R&D labs constantly experiment with), it becomes a tax on prototyping speed rather than an enabler.\n- A middle path might be better: lightweight hooks + configuration rather than a heavy \"middleware library.\" But the proposal as stated is a full middleware layer.\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- Python API wrapper for timestamping and hashing: very doable, low complexity\n- Multi-framework abstraction: moderate to high complexity depending on how deep the integration needs to go\n- Immutable log store: doable (append-only storage, possibly using existing solutions like blockchain-like ledgers or simply WORM storage), but \"secure\" adds requirements around access control, integrity verification\n- The overall architecture is te