irectly conflicts with the balance between scientific ambition and practical throughput.\n- The proposal says \"new institutional standard\" - but do we already have documentation practices? If so, is this a full replacement or an augmentation? Building entirely new systems when existing ones can be adapted would be wasteful.\n- However, linking to version control IS using existing infrastructure effectively.\n- The resource commitment: implementing mandatory protocols across all R&D pipelines is a significant one-time investment plus ongoing maintenance cost. But if done once well, it scales across projects.\n\n**Balancing assessment:**\nThe proposal has strong alignment with what Article 12 requires (documentation of algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes; traceability). The version control linkage is smart for resource efficiency. However, the \"peer-review checkpoints at each stage\" is where I have concerns about scientific throughput and resource allocation. In an R&D environment, adding multiple mandatory gates can be a significant drag on iteration speed.\n\nBut then again - if we're already subject to Article 12 requirements, we NEED some form of documentation at these stages anyway. The question is whether the proposed implementation is efficient or overwrought.\n\nThe proposal is reasonable but somewhat heavy-handed in its \"mandatory\" and \"each stage\" language. A more efficient approach might be risk-proportionate (not every internal experiment needs full peer-review, only things heading toward high-risk deployment). But as a top-level protocol framework, it's not unreasonable.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective:\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (version control) - good\n- Standardization reduces per-project cost at scale - good\n- But \"each stage\" peer review is potentially overkill for early-stage research - concerning\n- The one-time implementation cost is non-trivial but the ongoing cost of templates is manageable\n- It doesn't require significant new funding beyond process engineering\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a sensible, if somewhat heavy, approach to compliance that leverages existing infrastructure (version control) and standardizes processes. The main concern about \"each stage\" peer review could be mitigated in implementation by making the depth of review proportional to the project's risk classification. But as a top-level protocol, it's approvable.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-68. It's solid but not exceptional from my efficiency perspective because of the \"each stage\" mandate which could be over-prescriptive for an R&D lab. The version control linkage saves points from being lower though. Let me settle on 65.\n",
