quire new servers, new SaaS subscriptions, or significant engineering headcount beyond writing conversion scripts.\n- The \"live\" nature means the documentation is always current without a separate maintenance process.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Recital 71 requires documentation to be in \"clear and comprehensive form\" - can auto-generated docs meet that bar? There's a risk of producing technically complete but poorly structured documents that don't satisfy regulatory expectations. However, this is a solvable problem with template design.\n- The proposal says \"comprehensive reports required by Recital 71\" - Recital 71 mentions general characteristics, capabilities and limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes, risk-management system documentation. Some of these (like risk-management system documentation) may not be fully captured in MLflow/W&B metadata alone. The proposal might overstate what can be auto-generated from those sources.\n- \"Live\" documentation for a high-risk system has security considerations - who has access to the generated docs? But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It:\n1. Uses existing infrastructure (MLflow, W&B) → no new capex\n2. Automates a recurring compliance cost → ongoing OPEX savings\n3. Frees researcher time for scientific work → protects ambition within budget\n4. Is implementable as \"scripts\" rather than a large platform project → low risk of scope creep\n5. The \"live\" aspect prevents documentation decay, which is a real problem in R&D\n\nThe main weakness is that not ALL required documentation (risk-management system, full algorithm descriptions) will naturally flow from experiment trackers - some curation and template work will be needed. But the core value proposition (automating the bulk of technical doc generation from data we already capture) is sound.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my perspective. It's efficient, leverages existing assets, and directly addresses a recurring cost center. I'll approve it with a high promise score. Maybe 82-85 range - strong but not perfect because the \"comprehensive\" claim slightly overstates what MLflow/W&B metadata alone can produce (you'd still need risk-management docs, capability/limitation narratives that aren't in experiment logs).\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
