lutions per project. It's a one-time investment with recurring savings.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n1. \"Unified\" and \"standardized... regardless of the specific model architecture\" — this could be overly rigid. Different AI systems (e.g., a large language model vs. a computer vision pipeline for biometric verification) may have fundamentally different event structures that don't map cleanly to one schema. Over-standardization could force awkward abstractions or create bottlenecks when the framework can't accommodate novel architectures.\n2. \"Mandatory for all project pipelines\" — this creates a single point of failure in terms of development velocity. If the shared library has bugs, lacks features, or evolves slowly, every team is blocked. This could stifle scientific ambition if teams need to iterate quickly on research prototypes that don't quite fit the standardized framework.\n3. The engineering team building it — who maintains it long-term? This creates a dependency and potential bottleneck. If the central team is small, it becomes a scaling constraint.\n4. \"Integrated into our existing deployment clusters\" — good for infrastructure reuse, but high-risk AI systems that are still in development/research phase may not be on those clusters yet. The framework needs to work pre-deployment too (for training logs, validation events).\n5. Article 12(3) has very specific minimum requirements for biometric verification systems (Annex III point 1(a)) — the framework must handle these specific data points, which may not align with a \"generic\" logging approach.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses my core concerns about resource efficiency and infrastructure reuse. The centralization argument is compelling for an R&D lab where engineering resources are scarce. The integration into existing deployment clusters shows awareness of not wasting capital on new infrastructure. However, the \"mandatory\" and 