ell-aligned with the regulatory text, reduces operational compliance risk, supports institutional defense in case of incidents or audits, and demonstrates to regulators that we have robust technical measures for record-keeping. The immutability feature is particularly important because if a regulator questions whether logs were modified after an incident, having cryptographic proof of integrity is invaluable.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score — it directly serves my core mandate (regulatory compliance and institutional survival). I'd say around 82-85. It's not a perfect 100 because:\n- The proposal as written emphasizes one aspect (reference database versioning) more than others in Article 12(3)\n- \"Immutable ledger\" has implementation complexity and cost that could be overkill for some use cases\n- The claim of eliminating manual record-keeping entirely is slightly optimistic given 14(5) human verification requirements\n\nBut it's clearly worth pursuing. I'll go with 83.\n",
