umbing.\n- The decorator pattern is low-friction: wrap your inference call, you get logs. Minimal disruption to existing code.\n- However, \"unified\" could become a bottleneck if the middleware layer becomes overly complex or if it introduces performance overhead during rapid prototyping (e.g., logging every intermediate tensor).\n- Risk: building a \"unified\" framework upfront before we know exactly what all our high-risk systems need could lead to over-engineering. But the basic interceptor pattern is simple enough that this risk is manageable.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal says \"all high-risk prototypes\" — but in an R&D lab, prototypes evolve rapidly. A rigid middleware layer might fight against the iterative prototyping process. If I'm rearchitecting a pipeline every two weeks, does the decorator pattern survive?\n- Article 12(3) requires very specific data points (reference database identity, natural person IDs). A generic \"log inputs and outputs\" won't capture those without domain-specific configuration. The proposal doesn't address how it handles the Annex III-specific requirements.\n- Performance overhead: during prototyping, I might be running thousands of inferences per minute for hyperparameter sweeps. If the logging layer adds latency or disk I/O bottlenecks, that slows down my work.\n- \"Sidecar\" terminology suggests a separate process/service — for internal Python libraries at an R&D lab, this is heavier than needed and introduces inter-process communication overhead. A simple decorator or context manager would be more prototyping-friendly.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea (automated logging via decorators/interceptors to remove manual burden) is technically sound and directly beneficial to prototyping speed. It's a practical, implementable solution. The \"unified\" framing adds organizational value. However, the proposal is somewhat generic — it captures inputs/outputs/timestamps but doesn't address how it would hand