e gaps that could lead to regulatory sanctions\n- \"Mandatory for all production-ready models\" establishes clear governance\n- Tamper-proof database addresses integrity concerns that regulators would scrutinize\n- The \"without relying on the inconsistent diligence of individual researchers\" framing is exactly right from a compliance perspective - systemic controls beat individual accountability\n- Addresses Recital 71's call for comprehensive, up-to-date documentation\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's described as \"universal API wrappers\" which might be too rigid - different high-risk systems have very different architectures. A one-size-fits-all middleware could create compliance gaps if it doesn't capture system-specific events relevant to 12(2)(a)-(c)\n- Doesn't explicitly address the Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements in 12(3)\n- \"Input hashes\" alone may not satisfy 12(3)(c) which requires recording the actual input data for matches\n- No mention of Article 72 post-market monitoring integration specifically\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level/abstract - it's an architecture direction rather than a concrete implementation plan with specific compliance mappings\n- \"Lifetime of the system\" retention requirements aren't addressed (data governance, storage duration)\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\nThis is fundamentally sound. A centralized, mandatory logging infrastructure is exactly what a regulator would expect to see as evidence of organizational commitment to compliance. If we get audited and can show every team uses the same standardized logging layer with tamper-proof records, that's a strong defense against non-compliance findings. The risk of NOT having this is significant - if individual researchers handle their own logging, there will inevitably be gaps, and those gaps become evidence of systemic non-compliance under the Act.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\nHaving a documented, standardized compliance frame