g.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- Building a custom framework from scratch (even open-source) will slow down prototyping significantly during the initial build phase. Engineers need to design, implement, test, and debug the framework before it can be used.\n- However, once built, it could accelerate future prototyping by automating provenance capture that would otherwise be manual or ad-hoc.\n- The \"integrate into existing ML pipelines\" part is where friction occurs - every integration point requires engineering time, potentially disrupting ongoing prototyping cycles.\n- There's a question of whether this is building vs. buying/adopting. Open-source tools already exist (MLflow, DVC, W&B) that cover 80%+ of what's described. Building custom adds engineering cost but may offer better integration with specific lab workflows.\n\n**Regulatory Fit Assessment:**\n- The proposal addresses Article 12(1) - automatic recording of events/logs over the lifetime of the system.\n- It partially addresses the \"technical documentation\" requirements mentioned in Recital 71 (algorithms, data, training processes).\n- However, it's somewhat narrow on the operational/inference side. The regulation requires logging of events during *use* (operation), not just during development/training. The proposal focuses heavily on \"training runs\" and \"development lifecycle,\" which is primarily pre-deployment. Article 12(3) specifically requires recording periods of each use, reference databases, input data matches, and identification of verification persons - these are operational/inference-time requirements that the proposal doesn't clearly address.\n- The \"open-source\" aspect adds complexity: maintaining an open-source framework requires ongoing commitment (bug fixes, security patches, community management) which diverts engineering resources from core prototyping work.\n\n**Key Concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Scope creep risk: \"entire AI development lifecycle\" 