tegrate with whatever logging infrastructure we're building for compliance anyway. It's not standalone; it's a consumer of the logging system.\n- The risk: if we build this audit tool before the underlying logging is solid, we're prototyping against a moving target. But as a prototype, you could define expected log schemas and validate against those.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- This is genuinely useful as an internal quality gate. It catches compliance gaps early in development rather than at regulatory review time.\n- However, from my vantage point (technical viability and prototyping speed/quality), the concern is: this is somewhat of a \"wrapper\" tool. The real engineering effort should be on making the logging system itself produce compliant data. An audit dashboard that flags missing fields is useful but is downstream of the core implementation work.\n- There's also a question of scope creep risk: \"cross-references active system logs with the EU AI Act's specific recording requirements\" could balloon into a full regulatory compliance platform rather than staying focused.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is technically viable but somewhat low-leverage as a standalone project. The real value is in ensuring our logging implementation meets Article 12(3) requirements from the start. An audit tool is a verification layer on top of that. It's a reasonable prototyping target — not hard to build a proof-of-concept, but its utility depends heavily on the upstream logging system being well-defined first.\n\nI'd say this is moderately promising. It's technically feasible (not 0), it could be prototyped reasonably quickly (good for speed), and it provides a quality gate (good for quality). But it's somewhat derivative — you're building a checker rather than the thing being checked. The risk is that we spend engineering time on a dashboard while the actual logging implementation lags.\n\nI'll lean toward approve with a moderate promise score. It's