        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1394\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core architecture (compute-cluster wrapper + centralized ledger) is technically sound and the automation-for-adoption rationale is good, but 'logging every epoch's parameters' is a major feasibility red flag: full weight-tensor capture per epoch across all high-risk models would create prohibitive I/O and storage costs without a clear retention policy. More critically, this proposal targets training-time provenance documentation (closer to Annex IV / Article 11 territory) rather than the runtime automatic event recording that Article 12(1)-(3) actually mandates, so it does not directly close the regulatory gap we are screening against; and as a project shape it is a multi-quarter infrastructure build that would consume prototyping capacity rather than accelerate it.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1394 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal: \"Automated Provenance Pipeline\" — an integrated system that automatically captures training metadata, data lineage, and versioning for all high-risk models. It involves implementing an automated wrapper around the compute cluster that logs every epoch's parameters and dataset hashes to a centralized ledger. The stated goal is keeping technical documentation up-to-date without diverting researcher focus from core experimentation.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- Wrapping a compute cluster with logging is technically feasible. Tools like MLflow, Weights & Biases, or custom solutions exist for tracking training runs. Dataset hashing (e.g., content-based hashes) is standard practice. Versioning of models and data is well-trodden territory.\n- However, \"every epoch's parameters\" is a critical detail that raises technical concerns. Logging full parameter tensors every epoch would be enormously expensive in storage and I/O. For large models, this could be terabytes per run. Even logging hyperparameters + loss curves + dataset hashes per epoch is manageable, but \"parameters\" likely means model weights, which is impractical at scale for every single epoch of every run on the cluster.\n- \"Centralized ledger\" — this implies a single point of aggregation. For a large R&D institution with many researchers running parallel experiments, this could become a bottleneck or require significant infrastructure investment. It's feasible but not trivial.\n- The wrapper approach around the compute cluster is reasonable architecturally — it intercepts at the job-submission and execution layer rather than requiring each researcher to manually instrument their code. This is good for adoption.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- The core value proposition here is that it doesn't divert researcher focus from experimentation. This is a strong point for prototyping speed — if compliance logging is automated and transparent, researchers don't have to stop and manually document things.\n- However, building this \"integrated system\" itself requires significant engineering effort: the wrapper, the ledger infrastructure, the data pipeline, error handling, etc. This is not a quick prototype — it's an infrastructure project that could take months.\n- The scope says \"all high-risk models\" which means it needs to handle heterogeneous training pipelines (different frameworks, different data types, distributed training, etc.). That complexity could significantly slow down the build.\n\n**Alignment with Article 12:**\n- Article 12 is primarily about runtime event logging for high-risk AI systems in deployment — automatic recording of events over the system's lifetime, identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, and specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification).\n- This proposal is focused on TRAINING provenance — capturing training metadata, data lineage, versioning. While Recital 71 does mention \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used,\" Article 12's specific requirements are about runtime logging capabilities of the deployed system, not about training documentation capture.\n- There's a misalignment: the proposal addresses technical documentation (which is more Article 11/Annex IV territory) rather than the automatic event recording requirement of Article 12 itself. The \"lifetime\" in Article 12 refers to operational lifetime, not training lifecycle.\n- However, keeping technical documentation up-to-date IS mentioned in Recital 71, and the broader compliance picture does require this. But strictly speaking, this proposal doesn't directly implement what Article 12 mandates (automatic recording of events at runtime).\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- Technical feasibility: The concept is sound but \"every epoch's parameters\" is a red flag for storage/I/O costs that could make it impractical without careful engineering. The wrapper approach is good for adoption.\n- Prototyping speed: Building this as an integrated system across the compute cluster is a significant infrastructure project. It's not something you prototype quickly — it requires understanding all the training frameworks in use, all the data pipelines, and building robust capture at each layer. This could consume a lot of engineering time before delivering value.\n- The promise of \"without diverting researcher focus\" is good but only true once it's fully built and integrated. During build-out, there will be significant friction.\n- Misalignment with Article 12: While useful for compliance broadly, this doesn't directly address the runtime logging that Article 12 requires. It addresses documentation (more Annex IV territory). This means it might not satisfy the specific regulatory requirement we're screening against.\n\n**My decision:**\nFrom my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n- The idea is directionally correct for broader AI governance compliance\n- But it's a large infrastructure project with significant engineering complexity\n- \"Every epoch's parameters\" is technically problematic without further scoping\n- It doesn't directly implement Article 12's runtime logging requirements — it addresses training documentation instead\n- As a prototyping exercise, this is more of a platform/infrastructure build than something that can be rapidly iterated on\n- The technical approach (wrapper + centralized ledger) is reasonable but the scope (\"all high-risk models\") makes it a multi-quarter commitment\n\nI think I'd reject this from my vantage point. It's solving a different problem (training documentation vs. runtime event logging), it's technically heavy with unresolved scaling concerns (full parameter logging per epoch), and it doesn't enable fast prototyping — it IS the prototype/project that consumes engineering time. The promise would be moderate because the underlying idea has merit but the execution scope and alignment issues are significant.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My role is to assess technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. Is this technically viable? Largely yes, with caveats. Does it enable fast prototyping? No — it's itself a large build. Does it address the regulatory text I'm screening against (Article 12)? Only tangentially through Recital 71's mention of training documentation.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n1. It doesn't directly implement Article 12's core requirement (automatic runtime event recording)\n2. \"Every epoch's parameters\" is a technical red flag for feasibility at scale\n3. As an engineering project, it's a heavy infrastructure build that would consume prototyping capacity rather than enable it\n4. The scope (\"all high-risk models\") without framework-agnostic design details suggests significant complexity\n\nPromise: Maybe 35-40. The general direction (automated capture of provenance) is good and the wrapper architecture is sensible, but the specific technical choices raise viability concerns and it misaligns with what Article 12 actually requires in terms of runtime logging.\n",
