gs is a well-studied area in the literature. Secure multi-party computation for audit trails is also explored. The proposal says \"explore how to maintain detailed event logs without compromising privacy\" — but Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification. You can't really apply differential privacy to a log that must identify specific individuals performing verification tasks. The tension the proposal identifies may be more apparent than real: the \"identification of natural persons involved in verification\" is an internal operational record (who performed QA), not a data subject's personal data flowing through the system. GDPR applies, but it's manageable with access controls and retention limits rather than needing exotic cryptographic protocols.\n- The proposal says \"We will publish our findings on balancing transparency with anonymity.\" This suggests an academic output orientation rather than a practical engineering deliverable. From a resource-allocation standpoint, publishing papers doesn't directly help the lab meet its Article 12 compliance obligations.\n- Secure multi-party computation for logging metadata is computationally expensive and adds significant overhead to what should be a relatively lightweight logging infrastructure. For high-risk AI systems that need \"automatic recording of events,\" adding MPC layers could create performance bottlenecks and complexity that undermine the practicality of the logging system itself.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how it connects to the lab's existing R&D infrastructure or how it would leverage current capabilities. It reads as a standalone research exploration.\n- Resource efficiency: This is a \"nice to have\" optimization (making logs privacy-preserving) rather than a \"must-have\" compliance requirement (implementing logging at all). The lab should first ensure basic Article 12 compliance, then consider optimizations. Spending scarce implementation resources on exploratory crypto research before the basic logging infrastructure exists is poor sequencing.\n- The scientific ambition here is high but the funding constraint reality means we need to prioritize. There are likely more immediate, lower-effort ways to handle GDPR in logging (access controls, pseudonymization, retention policies) that don't require novel cryptographic research.\n\n**My assessment:** From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal has a fundamental sequencing problem and an over-engineering risk. It treats what is largely a data governance and access control problem as if it requires novel cryptographic research. The \"tension\" between Article 12(3)(d) (identifying verification personnel) and GDPR is real but manageable with standard techniques (pseudonymization, role-based access, limited retention) without needing differential privacy or MPC on audit trails. Spending scarce implementation resources on exploratory crypto research when the lab doesn't yet have basic logging infrastructure in place is poor resource allocation. The publication-oriented framing further suggests this is more academic curiosity than operational necessity.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge: if the lab's high-risk systems fall under Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification/verification), then Article 12(3)(d) does require identifying natural persons in verification, and if those are data subjects with sensitive data involved, there IS a genuine tension. But even then, standard GDPR techniques handle this without needing novel DP/MPC research.\n\nI'll lean toward reject, with a moderate promise score acknowledging the intellectual interest but flagging the resource misallocation. Maybe a 30-40 range — it's not terrible thinking, and the problem it identifies is real, but as a funded implementation commitment from my vantage point, it's poorly sequenced, over-engineered relative to available solutions, and doesn't leverage existing infrastructure effective