b already has specific systems it needs to make compliant. Building a generic standard before addressing concrete compliance needs inverts the engineering workflow. Better to prototype logging for specific systems first, discover patterns, THEN abstract into a reusable protocol if needed.\n- \"Positions the institution as a primary scientific authority in defining the global standards\" - this is a strategic/PR goal dressed up as an engineering project. From my vantage point, I'm concerned about whether this produces shippable software quickly or becomes another whitepaper exercise.\n- Open-source commitment adds maintenance burden and community management overhead that slows down prototyping velocity.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- \"Common language for AI observability\" is vague. What does interoperability mean here? Is it about data formats (JSON, protobuf)? Event taxonomies? Semantic meaning of events? The proposal doesn't specify enough to be technically actionable as stated.\n- The scope is essentially trying to solve the Byzantine generals problem for logging across heterogeneous systems. In practice, you'd end up with either an overly generic schema that loses important domain-specific information, or a patchwork that isn't truly \"unified.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a prototyping speed and technical viability standpoint, this is a high-risk, low-immediate-ROI proposal. It's a large research effort that doesn't directly produce compliance-ready artifacts for the lab's specific systems. The existing ecosystem (OpenTelemetry + domain-specific extensions) already provides 80% of what's needed, and the last 20% is better solved in the context of actual system implementation rather than as an abstract standardization project.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge there IS some value: if done well, a shared schema could reduce per-system implementation effort over time. But the framing as a \"dedicated research project\" to create a \"standardized protocol\" and position the lab as \"primary scientific authority\" makes this sound more like an academic/strategic play than a practical engineering initiative.\n\nMy decision: This doesn't clear my bar for technical viability AND prototyping speed. The scope is too broad, the timeline is likely too long before producing usable artifacts, it duplicates significant existing work (OpenTelemetry), and the strategic positioning goal isn't really an engineering concern. I'll reject it but give a moderate promise score because the underlying idea (reusable logging abstractions) has some merit if scoped more tightly.\n\nI'd say around 30-35 for promise - the concept isn't crazy, but as framed, it's too ambitious and slow for my function's needs, and the technical risk of achieving true cross-architecture unification is high.\n",
