ally mitigate the compliance risk effectively? Yes — programmatic verification is more reliable than manual spot-checks. It creates an ongoing assurance mechanism rather than a one-time check.\n- Is there a gap? The proposal focuses on Article 12(3) specifically (Annex III point 1(a) systems). It doesn't address Articles 12(1)-(2) logging for other high-risk systems, or the broader technical documentation requirements in Recital 71. But the proposal is scoped to what it says — validating Article 12(3) data points specifically. That's a legitimate, focused contribution.\n- Is this \"exploring whether an idea might someday work\" vs. \"committing implementation resources\"? It sounds like building a concrete testing suite with specific scripts — that's implementation, not exploration. The scope is bounded: trigger events → verify logs match required data points.\n\nThis seems like a solid, well-scoped compliance assurance tool. It directly addresses a hard regulatory requirement and the risk of silent non-compliance. From my vantage point as Compliance Officer protecting institutional survival and regulatory standing, this is valuable.\n\nOne concern: it's specifically for Annex III 1(a) systems. If the lab also develops other high-risk AI systems, the logging verification needs would be broader. But the proposal doesn't claim to cover everything — it covers a specific, clearly defined subset. That's fine as a scoped commitment.\n\nDecision: Approve. This is directly relevant to compliance with Article 12(3), bounded in scope, addresses a real and serious risk (silent logging failures detected only at audit time), and the implementation is concrete rather than exploratory.\n\nPromise score: From a compliance perspective, this is highly valuable but it's one component of a larger assurance program. I'd put it around 78-82. It's clearly useful and well-scoped, but it only covers Article 12(3) for a specific category of systems. I'll go with 80.\n",
