uilds something that's too abstract to handle domain-specific event types without becoming bloated.\n- However, this is manageable: a core library with extension points (pluggable event schemas) can handle this. The key technical challenge is keeping it genuinely lightweight so it doesn't slow down prototyping.\n- \"Our core engineering team will build\" — this concentrates ownership, which is good for consistency but creates a potential bottleneck if the core team's roadmap doesn't align with fast-moving research projects needing quick logging hooks.\n\nSpeed/Quality of Prototyping:\n- For a development engineer, this is net positive. Instead of each project spending 2-4 weeks figuring out what to log and in what format, they plug into an existing API. This accelerates prototyping.\n- The risk is if the library's initial version doesn't cover enough event types for early prototypes, researchers might need workarounds that fragment the system anyway. Mitigation: make the API extensible from day one (custom event types) while enforcing the mandatory fields by default.\n- Quality-wise, a single codebase means bugs get fixed once, not N times. Log format consistency makes downstream compliance audits simpler and reduces the chance of a subtle formatting error causing a compliance gap.\n\nOverall: This is a technically sound, well-scoped proposal. It addresses a real pain point (redundant bespoke implementations), uses standard engineering patterns, and directly supports the \"automatic recording\" requirement of Art 12(1). The main risk is scope creep or over-abstraction, but the \"lightweight API\" framing and default-capture approach mitigate that. From my perspective as someone who cares about prototyping speed and technical viability, this is a clear win — it removes compliance-logging from the critical path of each new prototype.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: I'd say around 82. It's technically very viable and directly accelerates prototyping. The small de