d technical documentation kept up to date. The proposal's \"immutable ledger\" addresses persistence, which is good for traceability over lifetime.\n\nNow let me assess from my compliance vantage:\n\nStrengths:\n- Automates logging (addresses Art 12(1)'s \"technically allow for automatic recording\")\n- Immutable ledger provides tamper-evidence — critical for regulatory audit trails and institutional survival\n- CI/CD integration ensures consistent implementation across all prototypes — reduces risk of non-compliant systems shipping\n- Reduces manual errors — a key compliance concern (human error in logging)\n- \"Shared library\" approach ensures consistency, which is good for uniform compliance posture\n- Start/end times explicitly addressed (covers 12(3)(a))\n\nWeaknesses/Gaps from compliance perspective:\n- Doesn't specifically address Art 12(2)(a) — identifying risk situations or substantial modifications. The framework needs to capture not just operational events but also model changes, threshold adjustments, etc.\n- Doesn't address Art 12(3)(b), (c), (d) for Annex III biometric systems — reference database, matched input data, and verification personnel identification are missing from the described scope\n- \"Input metadata\" is vague — does it include the actual reference database identifier? The specific match data? The verifier identity?\n- The proposal is framed as a \"prototype\" tool (\"across all high-risk prototypes\") — but Art 12 applies to deployed systems over their full lifetime. The framework must persist beyond prototyping into production and post-market monitoring phases.\n- No mention of log retention policies, access controls, or integration with post-market surveillance processes (Art 72)\n- \"Primary development language\" limitation — what about multi-language environments?\n\nFrom my compliance vantage: This is a solid foundational infrastructure proposal that addresses the core mechanical requirement of Art 12(1) and partially 12(3)(a). The immutability and automation aspects are genuinely valuable for regulatory compliance. However, it's incomplete as stated — it doesn't cover all the specific data points required by Art 12(3), nor does it explicitly address the risk-identification and post-market monitoring logging requirements of Art 12(2). \n\nBut wait — I'm screening whether to approve (commit resources) or reject. The proposal is a framework/library, not the complete compliance solution. As a foundational layer that automates the mechanical act of logging with immutability, it's valuable infrastructure. The gaps can be addressed as extensions to the framework. Approving this doesn't mean we're done with Art 12 — it means we're building the plumbing correctly.\n\nHowever, I'm concerned about:\n1. Scope creep risk: \"across all high-risk prototypes\" could pull resources into a generic solution that isn't tailored to specific Annex III category requirements\n2. The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, data minimization (GDPR interplay), or access for regulatory authorities\n3. It's framed as a developer tool (\"preserves prototyping speed\") rather than a compliance artifact — the compliance officer needs assurance this is treated as a regulated system component, not just a convenience\n\nOn balance: The core idea is sound and addresses genuine compliance needs (automation, immutability, consistency). The CI/CD integration is smart for ensuring no system ships without logging. But it's incomplete and slightly misframed. I'd approve it with moderate confidence because the foundational infrastructure is right, even though significant compliance-specific extensions will be needed.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-68. It's a good foundation but has notable gaps from my specific compliance lens, and the framing as a developer convenience rather than a regulatory control system concerns me slightly. Let me settle on 65.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"forces the logging 