cant resource saving.\n- The centralized approach means one secure database, one security review, one maintenance burden - versus N projects each maintaining their own logging stack.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This integrates into EXISTING training and deployment pipelines rather than requiring new infrastructure. That's good - it leverages what we already have.\n- The \"centralized secure database\" is new, but the API hooks are additive to existing pipelines.\n- It standardizes across projects, which means our existing infrastructure (training clusters, deployment environments) gets a uniform compliance layer without major architectural changes.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Universal API hooks\" is ambitious - high-risk systems under Annex III point 1(a) have specific logging requirements (start/end timestamps, reference database, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons). A truly universal hook set might be over-generalized or need per-project customization anyway.\n- The platform team developing this diverts them from other work. But it's a bounded, well-defined engineering task with clear regulatory requirements to drive scope.\n- \"Centralized secure database\" introduces a single point of failure and a data governance question - who owns the logs? Where are they stored? GDPR considerations for biometric/identity data in Annex III 1(a) systems (which involves verification of natural persons).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived, resource-efficient approach to compliance. It aligns strongly with my domain concerns:\n- Maximizes ROI on engineering investment (build once, use across projects)\n- Reduces per-project overhead, freeing scientific resources for actual research\n- Leverages existing pipelines rather than requiring parallel infrastructure\n- The scope is well-bounded by the regulation itself (Article 12 gives clear minimum requirements)\n\nThe main risk is over-engineering if we have very few high-risk systems currently, but even then, having the middleware ready means when a project does become high-risk, compliance is already baked in rather than being an afterthought that delays deployment.\n\nI would approve this. It's one of the most efficient ways to handle Article 12 compliance from a resource perspective. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. It's not a 90+ because there are execution risks (the \"universal\" hook set might need significant per-project adaptation, the centralized database has data governance complexities), but conceptually it's exactly the right approach for my domain.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 84.\n",
