ckchain-like mechanisms or simply well-governed append-only databases.\n- The modular wrapper approach is sensible — it shouldn't require rearchitecting existing systems. But \"integrates with existing model architectures\" still implies engineering work across multiple codebases.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is where I need to think carefully. Does the lab already have logging infrastructure? Most ML/AI labs already log training runs, inference events, etc. (MLflow, W&B, custom dashboards). The question is whether they can be repurposed or extended rather than building a new \"unified compliance logging framework.\"\n- A \"modular wrapper\" suggests it's designed to sit on top of existing systems, which is good for not disrupting current workflows. But building a separate immutable database in parallel with existing logging creates redundancy and maintenance burden.\n- The proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing observability/logging tools already in the lab's stack.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events over the lifetime — this is about system-level capability, not just a library call. A \"software library providing API calls\" addresses part of this but the \"technically allow for the automatic recording\" requirement means it needs to be built into the system architecture, not just an optional API someone might forget to call.\n- Article 12(3) has very specific minimum requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)) — timestamps of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons involved in verification. A generic \"unified framework\" may need significant customization for these domain-specific requirements.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic and doesn't address the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) or the event-relevance criteria in 12(2)(a)-(c).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe direction is right — a shared compliance logging component avoids duplication. But as framed, it risks being an over-engineered upfront investment (\"unified framework,\" \"immutable database,\" \"modular wrapper\") that may not efficiently leverage what the lab already has. The engineering cost of building this from scratch vs. extending existing observability tooling is unclear and could be substantial. It also doesn't address the specific, domain-dependent logging requirements in Article 12(3).\n\nThat said, if the lab runs multiple high-risk AI projects (which seems likely for an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act), a shared compliance layer does make sense. The key question is whether this can be done by extending existing infrastructure rather than building something new from scratch.\n\nI think this is a reasonable direction but as stated, it's somewhat over-scoped and under-specified regarding how it leverages (vs. replaces) existing infrastructure. I'd lean toward approving the general direction but with concerns about implementation efficiency. From my vantage point specifically — resource allocation and use of existing infrastructure — the proposal to build a NEW unified framework + immutable database when we likely already have logging/observability tooling is concerning from an efficiency standpoint.\n\nLet me settle on: The concept of shared compliance logging infrastructure is sound for resource efficiency (avoid N teams each building their own). However, the framing as building a new \"unified framework\" with a separate \"immutable database\" rather than extending existing observability/logging infrastructure suggests potential waste of resources and underutilization of what we already have. The proposal also doesn't address the specific domain requirements in Article 12(3). I'll approve it conditionally — the direction is right for resource efficiency if scoped properly, but as written it risks over-investment. Actually, let me reconsider: my job is to 