ototype of this could be built relatively quickly for the simpler parts: git hooks that trigger document updates, metadata annotations on datasets in a data lake. Maybe 2-4 weeks for an MVP covering code changes and dataset versions.\n- However, full coverage of \"training data, validation processes, and risk management updates\" is a much larger project. The risk management documentation part especially requires human judgment and narrative content that's hard to auto-generate meaningfully.\n- The proposal as stated is somewhat broad and ambitious for what could be a prototype. But the core idea - linking VCS/pipeline metadata to regulatory doc generation - is technically sound and prototypable.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Guaranteeing\" a current audit trail is a strong claim. Automation reduces burden but doesn't eliminate gaps (e.g., if someone does exploratory work outside tracked systems, or if risk assessments aren't properly tagged).\n- The proposal conflates several different integration challenges into one pipeline. This could make prototyping slower because the scope is too broad for a single prototype.\n- However, the core technical approach (metadata + hooks + auto-generation) is sound and buildable.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is technically viable in its core concept. The metadata tagging and version control hook approach is standard engineering. An MVP could be prototyped relatively quickly for the code/dataset tracking aspects. The broader scope (risk management narrative, validation process semantics) makes full implementation harder to prototype quickly, but the architectural direction is sound.\n\nFrom my vantage point: I'd lean toward approving this because:\n1. The core technical approach is proven and buildable\n2. An MVP (git hooks + dataset metadata → auto-generated doc sections) is a reasonable 2-4 week prototype\n3. It directly addresses Article 12's requirement for up-to-date documentation\n4. It reduces ongoing maintenance burden which frees dev 