trol for datasets and hyperparameters supports traceability\n- Automatic logging aligns with Article 12(1)'s requirement for \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime\"\n- Risk management decisions logged - addresses the risk-management system documentation requirement\n- The \"compliance dashboard tracking the entire AI lifecycle\" is relevant to post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and operation monitoring (Art. 26(5))\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on the R&D phase (\"as they occur in the R&D phase\") - Article 12 requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which includes post-deployment operational use. The proposal mentions \"entire AI lifecycle\" but emphasizes R&D. This is a gap.\n- It doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification): recording period of each use, reference database against which input data checked, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. If the lab develops such systems, this pipeline would need to capture those specific operational logs during deployment, not just R&D.\n- The \"compliance dashboard\" concept is good but somewhat vague - what does it actually track? What triggers alerts? How does it connect to post-market monitoring obligations under Article 72?\n- It addresses the \"technical documentation\" aspect well (Recital 71) but the operational logging during deployment (Article 12(1)-(3)) seems underemphasized.\n\nHowever, from a compliance officer's perspective:\n- This is fundamentally aligned with what we need. The core idea of automated, real-time documentation generation and version control directly serves our compliance obligations.\n- It mitigates the risk of \"institutional failure during regulatory audits\" which is exactly my mandate.\n- The fact that it's automated reduces human error in record-keeping, which is a significant compliance risk.\n- It addresses traceability requirement