an take a decision, trace back through the network's activations to identify which features/patterns drove the output, and then express that as a structured semantic trace, that would be genuinely novel.\n- However, the framing is somewhat forced — \"transforming a compliance burden into an intellectual contribution\" suggests this is primarily motivated by regulatory obligation rather than scientific curiosity. The research question should stand on its own: can we build real-time causal explainers for deployed models? That's interesting regardless of whether the EU AI Act exists.\n- The proposal is quite vague on methodology. \"Causal tracing mechanisms\" could mean many things. It doesn't specify what class of models (LLMs, vision transformers, smaller classifiers), what interpretability approach (attribution methods, causal interventions, circuit discovery), or what the \"semantic trace\" format would be.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, real-time causal explanation for deployed AI systems would have enormous impact far beyond EU compliance — it could transform how any organization audits AI decisions in healthcare, finance, criminal justice, etc.\n- The connection to regulatory requirements gives it a concrete application that could drive adoption of interpretability methods in production settings, which is currently a major gap (most interpretability research happens offline on academic models).\n- However, the proposal as stated reads more like a compliance engineering project with an interpretability veneer than a genuine research frontier push. The \"in real time during inference\" constraint and the mapping to \"required regulatory log entries\" suggests the primary goal is meeting Article 12 requirements, with the semantic layer being added on top.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Feasibility: Real-time causal tracing at scale is not currently possible for most model architectures in any meaningful sense. This would be a multi-year research challenge