ion of automated logging, which IS necessary and DOES free researcher time. \n\nI think I'll approve it but with a moderate promise score reflecting that while the direction is right, the implementation as described has significant resource-allocation inefficiencies (distributed ledger overkill, over-scoped \"every execution path,\" no phasing). Actually wait — let me re-read the instructions: \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not. Judge each proposal strictly on its own merits.\"\n\nThe question is whether this clears my bar as Lab Director focused on resource efficiency. The proposal's core value (automated logging, removing manual burden) is high-value for compliance AND researcher productivity. But the \"distributed ledger\" and \"every execution path\" framing represents a significant misallocation of resources — we could achieve 90%+ of the regulatory requirement with a secure append-only database at maybe 20-30% of the cost.\n\nI think I'll approve it because:\n- The direction is correct and necessary\n- Removing manual logging from researchers IS an efficiency win for the most scarce resource (human expertise)\n- It addresses Article 12 directly\n- The \"distributed ledger\" can be swapped for a cheaper solution during implementation without changing the fundamental architecture\n\nBut my promise score should reflect that I see significant inefficiencies in the specific implementation choices. Maybe 62-68 range. The direction is right (high value) but the execution as specified would waste resources (medium concern). I'll go with 65.\n",
