xplicitly framed as a \"research project.\" It's not a prototype or an engineering solution. It's exploring whether something *might* work someday.\n- The lab cannot commit scarce implementation resources to explore \"whether an idea might someday work\" - the prompt explicitly says approving means committing to pursue, not funding exploration.\n- Even if we could prototype ZKP-based logging for a simple arithmetic circuit (e.g., verifying a linear regression output), extending this to the complex non-deterministic pipelines used in modern AI systems is far beyond current capability.\n- The prototyping timeline would be measured in years minimum before any usable artifact exists, and even then the performance characteristics (latency, throughput) would likely make it impractical for real-time or near-real-time logging requirements.\n\n**Misalignment with Article 12's actual technical requirement:**\n- Article 12 says systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This is an engineering requirement: build a logging subsystem that captures start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, and verifier IDs. It doesn't say logs must be publicly verifiable or cryptographically protected against the operator itself.\n- The privacy concern is real but it's addressed by standard access controls, encryption at rest/in transit, data minimization, and GDPR compliance - not by ZKPs.\n- This proposal over-engineers a solution to a problem that doesn't exist as stated in Article 12, while the actual engineering task (building robust, performant logging infrastructure) is being overlooked.\n\n**My decision:** Reject. From my vantage point, this is technically not viable for prototyping within any reasonable timeframe, it misidentifies the technical requirement of Article 12, and it would consume resources on research that doesn't produce a deployable engineering artifact. The actual compliance need (build a logging system) can be met with