rly for Annex III 1(a) requirements which are very specific.\n- A centralized middleware reduces the risk of non-compliance through omission or inconsistency across projects. This is directly aligned with institutional survival (avoiding regulatory penalties) and market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance posture).\n- It also supports Article 72 post-market monitoring by creating a consistent data trail.\n- The \"without requiring researchers to write custom code\" aspect reduces the risk that research teams, under pressure, will skip or inadequately implement logging — a common failure mode in R&D environments.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- It's a significant infrastructure investment. But from a compliance standpoint, this is precisely the kind of structural investment that demonstrates due diligence to regulators.\n- The proposal says \"across all high-risk AI models\" — I need to make sure it's scoped correctly. Not all AI systems are high-risk, but within our institution, we'd know which ones are. This seems appropriately scoped.\n- The mention of \"timestamps and input matches\" aligns with Article 12(3)(a) and (c).\n- One concern: the proposal doesn't explicitly mention the full Annex III 1(a) requirements (reference database identification in 12(3)(b), natural person identification in 12(3)(d)). But \"input matches\" could be interpreted broadly, and as a compliance officer I'd want to ensure the spec covers all sub-requirements. However, the proposal is at an ideation level, not a final specification. The core architecture it proposes (unified telemetry) is the right foundation to implement all of 12(3).\n\n**Decision:** This is clearly aligned with my mandate. A unified logging infrastructure is one of the most direct and effective ways to ensure Article 12 compliance across an institution that develops multiple high-risk AI systems. It reduces per-project risk, ensures consistency, supports post-market monitoring, and demonstrates institutional co