ls is a real tradeoff. But non-compliance carries existential risk (fines, inability to deploy), so it's not optional.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A middleware approach leverages existing pipelines by adding a layer rather than rebuilding them. This is good for infrastructure utilization.\n- However, \"modular API that automatically captures timestamps, input data references, and user IDs across all high-risk system deployments\" — this requires understanding every pipeline's architecture. In practice, this can become a massive integration project that touches every team's code, which is disruptive to ongoing R&D.\n- The proposal says \"automatically\" but in reality, each research pipeline has different data flows, different input formats, different verification workflows (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems). True automation across all these would require significant customization per deployment, undermining the \"unified\" benefit.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound from an efficiency standpoint — centralizing compliance logging avoids N-fold redundant implementation. However, the execution risk is high: building a truly unified middleware for heterogeneous research pipelines is a large engineering project with uncertain timeline and scope. The proposal somewhat glosses over how different the actual research pipelines are (different models, different data types, different verification workflows). \n\nFrom a resource efficiency standpoint, I'd say this is a good direction but the proposal as stated may be too ambitious in its \"unified\" framing. A more phased approach — starting with the most common patterns and expanding — would be more realistic. But the fundamental principle (shared infrastructure for compliance logging) is correct and efficient.\n\nI'm leaning toward approve, but with moderate confidence because:\n- The efficiency gain of shared middleware over per-team solutions is real\n- It's a necessary investment given Article 12 requirements\n- BUT the \"unified\" framing risks scope creep and long development timelines that block R&D progress\n- The systems team's opportunity cost is significant\n\nI'll approve it because the fundamental resource-efficiency argument is strong (build once vs. build N times), but my promise score will reflect the execution risk from a resource-allocation perspective. Maybe 62-68 range — I'd back it in principle and direction, but I'm not \"without hesitation\" about the specific implementation approach as described.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise = 64. The efficiency logic is sound, it's necessary compliance work, but the \"unified across all deployments\" framing creates resource-concentration risk that concerns me from a lab-director perspective managing competing priorities.\n",
