turn on our specific research goals.\n- The lab's core mission is developing AI systems, not building infrastructure for the broader community. This is a scope creep from \"comply with Article 12\" to \"be the global reference implementation.\"\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Do we already have logging infrastructure in our pipelines? If so, wrapping it for external release is different from building from scratch as a multi-framework library.\n- However, the proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing internal tools — it describes building \"a modular software library\" and \"a wrapper,\" which sounds like new development rather than repackaging what we have.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core compliance need (implementing Article 12 logging for our own systems) is legitimate and necessary, but this proposal frames the effort as a public-facing open-source project with community maintenance obligations. That's a significant resource multiplier over simply implementing internal compliance. The \"gold standard\" aspiration is noble but distracts from efficient use of scarce implementation resources. We could implement Article 12 logging internally, perhaps share some code if useful, without committing to maintaining a multi-framework public library that serves the \"global research community.\"\n\nThis feels like scope inflation — taking a necessary compliance task and wrapping it in an ambitious public-facing mission that multiplies ongoing resource costs. From a pure resource-allocation standpoint, this is over-engineered for our actual needs. The promise of broader scientific impact is speculative compared to the concrete cost of maintaining a public library across multiple frameworks indefinitely.\n\nI'll reject this. It's not that logging isn't needed — it absolutely is. But this particular framing as an open-source community project with multi-framework wrappers and \"gold standard\" aspirations over-commits our scarce resources relative to