        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1995) from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act, specifically Article 12 (Record-keeping). My domain concerns are: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P1995: \"Compliance-Infrastructure Funding Reallocation\"**\n\nThe text proposes shifting hardware budget from non-critical compute expansions to dedicated long-term archival storage and auditing tools — specifically immutable, high-capacity storage for regulatory logs. The key claims are:\n1. It aligns technical capabilities with the legal mandate (Article 12 requires automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system).\n2. It doesn't raid individual scientific teams' budgets.\n3. It uses existing budget reallocation rather than new funding.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Article 12(1) explicitly mandates that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This requires infrastructure — storage, retention capabilities, accessibility. Without dedicated storage, you literally cannot comply.\n- The emphasis on \"immutable\" storage is directly relevant: logs must be preserved and tamper-evident to demonstrate traceability (Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information\" for \"traceability\"). If logs can be altered or deleted, the institution cannot prove compliance in an audit.\n- \"Long-term archival\" aligns with \"over the lifetime of the system\" — AI systems may have long operational lives, and Article 12 doesn't set a short retention period. The records must persist for the full lifetime plus potential post-market monitoring periods (Article 72).\n- \"High-capacity\" is practical: logging per Article 12(3) includes detailed data (input data, reference databases, timestamps, verification personnel IDs) which can generate substantial volumes over time.\n- The funding mechanism (reallocating from non-critical compute) is pragmatic — it doesn't require new budget approval and doesn't penalize research teams. From a compliance perspective, this makes implementation more likely to actually happen rather than getting stuck in budget committees.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- \"Non-critical compute expansions\" — who defines \"non-critical\"? If the reallocation is too aggressive, it could impair the institution's ability to develop and test AI systems at all, which indirectly undermines compliance (you need functioning systems to generate logs). But this is a secondary concern.\n- The proposal mentions \"auditing tools\" broadly but doesn't specify what those are. However, from my vantage point, I'm screening whether to approve the direction — the specifics can be worked out in implementation.\n- It focuses on storage infrastructure but Article 12 also requires the *automatic recording* capability (the logging mechanism itself), not just where logs go. However, having the right storage is a prerequisite for the logging system to function long-term. You could argue this proposal addresses one necessary component.\n- The proposal doesn't mention data protection implications (GDPR) of storing input data and natural person identifiers (Article 12(3)(d)) — but that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the strategic direction.\n\n**Decision from Compliance perspective:**\nThis is directly responsive to Article 12's requirement for lifetime logging infrastructure. Without dedicated, immutable storage, we simply cannot comply with the automatic recording mandate over the system's lifetime. The funding mechanism (reallocation rather than new budget) increases the likelihood of actual implementation. From institutional survival and regulatory compliance standpoint, this is a necessary investment. I would approve this.\n\n**Promise score:**\nThis is squarely within my domain. It addresses a core compliance requirement (Article 12(1) — lifetime logging). The immutable storage aspect directly supports traceability and audit-readiness. The pragmatic funding approach makes it implementable. However, it's one piece of the puzzle — you also need the actual logging mechanisms, access controls, data protection integration, retention policies, etc. So it's highly promising but not a complete solution on its own. I'd put this around 82-85. It's clearly necessary and well-aligned with the mandate, but it's infrastructure for one component of compliance rather than a comprehensive program. Let me settle on 83.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. From pure compliance survival standpoint: if we don't have the storage infrastructure, we literally cannot store logs over the lifetime of systems. That's a binary requirement — no storage = non-compliance = fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M under Article 99. This is existential for institutional survival. The proposal addresses that gap pragmatically. I'll go with 84.\n",
