leware-level hooks. A generic middleware can't know which database was referenced or who verified results without application-specific instrumentation.\n  - The \"without requiring manual coding for every new model iteration\" claim is optimistic. Even with a good SDK/framework, each new architecture will need some level of integration work to define what events are meaningful and how to capture them in that context.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- From my perspective as someone focused on prototyping speed: A mandatory middleware library for ALL production deployments could be a double-edged sword. On one hand, once built, it reduces per-project setup time. On the other hand, it adds a dependency layer that every prototype must go through, potentially slowing down early experimentation.\n- If researchers are building prototypes and need to integrate this library from day one (as \"required for all production deployments\" implies), it could slow prototyping cycles unless there's a lightweight mode or deferred integration path.\n- However, if the library is well-designed with sensible defaults and minimal configuration, it could genuinely reduce compliance overhead during rapid iteration.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A single standardized framework risks becoming a bottleneck - one team maintaining it while many teams depend on it. Any changes to logging requirements (as the Act's implementing acts evolve) would need to propagate through this central library.\n- The \"uniform\" approach may not fit all use cases well. Over-uniformity can lead to either over-logging (waste) or under-logging (compliance gaps).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound - you do want a shared infrastructure for compliance logging rather than every team reinventing the wheel. The technical feasibility is high in principle, though the \"without requiring manual coding\" claim is somewhat unrealistic for truly diverse model architectures. The biggest risk from my vantage point is that the \"one size fits all\" middleware approach could create friction during prototyping if it's not designed with developer experience as a first-class concern.\n\nHowever, I think this clears my bar. A well-designed logging SDK/middleware is technically viable, addresses a real need (Article 12 requires automatic logging over lifetime), and would genuinely help speed up compliance integration across projects. The key question is whether it can be designed to not impede prototyping - but that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the concept.\n\nI'd say this is a solid proposal with clear technical merit. It's a standard infrastructure investment that has high reuse value. My promise score would reflect that it's genuinely useful but has some over-optimism in its claims about eliminating per-model work entirely. I'll give it around 72-78. The idea is right, the execution will need careful design to avoid becoming prototyping friction.\n\nLet me settle on a decision: approve. The core concept is technically viable and addresses a real compliance requirement. From my vantage point (speed/quality of prototyping), having a pre-built logging layer that handles the mechanical aspects of Article 12 means researchers don't have to reinvent logging infrastructure for every new project, which does speed things up - as long as it's designed with low-friction integration in mind.\n\nPromise: I'll go with 74. It's solidly viable and useful, but the \"without requiring manual coding\" framing is a bit of a stretch for truly heterogeneous model architectures, and there's a real risk of adding prototyping friction if not designed carefully.\n",
