ould be very costly (fines under EU AI Act can be substantial).\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. \"Integrated MLOps system\" — building an integrated pipeline has upfront cost. The lab needs to invest engineering time to build/maintain this. Is this proportional? For a high-risk AI lab, yes, but the scope should be clear.\n2. \"Real-time compliance reports\" — is real-time necessary? Recital 71 says documentation should be kept up to date throughout lifetime. Real-time might be overkill; near-real-time or per-experiment-commit could suffice and reduce engineering complexity/cost.\n3. The proposal is somewhat broad (\"integrated MLOps system\") without specifying the actual build scope, which makes resource commitment harder to estimate precisely. But as a screening exercise, the direction is sound.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\n- This is highly aligned with resource-allocation efficiency: it converts recurring manual compliance overhead into a one-time (plus maintenance) engineering investment, freeing researcher time for scientific work.\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (version control, experiment tracking) rather than requiring new tooling purchases — good for funding constraints.\n- The \"living document\" approach is more efficient than periodic documentation sprints that disrupt research cycles.\n- Risk of over-engineering exists (\"real-time\"), but the core concept is sound and proportionate to what Recital 71 demands.\n\nThis proposal clears my bar. It's a well-scoped efficiency play that uses existing tools to meet a regulatory requirement with minimal incremental cost relative to the ongoing manual burden it replaces. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is strongly aligned with my concerns. The main reservation is the slight scope vagueness and \"real-time\" potentially being over-specified, but the core idea of automating doc generation from existing experiment tracking + version control is exactly what I'd want to see. I'd say 78-82. Let me se