rk or heavy tooling, just structured annotations that any developer can add in minutes.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Scrapes\" hyperparameters and dataset versions - this assumes our pipelines already emit these in parseable formats. If our training scripts are ad-hoc notebooks without structured output, there's upfront work to standardize. But that's a one-time cost and actually improves prototyping hygiene anyway.\n- The proposal focuses on Recital 71 (technical documentation) but Article 12 is about *logging* of events during operation. There's a scope mismatch - this pipeline generates static documentation artifacts, not runtime event logs. However, the proposal explicitly frames itself as addressing \"the traceability required by Recital 71\" which is about technical documentation content. So it's correctly scoped to its target.\n- One gap: \"capability and limitation reports\" auto-generated from code and test logs - how do you automatically generate a *qualitative* statement of limitations just from hyperparameters and validation results? You can report the numbers (accuracy, F1 on test set) but \"limitations\" often require engineering judgment (e.g., \"this model degrades on X demographic\"). The pipeline might need human-authored sections for truly qualitative assessments. This is a partial automation, not full automation.\n- That said, even 70-80% automation of the quantitative parts is a huge win.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically straightforward, well-scoped proposal that leverages existing CI/CD infrastructure and standard ML pipeline artifacts. The implementation complexity is low-to-moderate (metadata tagging convention + parser + template renderer in CI). It directly reduces documentation overhead for engineers, which accelerates prototyping cycles. The main limitation is it can't fully automate qualitative capability/limitation statements, but that's a minor gap. From my vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this is a clear win with low risk and high payoff.\n\nI'd rate this highly - maybe 82-85. It's technically very viable, directly serves the regulatory requirement in scope (Recital 71 documentation), reduces engineering overhead, and integrates naturally into existing workflows. The slight deduction is for the qualitative limitation reporting gap and the assumption that our pipelines already emit structured artifacts.\n",
