hrough.\n- However, there IS a genuine tension between GDPR data minimization (Article 5(1)(c) GDPR) and Article 12's requirement to log events including \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" (Art. 12(3)(c)). This is a real regulatory conflict that needs resolution.\n- But does this need a full R&D project? Or can it be handled by the existing engineering team implementing Article 12 compliance, perhaps with some consultation from the legal/compliance team and a few security engineers?\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The lab presumably has existing logging infrastructure for its high-risk AI systems. This proposal suggests building new cryptographic mechanisms rather than adapting what exists.\n- A full \"research project\" implies open-ended exploration, dedicated staff, publication goals, etc. This is not an efficient use of resources when the solution space is relatively well-understood (hashing, tokenization, secure enclaves are all known techniques).\n- The proposal says \"validate these methods against regulatory benchmarks\" — but there aren't really established regulatory benchmarks for privacy-preserving logging yet. This suggests the project might spend significant time trying to define success criteria rather than implementing them.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. This is framed as a \"research project\" but is largely an applied engineering task with known solutions. The lab's R&D resources would be better spent on genuinely novel scientific challenges.\n2. It creates organizational overhead: a separate initiative, dedicated researchers, validation phases — all for what could be addressed by the existing systems team implementing Article 12 compliance with input from security/legal.\n3. The \"conflicting legal obligations\" framing is somewhat misleading. GDPR doesn't strictly prohibit logging personal data; it requires appropriate safeguards and proportionate retention periods. A standard DPO c