        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1075 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"High-Risk AI Traceability Dashboard\" - a real-time monitoring interface that visualizes Article 12 logs, querying centralized log storage to track system usage periods and match input data against reference databases in real time, providing post-market monitoring capability while helping engineers debug production failures faster.\n\nLet me assess this from my development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- A dashboard/querying interface over centralized log storage is a well-understood software pattern. This is not novel or risky technology — it's essentially a read-only visualization layer on top of an existing (or to-be-built) logging infrastructure.\n- \"Real-time\" monitoring adds some complexity but is standard (WebSocket/SSE, streaming queries, etc.).\n- The key assumption here: it assumes centralized log storage already exists or will exist. This proposal is the *presentation* layer, not the *logging* layer itself. Article 12(1) requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" — that's the hard engineering part. This dashboard is downstream of that.\n- The scope seems reasonable: query logs, visualize them, track usage periods, show match data. These are standard CRUD/query operations.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- A read-only dashboard is relatively fast to prototype. You can get a working MVP in weeks if the log schema and storage are defined.\n- However, \"real-time\" adds friction — streaming infrastructure, scaling concerns, latency requirements. For a first prototype, even near-real-time (polling every few seconds) would be sufficient.\n- The dual purpose (regulatory compliance + engineering debug) is actually good for prototyping because you can validate the data pipeline against both use cases simultaneously. If it helps engineers debug, there's internal buy-in and faster iteration feedback loops.\n- Risk: if the underlying log storage doesn't exist yet or isn't well-designed, this dashboard becomes blocked. But that's a dependency issue, not a viability issue with the proposal itself.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal is somewhat thin on technical specifics — what's the log schema? What's the storage backend? What are the performance requirements (how many events per second, retention period)? These are things I'd need to flesh out before committing resources.\n- \"Real-time\" for a regulatory compliance dashboard might be overengineering for v1. The Act requires logs to exist and be retrievable; it doesn't mandate real-time dashboards. But as an engineering tool, near-real-time is fine.\n- The proposal conflates the *logging mechanism* (Article 12(1) - automatic recording) with the *visualization/monitoring layer*. The hard part — building the actual logging into the AI system's runtime — isn't addressed here. This dashboard is a consumer of logs, not a producer.\n- However, as a prototyping target, a dashboard is a good \"pull\" mechanism: it forces you to define what logs need to exist, their schema, and their query patterns. It's a reasonable way to drive the logging architecture design.\n\n**Decision:**\nThis is technically viable — it's standard software engineering (query + visualize). It's not risky. The prototyping speed is good because it's a well-understood pattern. The dual use case (compliance + debugging) gives good feedback loops for iteration. The main risk is dependency on the underlying logging infrastructure, but that's a sequencing issue.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a reasonable, bounded engineering project with clear deliverables and low technical risk. I'd approve it, though I'd note the dependency and the fact that \"real-time\" might be overkill for v1.\n\nPromise score: From my perspective, this i