tions \"input data hashes\" but Article 12(3)(c) requires recording the actual input data (or at least sufficient information), not just a hash. A hash alone may not satisfy traceability requirements — you need to be able to reconstruct or reference what was processed. Actually, let me reconsider: hashing could be a privacy measure, but the regulation says \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" — this might require actual data retention, not just hashes. This is a gap.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address logging of events relevant to identifying risk situations (Art 12(2)(a)) or facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b)). It focuses on basic event capture but the regulation requires logs to be purposeful — they need to enable specific traceability functions.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is a significant requirement. The proposal doesn't address data retention periods, archival, or what happens when a model is deprecated. Lifetime logging infrastructure needs to persist beyond active development.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, the reference database (Art 12(3)(b)) and match results aren't explicitly mentioned in this framework — though \"input data hashes\" might partially cover (c).\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it describes a middleware layer but doesn't specify how it handles the specific logging requirements for different categories of high-risk systems.\n\nFrom a compliance perspective:\n- This is a solid foundational step toward Art 12(1) compliance\n- It addresses the \"technically allow for automatic recording\" requirement well\n- The standardization and mandate aspect reduces institutional risk\n- However, it has gaps regarding the specificity of what must be logged (especially for Annex III systems) and lifetime retention\n- As a Compliance Officer, I'd want to see this approved but with the understanding that it needs supplementation\n\nThe proposal is directionally correct and addresses th