identifying natural persons involved in verification requires integration with authentication/authorization systems, which isn't purely a logging middleware concern.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A standardized middleware layer is excellent for prototyping speed because:\n  - It's reusable across projects — build once, deploy to all high-risk services.\n  - Researchers don't need to implement logging per project.\n  - The decorator pattern allows incremental adoption (wrap one service at a time).\n  - Separation of concerns keeps the AI model code clean.\n- However, \"standardized\" can become a bottleneck if different high-risk systems have very different logging needs (Article 12(3) has specific minimums for Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems that differ from general requirements in Article 12(2)). A one-size-fits-all middleware might need configuration or extension per system.\n- The \"immutable centralized database\" is a single point of failure consideration and may introduce latency to the inference pipeline, which could affect prototyping iteration speed during development (though this can be mitigated with async writes).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable approach that aligns well with how a development team would actually implement logging compliance. The decorator + gateway plugin pattern is pragmatic and well-understood. It's not novel or cutting-edge, but it's solid engineering. The main technical risks are around completeness of event capture (especially for the specific Article 12(3) requirements) and performance overhead on the inference path. But as a prototyping approach — build the middleware first, then wrap services — it's very sound.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a well-scoped, technically feasible proposal that would accelerate compliance prototyping across multiple projects. I'd approve it. The promise score should reflect strong viability and good fit for development workflows, but with some caveats about completeness of the event schema for specific high-risk categories. I'd put this around 78-82.\n",
