rom the knowledge frontier perspective specifically:** This proposal treats the technical documentation requirement (Recital 71) as primarily an information-extraction problem — \"we have this data in Git/MLflow/dataset manifests, let's move it to a template.\" But Recital 71 asks for much more than raw metadata: \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes... drawn in a clear and comprehensive form.\" The intellectual substance of good technical documentation — explaining WHY certain design choices were made, what the actual limitations are (not just that a parameter was set to X), how the risk management system works conceptually — cannot be auto-generated from commit messages and experiment tracking metadata. This is where research judgment lives.\n\nThe proposal also has a subtle tension with my domain: it risks reducing the documentation process (which should involve deep scientific reflection on what was actually learned, what limitations exist, what the true capabilities are) to a mechanical data-assembly exercise. The \"clear and comprehensive form\" that Recital 71 demands is an interpretive act, not just a formatting one.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: as a pure engineering automation tool for the parts of documentation that ARE mechanically derivable (dates, versions, dataset identifiers), it has some utility. But from MY vantage point — knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact — this is essentially orthogonal. It doesn't advance science. It's an administrative efficiency measure dressed in technical clothing.\n\nMy decision: I should reject this from my vantage point. It doesn't advance the knowledge frontier, it has limited intellectual research merit (it's engineering automation), and its broader scientific impact is at best indirect (freeing researcher time). The promise score should be low — maybe 15-20. There's a sliver of value in ensuring that pr