        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2576 is about \"Research on Privacy-Preserving Traceability Logs\" — exploring differential privacy and secure multi-party computation applied to system event logging mechanisms for EU AI Act compliance.\n\nLet me assess this from my development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- Differential privacy (DP) applied to logs is a well-studied but genuinely difficult problem. Adding noise to logs to preserve privacy can severely degrade their auditability — which is the entire point of Article 12. The regulation requires \"clear and comprehensive\" records that enable traceability, verification of compliance, and post-market monitoring. DP adds statistical noise that fundamentally undermines deterministic traceability.\n- Secure multi-party computation (SMPC) for logging is extremely computationally expensive. SMPC protocols have high communication complexity and computational overhead. For a system that needs to log events \"over the lifetime of the system\" — potentially millions or billions of events — applying full SMPC per event would be prohibitively slow and resource-intensive. This conflicts with real-time or near-real-time logging requirements.\n- The tension identified is real, but the proposed solutions (DP, SMPC) are heavyweight cryptographic/statistical tools that may not be practical for high-throughput logging at scale.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is explicitly framed as a \"scientific project\" and \"research.\" It's pushing the knowledge frontier rather than building a deployable solution. As a Development Engineer, I care about solutions I can prototype and ship. A research exploration into DP and SMPC for logs has a very long timeline before it produces anything usable in a product.\n- The scope is broad: \"investigate applying\" two different complex techniques (DP AND SMPC), benchmark them against accuracy and auditability. This is a multi-year research program, not something that yields a prototype in the next sprint or quarter.\n- There's no clear path to a deliverable artifact I could integrate into an AI system's logging pipeline. \"Benchmark these methods\" doesn't tell me what the output would be — a paper? A library? A design pattern?\n\n**Does it address Article 12 specifically?**\n- Article 12 requires automatic recording of events, with specific minimum fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). The proposal addresses the \"privacy\" angle of logging but doesn't directly tackle the technical architecture needed to meet the letter of the regulation. It's more of a research exploration than an engineering solution.\n- The regulation says logs should be \"clear and comprehensive.\" DP inherently makes things less clear by adding noise. This is in tension with the regulatory requirement, not just data protection law.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n- The problem identified is real but the proposed solutions are heavyweight research programs unlikely to yield near-term engineering artifacts.\n- DP and SMPC for high-volume event logging face fundamental scalability challenges that make them impractical in the foreseeable future for production systems.\n- This is a \"research\" proposal, not an \"engineering solution.\" It doesn't give me something I can prototype against Article 12's requirements this quarter or next year.\n- The auditability conflict with DP is particularly concerning — if you add noise to logs, regulators may reject them as insufficient for traceability.\n- However, the problem space is legitimate and understanding privacy constraints in logging IS important for engineering design. But \"investigating\" at a research level isn't something that moves my needle on shipping compliant systems.\n\nI'll lean toward rejecting this from my vantage point. It's interesting research but doesn't deliver technical viability or prototype speed. The promise score should reflect that while the problem is real, the approach doesn't align with what I need as a Dev Engineer to build working solutions quickly. Maybe a 25-30? The problem identification has some value for informing design constraints, but the proposed research path (DP + SMPC) is far from engineering deliverables and faces fundamental practical obstacles.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 28.\n",
