, there IS some synergy: if we're already building logging infrastructure for compliance, the schemas we develop could potentially be standardized. But this proposal goes well beyond what's needed for our own compliance—it's an external leadership play.\n- The \"within our specific domain\" qualifier helps scope it somewhat, but multi-institution coordination and benchmarking are inherently resource-intensive and hard to control.\n- From a pure efficiency standpoint: we need to implement Article 12 logging for OUR systems first (that's mandatory). This initiative is additive and external-facing. It competes with the core compliance work for the same engineering resources.\n- The funding constraint angle: this is an open-ended collaborative project with no clear endpoint or budget cap. \"Invite other research institutions to validate them through shared benchmarks\" could become a long tail of coordination costs.\n\n**Decision:** I'm leaning toward reject. While there's intellectual appeal, from my vantage point as the person responsible for making sure we actually implement what we need with the resources we have, this is an ambitious external-facing initiative that:\n1. Doesn't directly address our mandatory compliance obligations (it's additive)\n2. Requires ongoing, open-ended resource commitment\n3. Has diffuse and long-term benefits (reputation, influence) rather than concrete operational improvements\n4. Competes for the same engineering resources needed to actually build our logging infrastructure\n5. The coordination costs of multi-institution collaboration are significant and hard to predict\n\nIt's not a bad idea in absolute terms—it could be valuable—but it doesn't clear my bar for committing scarce implementation resources when we still need to nail down our own compliance. It's more of a \"nice-to-have if/when\" rather than a priority commitment.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-40. There's genuine value in contributing to standards, and it aligns with the regulatory direction. But from my specific vantage point of resource allocation and making sure we don't overcommit before our own house is in order, it's not compelling enough to approve now. Maybe 35.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"within our specific domain\" which narrows scope. And if we're already building logging schemas for compliance, the marginal cost of packaging them as a standard might be modest. But the \"invite other research institutions to validate them through shared benchmarks\" part is where costs balloon and become uncontrollable.\n\nI'll go with reject at 35.\n",
