ding the pipeline itself requires investment — engineering time, testing, maintenance. But compared to ongoing manual documentation across multiple high-risk AI systems, the ROI should be positive fairly quickly.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Key strength: it builds on \"our experiment tracking software\" (e.g., MLflow, W&B, or similar). This is not a greenfield project; it's an extension of what we already run. Low marginal infrastructure cost.\n- The document generation engine mapping to regulatory templates is a bounded problem — the templates are fixed by the regulation, so this isn't open-ended R&D but engineering work with clear specifications.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Is this within the lab's core competence? As an R&D institution, we have software engineers, but building and maintaining a documentation pipeline is more of an infrastructure/ops task than scientific R&D. However, it's a necessary enabler for our compliance posture.\n- The \"regulatory templates\" will evolve as implementing regulations and guidelines come out (the EU AI Act has a long implementation timeline). The tool may need updates. But this is manageable maintenance.\n- The scope seems well-bounded: extract metadata from experiment tracking → map to template → generate document. Not a research project; an engineering task.\n\n**Decision reasoning:**\nThis proposal aligns very well with my domain concerns. It uses existing infrastructure (experiment tracking), frees up scarce researcher time (resource efficiency), has clear bounded scope, and directly addresses a recurring compliance obligation that would otherwise consume ongoing human resources. The upfront cost is modest relative to the recurring savings. I see no major red flags.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's not 100 because there are execution risks (template evolution, integration complexity), but from my vantage point it's a clear win on resource efficiency and infrastructure leverage. I'd say around 82-88. Let me settle on 85 — strong alignment, bounded scope, leverages existing tools, but some uncertainty in template stability and ongoing maintenance burden.\n",
