nd comprehensive\" documentation.\n\n4. **Minimizes friction on prototyping**: From an institutional survival perspective, if compliance tooling is so burdensome that teams bypass it or find workarounds, we're in real danger. A lightweight middleware approach (decorators, not heavy frameworks) keeps adoption high, which means actual compliance rather than paper compliance.\n\n5. **Addresses Article 12(3)**: For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the framework captures timestamps (start/end date-time of use), input data references, and system states — these map to the minimum requirements in paragraphs (a)-(d). The \"input references\" could capture the reference database against which input data was checked, and input data for matches.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it says \"timestamps, input references, and system states\" but doesn't explicitly map to the specific minimum fields in Art. 12(3) (reference database, identification of natural persons involved in verification). However, as a framework/library, it could be extended.\n- \"Without manual coder intervention\" is good for compliance consistency, but some Article 14(5) elements (identification of natural persons in verification) may require deliberate integration points that aren't purely automatic.\n- The proposal doesn't mention log retention periods, immutability, or access controls — which are important from a regulatory perspective (logs need to be preserved and auditable). But these could be considered implementation details within the framework.\n\nOverall: This is squarely in my domain of concern. It directly implements the core obligation of Article 12(1) across all high-risk systems. The consistency angle is critical for compliance — if I'm going to face a market surveillance authority or a regulatory audit, I want every system to log identically and completely. The \"without manual coder intervention\" aspect reduces the risk that developers skip logging st