y. You can build a minimal version quickly: wrap existing pipeline steps in logging decorators, hash datasets on ingestion, store structured JSON events in an append-only store. A working prototype could be built in days to weeks.\n- The integration with existing training pipelines is the main engineering lift, but it's incremental — you're adding observability hooks, not restructuring the pipeline fundamentally.\n- However, \"full lineage\" across all stages (data ingestion → preprocessing → training → validation → deployment) requires touching many systems. The breadth means more integration points and more surface area for bugs. But each individual hook is small.\n- The \"immutable ledger accessible for audits and peer review\" part adds a query/visualization layer on top, which is moderate effort but straightforward.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system. This proposal addresses the development/provenance side rather than the operational runtime logging that Article 12 primarily targets. The recital mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used\" as part of technical documentation, so there IS a connection to record-keeping of how the system was built.\n- However, Article 12 is more about runtime event logging (what happens when the system operates) rather than development provenance. The proposal is somewhat adjacent — it captures HOW the model was made, not necessarily what events occur during deployment/operation. That said, Recital 71 explicitly mentions \"how high-risk AI systems have been developed\" and \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used,\" so there's a legitimate connection to the broader record-keeping regime.\n- The proposal doesn't clearly address Article 12(2)(a)-(c) — identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, or monitoring operation per Art 26(5). It's more about