t comprehensible or well-structured for regulatory reviewers, it could create a compliance gap. However, this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (algorithms, data, training, testing) but Article 12 also requires automatic recording of events/logs over the lifetime. The proposal mentions \"experiment tracking logs\" which partially addresses this, but I need to consider whether it covers the full logging requirement of Articles 12(1)-(3), including the specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, natural person identification).\n- The proposal is primarily about documentation generation, not about the logging capabilities themselves. Article 12(1) requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" The proposal assumes logs exist and generates documentation FROM them, but doesn't necessarily build the logging infrastructure itself. However, it does say it will \"parse experiment tracking logs\" which implies those logs need to exist first.\n- For high-risk systems under Annex III point 1(a), the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) (recording periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification) may not be fully captured by \"experiment tracking logs and configuration files.\" These are operational/runtime logs, not just training-time metadata.\n\n**Assessment from compliance vantage:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses the documentation obligation under Recital 71 and Article 12. The automation aspect significantly reduces compliance risk (missing updates, human error, versioning gaps). The trigger on model versioning events is well-aligned with the \"throughout the lifetime\" requirement. However, there's a gap: it focuses on training-time metadata and documentation generation but may not ful