ht already have audit/logging infrastructure.\n- \"Strictly enforce\" and \"absolute legal safety\" language suggests a compliance-first approach, which is fine, but I need to ask: does this require dedicated R&D resources (scientists, engineers) or is it more of an engineering/infrastructure task? If it's the latter, it might be better handled by existing ops/engineering teams rather than consuming R&D lab resources.\n- The scope is narrow and well-defined, which is good for resource allocation - you can estimate the effort reasonably. It's not a \"moonshot\" that could consume resources indefinitely.\n- However, if the lab doesn't actually develop Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification), then this proposal is entirely moot and would waste resources building capability for a product line that doesn't exist.\n\n**Resource allocation assessment:**\n- The proposal is well-scoped and bounded, which is good for resource planning.\n- It's compliance-driven rather than science-driven, so it's \"table stakes\" work if we have biometric systems in our portfolio.\n- The main risk from my perspective is whether this constitutes a new build vs. an extension of existing logging infrastructure. If the lab already has event logging (which Article 12(1) requires for ALL high-risk systems), then the incremental work for 12(3)'s four specific fields should be modest.\n- The \"secure ledger\" framing could push toward over-engineering (blockchain? distributed ledgers?) when a well-structured database with proper access controls and audit trails would satisfy the requirement.\n\n**Decision:**\nThe proposal is reasonable and addresses a real, legally mandated need IF the lab develops Annex III 1(a) systems. It's well-scoped enough to be resource-efficient. However, I have concerns about whether it needs to be built as a separate \"module\" versus integrated into existing logging infrastructure. The framing as a standalone \"specialized verification tool\" and \"secure ledger\