        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is P1432: \"Unified Automated Telemetry Middleware\" - a standardized logging wrapper that acts as a transparent proxy between the UI and AI core, intercepting requests and logging them to a secure database.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain concerns (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A middleware/logging proxy layer is a well-established pattern in software engineering. API gateways already exist for routing traffic, so adding a logging interceptor is technically straightforward.\n- \"Transparent proxy\" between UI and AI core - this is feasible but has caveats. If the system isn't purely request/response (e.g., streaming, long-running batch jobs, multi-step agent workflows), a simple intercept-log pattern may miss critical events. The EU AI Act Article 12(2) requires logging of \"events relevant for\" risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring - this goes beyond just inputs/outputs/timestamps.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the requirements are more specific: reference database used, input data matched, identification of natural persons involved in verification. A generic \"inputs, outputs, timestamps\" wrapper may not capture these domain-specific fields without additional configuration or hooks.\n- The claim that it \"removes the need for individual researchers to implement custom logging code\" is partially true - you get baseline compliance logging for free, but the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for biometric systems and the risk-event tracking of Article 12(2)(a) may still require domain-specific instrumentation.\n- \"Total compliance across all projects\" is an overclaim. The middleware handles the transport-level events (start/end timestamps, input/output data), but i