ramework requires significant engineering time upfront before any payoff is realized. If we only have 1-2 high-risk systems, the ROI might not justify the build cost vs. just implementing logging per-project.\n2. Scope creep risk: \"Unified\" and \"standardized\" can become their own project that consumes resources indefinitely. The modular plugin architecture sounds good but could lead to a platform team that's hard to scope down.\n3. Maintenance burden: A shared library becomes a critical dependency. Bugs, updates, and deprecations need to be managed centrally, creating a single point of failure and ongoing maintenance cost.\n4. Opportunity cost: The engineering time spent building this framework is time not spent on the actual AI research that generates our value and revenue/funding.\n5. However, if we anticipate multiple high-risk systems (which an R&D institution developing AI systems likely does), the amortized cost per system becomes very attractive.\n\n**Balancing assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is actually quite sensible IF there are (or will be) multiple high-risk projects. The key question is whether the lab has enough concurrent or sequential high-risk AI development to justify a shared framework vs. ad-hoc per-project logging. Given that we're an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, it's reasonable to assume multiple projects will need this.\n\nThe \"compliance by default\" through pipeline integration is smart — it reduces the risk of individual researchers forgetting or misimplementing logging requirements, which would be far more costly in terms of remediation and potential non-compliance penalties.\n\nThe modular plugin architecture is good for resource efficiency because it means you build the core once and add specific captures per system type without rebuilding.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful about the \"unified\" framing — if this becomes a 6-month platform project before any act