stment that directly addresses a regulatory obligation (Article 12) while reducing recurring labor costs across the lab's high-risk systems portfolio. It uses mature, open-source technology (ELK), leverages existing log outputs rather than requiring system modifications, and separates concerns cleanly (recording vs. reporting). From a resource-allocation standpoint, it converts variable manual cost (each audit requires engineer-hours) into fixed infrastructure cost (maintain the pipeline once, use it for every system and every audit cycle). The scientific ambition is preserved because this doesn't pull researchers off their work; it's DevOps/data-engineering labor.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly from my vantage point. It's efficient, bounded, leverages existing infrastructure, and reduces recurring compliance overhead without competing with research for core resources. I'll approve it with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. The slight deduction is for the ongoing maintenance overhead of an ELK cluster in a lab that may not have dedicated infrastructure staff, and the small risk that \"real-time monitoring\" language could invite scope expansion beyond compliance into general observability.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
