ure, it doesn't constitute research in any meaningful sense.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\nCould this have broader impact? Potentially marginally — if a standardized logging framework for AI systems were published and adopted across the industry, it could create a common language for auditing AI behavior. However, as described, this is an internal tool (\"our base model templates,\" \"our high-risk prototypes\"). It's not positioned as a contribution to the scientific community. There's no mention of publication, open-sourcing, or generalizing beyond the lab. The broader scientific impact is negligible.\n\n**Counter-considerations:**\nOne could argue that standardized telemetry for AI systems touches on interpretability and transparency research, which are legitimate frontier areas. But this proposal doesn't engage with those questions at a research level — it's purely a compliance plumbing solution. It records \"what\" happened (timestamps, hashes) but not \"why\" the system behaved as it did. It doesn't advance understanding of AI behavior in any scientific sense.\n\nAnother angle: could this framework enable future research by providing clean, consistent data for post-hoc analysis? Perhaps marginally, but that's a stretch and not what the proposal actually does or aims to do.\n\n**Decision:** From my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal is an engineering compliance task with minimal to no research content. It doesn't advance any scientific frontier, has low intellectual merit as a research contribution, and its broader scientific impact is negligible as described. I should reject it from this perspective.\n\n**Promise score:** This is where I need to be careful. Even though I'm rejecting it from my vantage point, the promise score reflects how promising it is FROM MY VANTAGE POINT. Given that it has essentially no research content and minimal scientific contribution potential as described, I'd put this quite low. Maybe 8-12. It's not zero because a standardized logging infrastructure could theoretically create datasets useful for research, but as proposed, it's purely engineering/compliance with no research ambition.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise ~10.\n",
