hitectures might find it slow to adapt their experimental pipelines to fit the logging framework's API.\n- However, if well-designed (plug-and-play, minimal configuration), it could REDUCE the burden of each individual project having to build its own compliance logging from scratch. This is a net positive for prototyping speed IF the framework is mature and stable before being mandated.\n- The risk: building the framework itself consumes significant engineering resources before any product value is delivered. If the framework is not ready when projects need it, it becomes a blocker rather than an enabler.\n- \"Institutional gold standard\" language suggests a long-term commitment to maintainability and backward compatibility, which can slow iteration but ensures consistency.\n\n**My Professional Vantage Point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality):**\n- The core idea is technically viable — structured logging frameworks are well-trodden engineering territory.\n- The main concern for my function: mandating a single framework across \"all high-risk project repositories\" and \"different model architectures\" before it's proven could be premature. A better approach might be to build the core (timestamping, version capture, generic event recording) and let domain-specific extensions handle architecture-specific data. But the proposal as stated does say \"modular API,\" which suggests some flexibility.\n- The promise of reducing individual developer workload is real — if you've already built the compliance logging plumbing once, each new project saves days/weeks of that work.\n- Technical risk: cross-architecture generality can lead to either an overly complex framework or one that's too thin to be useful for specific compliance requirements (like Art 12(3)'s very specific fields).\n\nOverall: This is a technically viable and well-motivated proposal. It addresses a real engineering need (avoiding N implementations of the same logging requirement) and aligns wi