        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P2198 from the perspective of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2198:\n\n\"Regulatory Overhead Resource Allocation Model. A new financial and operational budgeting framework that explicitly accounts for the 'compliance tax' in every high-risk project. We will introduce a mandatory line item in project proposals for compute and man-hours dedicated specifically to Article 12 record-keeping. It prevents invisible overhead from eroding our scientific ambition and ensures funding is transparently aligned with legal obligations.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:\n\n**Rationale:**\nThis proposal directly addresses a core concern in my domain. Article 12 requires automatic logging over the system's lifetime, which means ongoing compute for log storage, processing pipelines, data retention infrastructure, and human oversight for verification (especially under Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems). If these costs are not explicitly budgeted at project inception, they become hidden overhead that erodes scientific R&D capacity. By making it a mandatory line item in project proposals, we ensure:\n- Funding requests are honest and complete (no underestimation of true cost)\n- We can make informed go/no-go decisions on projects with full visibility into compliance costs\n- We protect scientific ambition by preventing compliance from silently consuming research budgets\n- It leverages existing budgeting infrastructure rather than requiring new systems\n\nThis is a governance/budgeting process change, not an expensive technical build. It's low-cost to implement (updating proposal templates and review criteria) but high-value in terms of resource allocation transparency. It aligns perfectly with my mandate of balancing scientific ambition with funding constraints.\n\nHowever, I should consider potential downsides:\n- Could it create bureaucratic friction? Possibly, but a single line item is minimal overhead\n- Does it assume Article 12 costs are separable? In practice, logging infrastructure might be shared across systems, so the \"per-project\" allocation might be artificial. But for planning purposes, even an estimated allocation is better than none.\n- Is it too rigid? Making it \"mandatory\" could be seen as inflexible, but that's actually a feature for accountability.\n\nOverall, this is a strong proposal from my perspective. It's a process improvement that enhances resource-allocation efficiency without requiring significant new infrastructure investment. It directly serves the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints by making compliance costs visible and explicit rather than hidden.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: I'd rate this highly - maybe 82-85. It's well-aligned with my domain, low implementation cost, high visibility benefit. The main caveat is that \"per-project\" allocation of shared logging infrastructure could be somewhat artificial, and the framework needs to account for economies of scale across projects. But conceptually it's sound and actionable.\n\nLet me settle on 83 - strong alignment, practical, but with some implementation nuance around shared vs. project-specific costs.\n",
