otyping and development work. It's imposing overhead before the tooling is mature.\n4. The proposal conflates governance/standards activity with engineering delivery. My domain is technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping. This is more of a policy/governance initiative that happens to have a technical artifact (the JSON schema).\n5. \"Establishes our institution as a leader in AI governance\" — this is a positioning/marketing goal, not an engineering one. It doesn't directly improve our ability to build and iterate on AI systems quickly.\n6. The schema itself: while a JSON schema for documentation metadata is straightforward technically, making it truly \"comprehensive\" enough to capture training processes, risk management strategies, algorithms, data provenance, etc., in a machine-readable way that's both useful and maintainable across different system architectures is extremely hard. We'd likely end up with either an overly narrow template (useless for diverse systems) or an overly complex one (unmaintainable).\n7. Cross-lab collaboration adds significant coordination overhead to what should be an internal engineering efficiency tool.\n\n**Technical viability:** The core idea (JSON schema for doc metadata) is viable, but the \"comprehensive\" and \"cross-industry standard\" framing makes it a multi-year standards effort rather than a focused engineering project.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping impact:** Piloting on ALL current high-risk projects would slow us down. A half-baked or overly rigid schema imposed early could create rework when the standard evolves through cross-lab negotiation.\n\nMy assessment: The underlying idea has some merit as an internal tooling exercise, but as framed — a cross-EU collaborative standard with comprehensive scope and immediate pilot across all projects — it's a governance/standards project masquerading as an engineering one. It would consume significant development resources in coordination and schema d