k of missing log fields.\n- Cons: There's an upfront cost of building the middleware itself (API design, data sink architecture, security considerations for storing sensitive biometric data, etc.). For a lab that may only have 2-3 high-risk projects at any time, this could be over-engineering. The \"wasteful duplication\" concern is valid but the scale matters.\n- The proposal says researchers \"plug into their existing pipelines\" — this implies some integration work per project, but less than building from scratch. That's reasonable.\n- Risk: If the middleware design doesn't account for all the specific logging requirements across different Annex III categories, you'll need to extend it repeatedly, which can become a bottleneck itself.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound — centralized logging infrastructure is standard practice and reduces per-project effort. From a prototyping speed perspective, having a ready-made logging layer means new projects can focus on model development rather than compliance plumbing. However, the proposal glosses over the domain-specific requirements in Article 12(3) that may make a truly \"unified\" API harder to design than it sounds. The promise of eliminating all per-project traceability logic is somewhat overstated — you'll still need project-specific event definitions and data field mappings.\n\nI think this is a viable and useful proposal, but the scope as stated is slightly over-simplified. It's technically feasible (logging middleware is not cutting-edge), it speeds up prototyping by removing compliance boilerplate, but I'd want to see more detail on how it handles the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements vs. other categories.\n\nDecision: I lean toward approve — the core technical pattern is sound, it reduces per-project overhead, and it's a reasonable engineering investment for an institution with multiple high-risk projects. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not without some design challenge