n as a \"new standard\"\n- Focused on defining concepts rather than building compliant systems\n- Ablation studies comparing granularity levels — this is academic methodology, not engineering\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point, I need logging capabilities BUILT and OPERATIONAL. The regulation's requirements are specific enough (especially Annex III paragraph 3 which gives minimum requirements). Even for probabilistic models, the obligation is to log events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring — I can work with engineering to determine what that means in practice without a multi-year ablation study program.\n\nThe \"new standard\" aspiration also worries me from a regulatory perspective — we are not positioned to create standards; the Commission and notified bodies do that through harmonized standards (CEN/CENELEC). Positioning our research output as a \"standard\" could create confusion about our compliance posture.\n\n**Decision**: Reject. This is a research/academic project dressed in compliance clothing. It doesn't directly advance our Article 12 implementation obligations. The regulatory text is sufficiently specific that we don't need to be defining what an \"event\" is — we need to build the logging infrastructure per the specification. Committing resources here delays actual compliance work and creates reputational risk through the \"new standard\" framing.\n\n**Promise score**: There's a small kernel of technical relevance (determining granularity for probabilistic models), but the proposal as framed is wrong for my function. I'd give it maybe 15-20. It's not zero because the underlying technical question has some validity, but from a pure compliance/officer perspective, this doesn't serve institutional survival or regulatory compliance in any direct way. The framing around publication and \"new standard\" actually creates mild reputational risk. I'll say 15.\n",
