he start, the record-keeping is natural rather than retrofitted.\n- This is a preventive measure that reduces the probability of non-compliance, which directly protects institutional survival and market reputation.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- Training alone may not be sufficient. Article 12 requires technical capability (automatic recording), not just awareness. However, this proposal focuses on the human/design side, which is a necessary but not sufficient condition. It's not claiming to be the complete solution.\n- \"Series of workshops\" — sustainability and depth questions arise. A one-time workshop may not keep pace with evolving guidance from the AI Office.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how compliance will verify that researchers actually incorporated the requirements into their designs (i.e., it lacks an audit/verification mechanism). But as a training initiative, its scope is reasonable.\n- It's focused on \"designing experiments\" — but Article 12 also covers the operational phase (logs over lifetime, post-market monitoring). However, understanding the full lifecycle at design time is actually important because you need to plan for those capabilities.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal. The core risk I manage is that we build systems that don't meet Article 12's requirements, which would mean we can't place them on the EU market (Article 43 conformity assessment would fail), we face potential enforcement action, and our reputation suffers. Training researchers to embed compliance thinking into their design process is a foundational, low-cost preventive measure that directly reduces this risk. It addresses the root cause of non-compliance: misunderstanding of requirements at the design stage.\n\nThe fact that it pairs legal experts with scientists is smart — it ensures accuracy (not just \"compliance theater\") while being integrated enough not to slow research. The framing of \"minimizing friction\" while \"maintaining 