sses/Concerns:**\n- \"Leadership team will review\" — this adds a governance layer. Who does the actual classification work? If it's just leadership reviewing without technical input, they might misclassify systems and either over-allocate (wasting resources on unnecessary compliance) or under-allocate (missing a high-risk system). But this is more of an implementation detail than a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n- Quarterly might be too slow if projects evolve rapidly — a project that starts as non-high-risk could shift into high-risk territory mid-quarter. But for a lab, quarterly is probably reasonable given R&D cycles.\n- The proposal doesn't specify who does the Annex III mapping work or what infrastructure exists to track it. It's somewhat high-level.\n- However, as a \"proposal\" at the ideation stage, this level of detail seems appropriate — it identifies the WHAT and WHY clearly.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is squarely in my domain. Resource allocation efficiency is THE thing I care about. Identifying compliance obligations early in the development cycle so that budget can be planned for (rather than discovered as a surprise) is exactly how you balance scientific ambition with funding constraints. Without this, projects get approved based on research budget alone, and then compliance costs hit like a surprise tax at the end, either delaying publication/deployment or forcing scope cuts that undermine the science.\n\nThe quarterly cadence is efficient — not wasteful administrative overhead, but regular enough to catch changes. The tie to Annex III makes it actionable rather than vague.\n\nI would approve this. It's a direct, proportionate response to my core concern. Promise score: This is very well-aligned with my function. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because it's somewhat high-level (who does the actual classification? what tools/infrastructure support this review?) and \"leadership team\" is vague on accountability. But the 