 an R&D lab) can focus on scientific work rather than regulatory plumbing. This protects scientific ambition within funding constraints.\n4. **Uniform compliance across portfolio**: Reduces risk of gaps and inconsistencies that would require remediation later (expensive).\n\nConcerns/risks:\n1. **Upfront cost**: Building a unified middleware framework is a significant upfront engineering investment from the \"core engineering team.\" This competes with other engineering priorities. The lab must commit scarce resources before seeing returns across all projects.\n2. **Scope risk**: A truly universal wrapper for \"regardless of specific model architecture\" could become an ever-expanding scope project. Different architectures (transformers, diffusion models, graph neural nets, etc.) may have very different logging needs.\n3. **Maintenance burden**: A shared middleware becomes a single point of failure and ongoing maintenance responsibility. If it breaks, all projects are affected.\n4. **The Annex III 1(a) specific requirements** (biometric identification systems with specific recording requirements like start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, natural person identification) might need specialized handling that a \"standard API\" may not fully cover without becoming too complex.\n\nHowever, the core value proposition from my vantage point is strong: it's the most resource-efficient way to meet Article 12 across a portfolio of projects. The upfront investment amortizes quickly once you have multiple high-risk systems. It uses existing infrastructure (existing training loops, existing frameworks). It protects researchers' time for science.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep and over-engineering trying to be universal from day one. But the proposal says \"modular\" which mitigates this somewhat - you can start with core logging and add modules.\n\nMy assessment: This is a well-conceived, resource-efficient approach that aligns strongly with my mandate. Th