 / Concerns:\n- The proposal says \"verify the presence and correctness of mandatory logs for high-risk systems.\" This is somewhat narrow — Article 12 requires the system to *technically allow* for automatic recording over its *lifetime*, not just at deployment. A CI/CD gate checks a snapshot; it doesn't verify that logging continues correctly throughout operation (e.g., after model updates, data pipeline changes, infrastructure migrations).\n- \"Staging environment\" testing may not capture production-specific issues (different data volumes, latency constraints affecting log persistence, different infrastructure configurations).\n- The proposal focuses on \"presence and correctness of mandatory logs\" but Article 12(2) requires that logging be *appropriate to the intended purpose* — this is a more nuanced requirement than binary presence/absence checks. A system might have logs present but they may not capture the right events for traceability.\n- The phrase \"without hindering initial rapid experimentation\" suggests it's positioned as lightweight, which could mean it doesn't deeply validate whether the logging actually meets the substantive requirements (e.g., does it truly enable post-market monitoring per Article 72?).\n- It only addresses the *system's* technical capability for logging, not the broader documentation and record-keeping obligations that Recital 71 emphasizes (technical documentation kept up to date throughout lifetime).\n\nHowever, from a pure compliance vantage point:\n- This is a genuinely useful control. Having an automated gate that catches missing or incorrect logging before deployment significantly reduces the risk of shipping a non-compliant high-risk system.\n- It creates an audit trail (the CI/CD logs themselves become evidence of due diligence).\n- It institutionalizes compliance rather than relying on manual checks.\n- For market reputation: it demonstrates to regulators and business partners that we have systematic quality gates for AI compliance.\n\nThe main gap is that it's a point-in-time check at deployment, not a continuous lifecycle verification. Article 12 requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" and Recital 71 emphasizes keeping documentation up to date throughout the lifetime. A CI/CD gate helps but doesn't fully close this loop — you'd also need runtime monitoring to confirm logs are actually being generated correctly in production.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, directly relevant compliance control that addresses the most critical risk (shipping non-compliant logging). It's not complete on its own for full Article 12 lifecycle compliance, but as one component of a broader compliance program, it's highly valuable. From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this is clearly worth approving. It's a concrete, implementable control with direct regulatory mapping.\n\nI'll approve it with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. High because it directly addresses the \"shall\" requirement in Article 12(1) and creates a systematic control. Not perfect (90+) because it only covers the deployment-time check and doesn't address continuous lifetime logging verification, and the \"without hindering rapid experimentation\" framing might lead to under-scoping of what \"correctness\" means for substantive traceability requirements.\n",
