g solutions. This is a strong compliance posture.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate systematic, standardized compliance infrastructure signals maturity to business partners and regulators.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Without requiring researchers to write custom code\" - this is good for adoption but raises the question of whether it can handle all edge cases across different system types. However, from a compliance perspective, standardization is a strength, not a weakness.\n- Integration with existing compute clusters - practical concern but doesn't undermine the compliance value.\n- The proposal says \"automatically\" which aligns perfectly with Article 12(1)'s \"automatic recording of events.\"\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis is a directly on-target implementation of Article 12's core requirement. It addresses the \"technically allow for automatic recording\" mandate, ensures consistency across projects (critical for institutional compliance), and provides an auditable single point of reference. For Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, the middleware hooks capturing input data, timestamps, and verification IDs map directly to the minimum requirements in Article 12(3)(a)-(d).\n\nThis is a high-value compliance proposal. It reduces regulatory risk (non-conformity due to inconsistent or missing logging), reduces audit burden (one implementation to explain vs. many), and strengthens our market position as an organization that takes AI governance seriously.\n\nDecision: Approve. This directly implements the core obligation of Article 12 in a way that protects institutional compliance posture.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this is very strong. It's not just \"nice to have\" - it's implementing a mandatory technical requirement (Article 12(1) uses \"shall technically allow\"). The cross-project standardization aspect makes it even more valuable from an institutional compliance standpoint because it eliminates the risk of one project having inadequate logging while others are fine. I'd rate this around 85-90. It's not a 100 because there could be implementation gaps (does it truly cover ALL Annex III categories? Does it handle the \"lifetime\" requirement for systems with very long operational periods?), but from my compliance vantage point, it's extremely promising and directly addresses a mandatory obligation.\n\nI'll go with 88.\n",
