point, a single synchronized repository serving as \"primary evidence\" is more efficient than scattered documentation across teams.\n\n**Arguments for rejection / concerns:**\n- \"Mandatory\" and \"before any model deployment stage\" could create significant friction in R&D workflows. Researchers iterating quickly on experiments might find this gating mechanism slows down scientific exploration, especially during exploratory phases that aren't yet heading toward deployment.\n- The scope is broad: tracking evolution of algorithms AND training data across the entire lifetime. For a lab with many projects, the maintenance overhead could be substantial relative to funding.\n- \"Comprehensive traceability\" and \"permanent audit trail\" suggest potentially very large storage and curation requirements over time.\n- However, these are implementation details. The proposal itself is about establishing the protocol/framework.\n\n**My assessment from resource-allocation perspective:**\nThis is fundamentally a compliance necessity for high-risk AI systems. It's not an optional enhancement - it's the baseline documentation requirement that Recital 71 mandates. Without it, we can't demonstrate compliance. From a resource efficiency standpoint:\n- It leverages existing version control infrastructure (Git, etc.) which most R&D labs already have\n- It creates a single source of truth rather than fragmented documentation\n- The \"gate before deployment\" is a reasonable process step that doesn't require new funding per se - it's a workflow change\n- It's foundational for all other compliance work\n\nThe main resource concern is the ongoing maintenance burden on researchers, but this is inherent to the regulatory requirement and not something we can avoid by rejecting this proposal. We'd need to implement some form of this regardless. The question is whether we do it in an organized, efficient way (this proposal) or in a disorganized, reactive way (worse for resources).\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a compliance necessity that also aligns with good R&D practice (version control, documentation before deployment). It leverages existing infrastructure patterns. The resource cost is real but unavoidable and this approach is probably the most efficient way to handle it.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. It's necessary compliance work that maps well onto existing infrastructure patterns (version control), and a single synchronized repository is more resource-efficient than ad-hoc documentation. However, the \"mandatory before deployment\" gate could create friction in exploratory R&D workflows, and the long-term maintenance burden on researchers is a real cost against our funding constraints. It's not transformative or optional - it's required infrastructure that I'd fund but with some implementation concerns about workflow integration.\n\nI'll go with 74.\n",
