diagnostic model is resource-intensive. If the lab hasn't yet ensured its primary systems meet Article 12 baseline logging requirements, investing in this could be premature.\n- However, it does directly support compliance with Art 12(2)(a) about identifying substantial modifications.\n\nFrom market reputation:\n- Proactively going beyond minimum requirements can demonstrate safety commitment.\n- However, if the monitoring tool itself has issues or produces false positives/negatives, it could create liability.\n- The proposal mentions \"enhancing our market reputation for safety\" which is a valid strategic consideration.\n\nKey concerns from my compliance vantage:\n1. This is an enhancement beyond Article 12's minimum requirements. Article 12 requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" - it doesn't mandate automated analysis or alerting. The proposal adds value but isn't strictly required by the letter of Article 12.\n2. However, Article 12(2)(a) says logging capabilities shall enable \"identifying situations\" of risk or substantial modification. One could argue that merely recording logs doesn't fully satisfy this - you need a way to actually identify those situations from the logs. So there's an argument this is partially required.\n3. The proposal introduces a new AI system (the monitoring tool) which itself might be subject to AI Act requirements depending on how it's used and where it's deployed. This adds compliance complexity.\n4. Resource commitment: This is significant R&D effort. If the basic logging infrastructure isn't solid, this is building on sand.\n\nOverall assessment: The proposal addresses a genuine gap in ensuring Article 12(2)(a) compliance (not just recording but being able to identify risks/modifications from logs). However, it's an enhancement that goes beyond the strict minimum text of Article 12. It has merit for reputation and could support post-market monitoring under Art 72. But it also introduces new complexity (the monitoring AI itself needs governance) and significant resource commitment.\n\nI think this is a reasonable proposal but with caveats. From my compliance perspective, I'd lean toward conditional approval - the concept supports compliance objectives, but the implementation details matter enormously. The promise score should reflect that it's genuinely useful for compliance (identifying substantial modifications is explicitly required under Art 12(2)(a)) but it's an enhancement beyond minimum requirements and introduces new risks.\n\nI'll approve it because:\n- It directly supports Art 12(2)(a) compliance by enabling identification of substantial modifications\n- It supports Art 72 post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b))\n- It enhances market reputation for safety\n- The risk of NOT having this is that our logging becomes truly passive and we can't demonstrate to regulators that we're actively identifying modification risks\n\nBut my promise score won't be at the top because:\n- It's an enhancement beyond strict minimum requirements\n- It introduces a new AI system with its own compliance considerations\n- Resource-intensive relative to the marginal compliance gain over well-designed logging\n- The \"AI-driven\" aspect adds complexity; rule-based monitoring might suffice for many of the Art 12(3) specific requirements\n\nI'd give it around 72-78. It's genuinely useful and supports multiple compliance objectives, but it's not the single most critical thing and has secondary risks. Let me settle on 75.\n",
