ints; training-time logging doesn't.\n- \"Standardized\" is a red flag for speed of prototyping. Building one standardized layer before understanding the specific requirements across all lab systems could be slow and create technical debt if it's wrong.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The pitch is that this \"minimizes manual effort required from scientists, preserving research velocity.\" That's a good goal. But:\n  - Building the middleware itself takes engineering time before any scientist gets relief.\n  - A one-size-fits-all API risks being either too rigid (forcing awkward abstractions) or too loose (not capturing what's actually needed).\n  - The \"shared internal API\" approach means every researcher has to learn and integrate a new dependency. That's onboarding cost.\n  - For prototyping speed specifically, adding a mandatory middleware layer could slow down early-stage experiments where you just want to iterate quickly on model architecture without worrying about compliance plumbing yet.\n- On the quality side: centralized logging infrastructure can improve data consistency and make post-market monitoring easier to implement later. That's a real engineering benefit.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. \"A shared internal API\" is vague. What does it look like? SDK? Sidecar? Agent injection? Each has very different integration costs and technical tradeoffs.\n- It says \"automatically capture required metadata\" but the Article 12(3) requirements for biometric systems (reference databases, specific input data, natural person IDs) are not automatically capturable - they require domain-specific instrumentation that a generic middleware can't just infer.\n- The scope (\"all high-risk AI pipelines\") is very broad for what seems like a single initiative. This could become a long-running platform project rather than a focused engineering deliverable.\n\n**Positives:**\n- The direction is correct: compliance logging should be automated