ering setup and maintenance. Every new project, every new model architecture, every new training pipeline needs to be wired into this system. The marginal cost per new research stream is non-trivial.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering. In an R&D lab, code changes are frequent and exploratory. If every commit triggers documentation updates, we could create a compliance bottleneck or, worse, rubber-stamped documentation that gets auto-generated without meaningful human review. That creates a false sense of compliance while adding tooling overhead.\n- The proposal focuses on the *mechanism* (Sphinx + CI/CD) but doesn't address who owns content quality, what happens during exploratory/research phases where specifications are inherently fluid, or how this interacts with the broader technical documentation required by Article 11 (which is more substantive than just \"up to date\").\n- The promise of \"without slowing down iterative research cycles\" is optimistic. In practice, adding a mandatory documentation gate to every commit will slow things down at least marginally, and researchers may work around it.\n- Resource allocation concern: The initial build is modest (Sphinx config + CI hooks), but the *ongoing maintenance* of keeping templates aligned with evolving model architectures in an R&D context is where costs accumulate. This isn't a one-time build; it's an ongoing infrastructure tax.\n\nHowever, from a pure resource-efficiency standpoint:\n- The alternative to this is manual documentation tracking, which is far more expensive per unit of compliance and far more error-prone. Automating what can be automated is clearly better for resource allocation.\n- Using existing CI/CD infra means we're not adding new systems to maintain — just extending existing ones. That's the most resource-efficient path.\n- The \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" criterion is well-served: Git, CI/CD, Sphinx are standard tools already present or cheaply addable.\n\nOverall 