estion, low-latency inference, alerting pipeline. This is non-trivial engineering work.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype anomaly detector on top of structured logs could be prototyped relatively quickly (weeks) if you have clean logging in place. But the \"real-time\" aspect and integration into existing systems adds time.\n- The proposal frames this as a \"small team\" effort, which is reasonable for an initial prototype but the full production system would need more.\n- However, the dependency on having working logs first means prototyping timeline is gated by another workstream.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Dependency problem: Can't build this without the logging infrastructure existing first. This isn't a standalone deliverable.\n2. The \"existing failure datasets\" assumption is risky and underspecified. Without domain-specific data, the model won't be useful for detecting *our* systems' specific drift patterns.\n3. \"Substantial modification\" detection per Article 12(2)(a) is genuinely hard — it's not just simple threshold-based anomaly detection. It requires understanding what constitutes a meaningful change vs. noise.\n4. The framing as \"transforming regulatory burden into scientific opportunity\" is a nice narrative but doesn't address the core technical challenges.\n5. Building an AI system to monitor other AI systems adds its own complexity, failure modes, and maintenance burden.\n\n**On the positive side:**\n- The concept itself (log-based anomaly detection for drift) is technically sound in principle.\n- If logging infrastructure exists, a lightweight prototype could be built quickly.\n- It addresses a real gap in Article 12(2)(a) compliance — you need something to actually *use* the logs for risk identification.\n\n**My assessment:** The technical concept is viable but the proposal as stated has significant gaps: it assumes data that may not exist, doesn't address the dependency on logging infrastructure being complete first, and underspecifies what \"operational drift\" means in practice for our systems. As a development engineer focused on prototyping speed/quality, I'd say this is a reasonable *direction* but as stated, it's not immediately actionable without more technical definition. The \"small team\" framing might be optimistic given the real-time infrastructure requirements and the ML training challenges.\n\nHowever, I should consider: is this technically feasible? Yes, in principle. Is it quick to prototype? A basic version could be prototyped if you have logs. But the full scope implied (real-time, detecting \"substantial modification,\" integrated into production) is substantial engineering work.\n\nI think from a development/prototyping perspective, I'd lean toward rejecting this as stated because:\n- It's not independently buildable (depends on logging infra)\n- The training data assumption is weak\n- \"Real-time\" adds significant complexity beyond what a \"small team\" can deliver quickly\n- The technical challenge of detecting \"substantial modification\" is much harder than \"anomaly detection\" implies\n\nBut it's not a terrible idea — it's just not ready for commitment as stated. I'd give it a moderate promise score maybe 35-45. It's a reasonable direction but needs the logging infrastructure first and more specific technical definition before we commit resources.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise around 38. The idea is in the right space but the proposal as written commits us to building something whose prerequisites aren't met and whose core technical challenge (detecting \"substantial modification\" specifically) is underspecified and harder than the framing suggests.\n",
