ture NOW for our high-risk systems. A research exploration into how to better analyze those logs doesn't satisfy the baseline requirement; it's an enhancement on top of it.\n- The proposal says \"explores\" and \"will develop new anomaly detection algorithms\" — this is exploratory R&D, not a compliance implementation project. My mandate is about ensuring we meet regulatory requirements, not about building cutting-edge AI safety research.\n- However, there IS value: if our logging infrastructure produces data that's actually analyzable for risk detection, it strengthens our post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operational monitoring (Article 26(5)) capabilities. The proposal directly supports what Article 12(2)(a), (b), and (c) require the logs to enable.\n- The \"scholarly contribution\" framing is a marketing wrapper, but the substance — building anomaly detection on regulatory logs — is genuinely relevant to compliance. If we can demonstrate to regulators that our logging doesn't just capture events passively but actively flags risk situations, that's a stronger compliance posture than mere data storage.\n- Risk: This could be seen as over-engineering or scope creep if it delays the basic implementation of Article 12 requirements. But it doesn't seem to replace the baseline — it builds on it.\n\n**My assessment:**\nAs Compliance Officer, my primary concern is: does this help us meet our obligations, protect institutional survival, and maintain market reputation? \n\nThe core compliance obligation under Article 12 is that systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" That's a technical implementation requirement. This proposal goes beyond that to analyze those logs for risk detection. While that's valuable for post-market monitoring and demonstrating traceability, it's framed as exploratory research rather than a compliance deliverable.\n\nHowever — and this is important — Article 12(2) says logging capabilities \"shall enable the 