ed on neural activation patterns\" could be a 5-year research program or a focused 6-month study depending on scope. But that's an implementation/resource concern more than a merit concern from my vantage point.\n- The connection to Article 12 is somewhat surface-level in the proposal — it says \"satisfying Article 12 requirements\" but the actual scientific contribution (understanding failure modes, defining meaningful events) stands independently of the regulation. This is actually good — the research has value regardless of regulatory context.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a genuinely interesting research direction at the intersection of interpretability, uncertainty quantification, and model monitoring. The core scientific questions are open and important. The intellectual merit is solid because it tackles non-trivial problems (defining meaningful events algorithmically, connecting latent space states to operational risk). The broader impact is real — understanding when models enter risky regimes has applications well beyond regulatory compliance.\n\nHowever, I should note that the proposal is somewhat high-level and could benefit from more specific methodological grounding. But from a pure \"is this intellectually worthy of research attention\" standpoint, it clears my bar comfortably. It's not incremental; it's asking questions we don't have good answers to yet.\n\nI'd rate this around 78-82. It's genuinely interesting frontier work with clear scientific value, but the vagueness in methodology and the somewhat broad scope make me slightly less than enthusiastic. The core idea (semantic vs. temporal event logging, latent space risk triggers) is strong, but the path from \"we'll do this\" to actual scientific contribution isn't fully articulated. Still, it's clearly above my bar for approval.\n\nLet me settle on 80. It's solid frontier research with real intellectual merit and broader impact, but the proposal as stated could be sharper methodologically.\n"