        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1689\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This is the governance prerequisite that makes Article 12 compliance actionable at all: without a reliable, documented process to determine which of our systems fall into the high-risk tier (and specifically Annex III point 1(a) for biometric verification logging under Art. 12(3)), we cannot scope our record-keeping obligations and risk deploying a system in violation with mandatory logging intact—exposing us to fines up to €35 M or 7 % of global turnover and to immediate market-reputational damage with regulators and enterprise clients who will audit our classification methodology. The proposal is somewhat generic on mechanics (board composition, decision thresholds, re-evaluation triggers), but the core control—a standing multidisciplinary gate at design phase—is a near-necessary condition for institutional survival under this Act and should be resourced now rather than retrofitted after a market-surveillance query.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P1689 as a Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically in the context of Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from my vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is a \"High-Risk AI Classification Protocol\" — essentially an internal process to categorize R&D projects by EU AI Act risk tier, establishing a multidisciplinary review board that evaluates project specs against Annex III criteria during initial design phase. The stated purpose is to identify which systems must adhere to Article 12 requirements, prevent deployment of non-compliant high-risk systems, and optimize resource allocation toward critical regulatory controls.\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- This is fundamentally about ensuring we know WHICH systems are subject to Article 12 in the first place. Without proper classification, we cannot even begin to comply with record-keeping obligations because we don't know what applies.\n- A multidisciplinary review board during the initial design phase is a sensible governance mechanism — it catches misclassification early rather than after deployment.\n- It directly supports \"institutional survival\" (avoiding penalties for non-compliance), \"regulatory compliance\" (knowing your obligations), and \"market reputation\" (demonstrating due diligence to regulators and customers).\n- The EU AI Act's risk-based approach means that without proper triage, you either over-invest in compliance for low-risk systems or under-invest for high-risk ones — both are costly.\n- This is a prerequisite for ANY Article 12 implementation work. You can't implement logging capabilities if you don't know which systems need them.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it describes the \"what\" (a review board, a classification process) but not much of the \"how.\" What are the specific criteria? Who sits on the board? What happens at each stage?\n- It could become bureaucratic overhead if not well-scoped. A multidisciplinary review board for every single R&D project might be resource-heavy.\n- However, from a compliance standpoint, the existence of such a protocol is essentially non-negotiable. The EU AI Act requires providers to understand whether their system is high-risk before they can comply with any specific obligations including Article 12.\n- It's not directly about Article 12 implementation (logging capabilities), but it's a critical upstream dependency.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a foundational governance control. Without knowing which systems are high-risk, we literally cannot comply with Article 12 for those systems. It protects against the catastrophic scenario of deploying a system that is high-risk (under Annex III) without implementing mandatory logging, which would be a direct violation subject to significant fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover). From a market reputation standpoint, having documented classification processes shows regulators we are taking our obligations seriously.\n\nI think this clears my bar strongly. It's not a flashy technical proposal but it's the governance backbone that makes everything else possible. I'd approve it. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. It's essential but somewhat generic in its description, and there's execution risk (the board could become a rubber stamp or an over-burdensome process). But from my vantage point, the existence of such a protocol is nearly mandatory for compliance.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 87.\n",
