ffice reviewing it.\n\nBut here's my concern from the Lab Director vantage: This proposal doesn't address the actual hard problem, which is building and maintaining the logging infrastructure itself (Article 12(1) — \"technically allow for automatic recording\"). A sign-off gate checks that logs exist, but the real resource commitment is in engineering those capabilities into the systems. The proposal is a governance wrapper around what should be an engineering capability. As a Lab Director focused on resource allocation, I'd want to see resources directed toward actually building the logging capabilities (which would come from other proposals) rather than adding another approval layer.\n\nAlso: \"protects our market reputation by preventing non-compliant systems from ever leaving the lab environment\" — this is a nice benefit statement but somewhat self-evident. Any responsible lab wouldn't deploy non-compliant systems. The value-add of a formal checklist vs. just having project leads ensure compliance before deployment is marginal.\n\nDecision: I'm leaning toward reject, or a low approve. Let me think about whether it clears my bar.\n\nMy bar from this vantage: Does this proposal make efficient use of scarce resources? Does it balance scientific ambition with funding constraints? Does it effectively leverage existing infrastructure?\n\nThe proposal adds a mandatory gate that consumes project lead time and compliance office bandwidth for every high-risk system. In a resource-constrained lab, this is a non-trivial recurring cost (every single system going through testing/deployment). The benefit — catching non-compliant logging before deployment — is real but modest, because if the engineering team built the system to spec, it would already be compliant. The gate primarily catches cases where teams cut corners on logging, which is more of an engineering discipline issue than a governance issue.\n\nI think I'll reject this. It's a reasonable idea in isolation, but from my vantage point on resource efficiency and scientific throughput, adding another mandatory approval layer to every project's pipeline consumes finite human hours (project leads preparing demos, compliance office reviewing) without proportional benefit. The more efficient approach is to embed logging requirements into the engineering specs and code reviews that already exist, rather than creating a separate audit gate. This proposal, as framed, creates parallel bureaucracy in an already resource-constrained environment.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 35-40. It's not a bad idea, and the core principle (verify before deploy) is sound. But from my specific vantage point on resource efficiency, it adds friction without clear proportional benefit, and the framing as a standalone \"framework\" suggests overhead that could be avoided by integration into existing processes.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 35.\n",
