ndatory Compliance Ledger where researchers document... in real-time\" is the main concern from my vantage. During rapid prototyping cycles (try an architecture, test it, iterate), adding mandatory documentation steps at each iteration creates overhead. This could slow down experimentation.\n- However, if the system is well-designed with auto-population (pulling model cards, training configs, validation results from existing pipeline artifacts), the manual burden decreases significantly.\n- The cryptographic linkage to model versions is a good idea technically — it ensures documentation integrity and makes audit trails clean. It's a small engineering lift (hash + store reference).\n- A centralized registry vs. distributed docs: centralization helps with compliance but during prototyping, teams may work on multiple variants simultaneously. If the registry requires a \"single\" version-controlled document per system, it could create merge conflicts or workflow friction when multiple researchers are iterating.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and technically viable — we're essentially building an enhanced documentation system with model-version pinning. The cryptographic linkage is elegant and low-cost to implement. The main risk from my vantage is the \"real-time mandatory\" aspect adding friction to prototyping speed. But this can be mitigated through good automation (auto-populating fields from pipeline metadata).\n\nThe proposal addresses Recital 71 well — it's about keeping technical documentation up-to-date and traceable. From a development engineering standpoint, this is a legitimate infrastructure need that would actually help us in the long run: when we need to spin up a new prototype variant of a high-risk system, having a registry with versioned docs and model hashes makes it easier to understand what changed and ensure compliance from day one.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable (no exotic tech required), it serves a rea