jects start with logging already wired in.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The promise to \"eliminate the risk of missing regulatory data while preserving our development speed\" is appealing. If logging is baked into the template, engineers don't have to add it manually per project.\n- A decorator approach is lightweight and non-invasive — you wrap a function/class and get logging for free. This is good for prototyping speed.\n- However, there's a risk: if the wrapper is too rigid or adds overhead (e.g., writing to a \"secure database\" on every call), it could slow down iterative prototyping loops. During rapid experimentation, you might not want every single inference logged to a persistent store.\n- The proposal doesn't address performance considerations — what happens when you're doing thousands of inferences during testing/tuning? Writing each one to a \"secure database\" adds I/O latency.\n- It also doesn't address how the wrapper handles multi-step pipelines, async calls, batch processing, or streaming inference — all common patterns in AI prototyping.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — standardize logging via a decorator/wrapper in the base template — is technically sound and aligns well with development speed goals. It's a reasonable engineering pattern. However, it's somewhat superficial:\n- It doesn't address the specific data fields required by Article 12(3) (reference database, verification persons).\n- \"System states\" is too vague to be implementable without further specification.\n- Performance implications of persistent logging during prototyping aren't addressed.\n- The scope (\"any model flagged as high-risk\") doesn't match the differentiated requirements in the regulation.\n\nThat said, as a *starting point* for an engineering implementation, it's a viable architectural direction. It's not a complete solution but it's a reasonable first component. From a prototyping speed perspective, having logging pre-w