nd reference to stored data.\n- It mentions \"reference database versions\" but Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" Version tracking seems adequate for this.\n- The proposal is specifically scoped to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics, border control, critical infrastructure) based on what it captures (reference databases, matches, verification of results). It doesn't seem to address the broader logging requirements in Article 12(2)(a)-(c) for all high-risk AI systems. But wait - re-reading it, it says \"every high-risk prototype is compliant by default,\" which suggests a broader scope. The specific implementation details mentioned (reference database versions, input hashes, matches) lean toward Annex III 1(a), but the framework itself could be extensible.\n- Technical viability: A wrapper library around model serving is very standard in ML engineering (similar to what you'd get from frameworks like TensorFlow Serving's logging hooks, or custom middleware). This is well within the scope of a dev team to build and maintain.\n- Speed of prototyping: This actually INCREASES prototyping speed by removing boilerplate. A researcher spinning up a new high-risk prototype just imports the library and wraps their serving endpoint - they don't need to design logging from scratch each time.\n- Quality: Standardized, consistent logging across all prototypes means more reliable post-market monitoring data and easier compliance audits.\n\n**Assessment:** This is a technically sound, well-scoped infrastructure project that directly serves my domain (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality). The engineering approach is straightforward - build a middleware/wrapper library with standardized hooks. It's not cutting-edge research; it's solid platform engineering. The risk of failure is low. The benefit to the org is high because it removes a compliance burden from every individual prototyping effort and replaces it with one shared infrastructure investment.\n\nThe main question is whether this is \"approving\" in scope - yes, this is a clear, bounded engineering project. It's not \"explore if logging might someday work\" - it's \"build a specific library that does X.\" The technical approach is well-understood.\n\nI'd give this a high promise score. From my vantage point, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes prototyping faster and more consistent while ensuring compliance. It's technically viable with standard engineering practices. I'll approve it with a high promise score - maybe 82-85. Not 100 because:\n- The scope seems slightly narrow (leaning toward Annex III 1(a) specifics in the implementation details mentioned)\n- \"Input hashes\" vs actual input data is a potential gap that needs clarification\n- It's an internal library, so it requires ongoing maintenance and versioning as model serving infrastructure evolves\n\nBut these are manageable concerns. I'll go with 83.\n",
