ntation, which accelerates prototyping cycles (no time spent writing up what parameters were used).\n- However, there's a risk of over-engineering if \"structured ledger\" gets too ambitious. But the core concept is sound and shippable fast.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- The proposal addresses technical documentation currency (\"keeping records... kept up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime\"). This aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on training, testing and validation processes being documented.\n- It partially supports Article 12(2)(b) - facilitating post-market monitoring by having a record of what was trained when, on what data.\n- It doesn't directly address the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" in the operational sense (runtime logs of the deployed system), but it does address the development/training phase documentation which Recital 71 explicitly calls for.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal says \"integrate hooks into our ML frameworks\" - this implies modifying or wrapping internal frameworks, which could introduce friction into existing prototyping workflows if not done carefully. But a well-designed non-invasive wrapper (callback-based) would minimize disruption.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded for an initial implementation.\n- It's not solving the full Article 12 problem (it doesn't cover runtime logging of deployed systems), but it's a legitimate, well-scoped piece of the puzzle that addresses the training/documentation side.\n\n**Decision:** This is technically viable, well-scoped, quickly prototypeable, and directly relevant to part of the regulatory requirement. From my vantage point as a development engineer concerned with technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this clears the bar. It's a practical engineering task with clear success criteria, low technical risk, and immediate utility in reducing documentation overhead.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solidly viable, quickly imple