ent lifecycle — exactly what regulators look for when assessing whether an organization has a robust compliance management system.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- Catching record-keeping gaps early prevents situations where the institution must recall or redesign systems due to logging failures discovered post-deployment. This is a significant cost saver and risk mitigator.\n- If a regulator inspects and finds incomplete logs, the penalties under the AI Act can be substantial (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for certain violations). A validation gate reduces this exposure.\n\n**Market reputation:**\n- Demonstrating a robust logging validation process signals to customers and partners that the institution takes compliance seriously. This is a competitive differentiator, especially in enterprise/B2B contexts where buyers increasingly demand AI Act compliance assurance.\n\n**Potential concerns/limitations from my vantage:**\n- It's an internal testing tool — it validates technical capability but doesn't itself constitute compliance documentation. The logs produced by the test runs would need to be retained as evidence of validation.\n- \"Edge-case failures\" is somewhat vague — what specific edge cases? But that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the concept.\n- It's scoped to pre-pilot-release, which aligns with Article 12(1)'s requirement for lifetime logging but focuses on proving the capability exists before deployment. This is appropriate.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a directly relevant, high-value compliance control that addresses the core requirement of Article 12. It's not merely aspirational — it's a concrete engineering deliverable with clear regulatory justification. From my vantage point, this strengthens our ability to demonstrate compliance, reduce regulatory risk, and protect institutional reputation. The \"final technical gate\" framing is exactly the kind of structured control that gives me confidence in our compliance posture.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — it directly serves my core mandate. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 100 because: (a) it addresses one article among many, (b) its value depends on the test suite actually being comprehensive and maintained over time (living documentation requirement from Recital 71), and (c) it doesn't by itself create the compliance records we'd need to present to authorities — those come from production logging. But as a validation control for Article 12 specifically, it's very strong.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
