        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2455 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"High-Risk System Observability Dashboard\" - A telemetry platform for post-market monitoring and risk identification as mandated by Article 12. It's a centralized dashboard aggregating live logs to detect performance drift or anomalous failure modes. The framing is that it transforms regulatory burden into a technical asset for debugging production systems.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1) requirement for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification.\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(b) - facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72.\n- A centralized dashboard for log aggregation is a sensible architectural approach to ensuring logs are available and accessible for compliance verification.\n- The \"lifetime of the system\" requirement means we need persistent, queryable logging infrastructure, not just ephemeral debug output.\n- Having a dedicated observability platform demonstrates to regulators that we've taken record-keeping seriously as an institutional capability, not an afterthought.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal is quite high-level. It says \"aggregates live logs\" but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires very specific data: start/end timestamps of each use, reference database used, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. A generic \"observability dashboard\" doesn't necessarily capture these specific fields unless explicitly designed for them.\n- The proposal frames this as \"debugging production systems\" which is an engineering motivation, not a compliance one.