tails about retention periods, data fields for Annex III systems, tamper-evidence, etc.), but as a resource-commitment approval, building this platform is necessary and appropriate.\n\nThe \"transforms regulatory burden into technical asset\" framing is a bit of sales language, but the underlying substance - that we need centralized log infrastructure to comply with Article 12 - is correct and important.\n\nI'll approve it because:\n- It addresses the core architectural requirement of Article 12(1) and (2)\n- Without this, we simply cannot demonstrate compliance\n- It's a necessary investment for institutional survival in the EU market\n- The post-market monitoring capability directly supports our obligations under Articles 72 and 79\n\nBut I'll note that it needs to be scoped carefully to include the specific Article 12(3) data fields and retention requirements. As stated, it's slightly too focused on \"performance drift\" (an engineering concern) rather than full regulatory traceability. Still, as a foundational platform commitment, it clears my bar.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly necessary and directionally correct, but the framing is somewhat narrow (focused on debugging/performance rather than full regulatory audit trail), and it doesn't explicitly address retention, integrity, or the specific Annex III data fields. As a compliance officer, I'd approve it as infrastructure investment but would want to see those specifics nailed down in implementation. I'll go with 74.\n",
