eanly. This could slow down prototyping if teams are forced to conform to a rigid interface that doesn't map well to their system's natural event flow.\n- \"Mandatory APIs\" — the word \"mandatory\" suggests a governance/policy layer on top of the technical one. From a pure engineering perspective, the technical library itself is fine; the mandate is an org decision. But if the APIs are poorly designed or too rigid, they become friction in prototyping cycles.\n- \"Secure, immutable storage system\" — building this is non-trivial (WORM buckets, access control, retention policies). It's not just a library; it's infrastructure. The scope here seems to conflate a logging SDK with backend infrastructure. That could slow down the initial prototype significantly.\n- The proposal doesn't mention how it handles Article 12(2)'s requirement that logging be \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" — different systems need different event granularities. A unified framework needs flexibility in what events are captured, not just a fixed set of fields.\n- \"Guarantees institutional readiness for regulatory audits\" is a strong claim. The technical library can help, but audit readiness also depends on data completeness, metadata quality, and organizational processes — not just having a logging call in the code.\n\n**Technical viability assessment:**\nThe core idea — a shared logging SDK with structured event schemas piped to append-only storage — is technically very feasible. It's essentially building an internal observability/compliance layer. The technology is well-understood (structured logging, message queues, WORM storage). A prototype could be built quickly: define event schemas for Article 12 requirements, wrap them in a thin SDK, and pipe to something like an append-only store or a database with no-update policies.\n\nThe risk to prototyping speed: if the framework is too rigid or if it requires significant architectural changes to existing systems before teams can adopt it,