so covers systems that are safety components of products regulated under specific EU harmonisation legislation (Annex I). A triage protocol that ONLY looks at Annex III criteria would miss these cases. This is a significant compliance gap.\n- The framing of \"only allocate expensive storage and compute resources to systems that actually require them legally\" has a cost-minimization undertone. From a compliance perspective, the risk is under-scoping: if you misclassify something as non-high-risk when it is, you face enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover), and reputational damage. The protocol should be designed with an \"in doubt, err on the side of compliance\" principle rather than a cost-savings framing.\n- However, the core idea — having a formal classification gate before committing resources — is sound and important for institutional survival.\n\nOverall assessment:\nThe proposal addresses a genuine and critical need (knowing when Article 12 obligations apply), but as written it has gaps that could create compliance risk (Annex III-only focus, monthly cadence potentially too slow, cost-savings framing rather than compliance-first framing). As a Compliance Officer, I would want this to exist but with modifications. \n\nHowever, my job here is to judge whether this proposal clears my bar for committing implementation resources. The core concept is sound and necessary — you MUST have some form of classification determination before you can comply with Article 12 (or any other high-risk obligation). Without it, the institution is flying blind.\n\nBut the specific design as proposed has meaningful gaps:\n1. Annex III only misses Annex I safety component cases under Art 6(1)\n2. Monthly cadence may be insufficient for fast-moving R&D\n3. The cost-minimization framing could lead to under-classification risk\n\nAs a Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival and regulatory compliance, I think the core idea is approvable in principle 