posal is directionally aligned with what compliance needs (interpretable, comprehensive logs that facilitate monitoring and modification detection). However, as a \"research initiative\" focused on \"novel methods,\" it's more of an exploratory R&D project than a compliance implementation. From my vantage point, I'd want to see:\n- Clear mapping to specific Article 12 sub-requirements\n- Defined deliverables with timelines\n- Integration into our existing logging infrastructure plan\n- Risk assessment around publishing\n\nBut the instruction says: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" So I'm not funding exploration — I'm committing implementation resources. A research initiative that will \"investigate novel methods\" and \"publish findings\" is not really an implementation project from compliance's perspective. It doesn't produce a compliant logging system; it produces papers on how to potentially make logs more interpretable.\n\nHowever, the proposal does say it \"directly address[es] the legal requirements for monitoring high-risk systems.\" If the research produces methods that we then deploy in our logging infrastructure, that would be compliance-relevant. But there's a gap between researching novel anomaly detection and having a deployed, validated, regulator-ready logging system.\n\nI think from a strict compliance vantage point:\n- The direction is right (interpretable logs for post-market monitoring)\n- But as framed, it's research, not implementation\n- No clear regulatory deliverables or timelines\n- Publishing adds some reputational benefit but also some risk\n- It doesn't create a near-term compliance capability\n\nI'll lean toward approve with moderate promise. The direction is genuinely aligned with what Article 12 and Recital 71 ask for (comprehensible information, traceability, facilitating post-market monitoring). The \"substantial modification\" detection angle maps well to Article 12(2)(