re validating that your logging design actually meets regulatory requirements before you commit to full implementation.\n- However, there's a dependency: it presupposes some level of existing or near-existing logging infrastructure to test against. If the lab is still in very early stages and has no logging architecture at all, this sandbox would be testing... nothing. But even then, building the sandbox alongside the initial logging prototype is reasonable — you build both in parallel.\n- The \"production-like data streams\" requirement adds some complexity — you need realistic synthetic data that exercises the logging paths meaningfully. This is doable but requires domain knowledge of what the high-risk system actually processes.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Production-like data streams\" could be scope-creep risk if interpreted as requiring a full production replica. But even a simplified mock with representative data patterns would serve the purpose.\n- The proposal says it \"verifies technical viability... before actual deployment\" — this is good engineering practice (test-before-deploy), which aligns well with my concern for prototyping quality.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a solid, technically feasible engineering task that directly serves the goal of validating whether our logging implementation meets Article 12(2)(a) requirements. It's not a moonshot — it uses established techniques (fault injection, synthetic data, sandboxed testing). It improves prototyping speed by catching design flaws early rather than in production. The scope is bounded and achievable.\n\nThe main risk is that \"production-like\" could balloon the scope, but even at minimum viable form (basic anomaly injection into a mock pipeline), it's useful.\n\nI would approve this. It's a practical engineering deliverable with clear technical feasibility and good ROI for prototyping speed/quality. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid but not groundbreaking — it's necessary infrastructure work, well-defined, and achievable, but it also doesn't carry the same \"wow factor\" as something more novel. However, from a pure technical viability and prototyping standpoint, it scores well because it's bounded, uses known techniques, and directly de-risks the main implementation. I'll go with 75.\n",
