equirement efficiently.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- A dedicated R&D track with a small team to \"explore\" compressed formats and filtering algorithms — this is slow. For a high-risk AI system that needs logging capability, I can prototype a straightforward structured logging solution (e.g., JSON/protobuf event logs with appropriate indexing) in days or weeks, not months of research into compression algorithms.\n- The \"intelligent event-filtering\" part adds complexity without clear regulatory benefit and actually adds risk to compliance.\n- However, there IS a legitimate technical concern here: for very high-throughput systems, naive logging CAN become a performance bottleneck. But the solution is typically engineering (async write-ahead logs, tiered storage, appropriate data formats) not \"research into compressed logging formats.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a dev engineering / prototyping speed perspective, this proposal has several issues:\n1. It's framed as research/exploration rather than implementation — but Article 12 is a concrete requirement that needs a working solution, not an academic exploration.\n2. The \"intelligent event-filtering\" component conflicts with the minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3) — you can't filter out required data points.\n3. Compressed logging formats are a nice-to-have optimization, not a core requirement. The regulation doesn't mandate any particular format or compression scheme.\n4. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is slow and over-engineered for what the regulation actually requires.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge: there IS a legitimate engineering challenge in logging at scale without degrading performance. But the way this is framed — as a \"research project\" with \"publishable scientific contribution\" ambitions — suggests it's not focused on delivering a working solution quickly. It's more of an academic exercise dressed up as compliance response.\n\nThe promise score: From my vantage point, the core insight (logging at scale has performance implications) is valid but well-known engineering territory, not really requiring \"dedicated R&D track\" exploration. The specific proposals (compressed formats, intelligent filtering) are either unnecessary or risky for compliance. I'd say this is maybe a 25-30 — there's a kernel of legitimate concern but the execution plan is misaligned with what's actually needed and introduces compliance risk through the filtering component.\n\nDecision: reject. The proposal doesn't clearly lead to a working, compliant logging solution; it proposes an exploratory research track for optimizations that aren't required by regulation, while introducing \"intelligent filtering\" which could conflict with minimum logging requirements. From my speed/quality of prototyping perspective, this is slow, over-scoped, and potentially compliance-risky.\n\nPromise: I'll give it around 25. The underlying concern about performance at scale is real but doesn't warrant this framing, and the specific technical approaches proposed are either unnecessary or risky.\n",
