dvancing the knowledge frontier.\" The regulation asks for specific things (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, identification of verifiers). It does NOT ask for causal tracing or attribution methods or internal activation recording.\n- From a resource-allocation perspective: if we have limited engineering resources to implement Article 12 compliance across our high-risk systems, spending them on speculative research into \"semantic provenance\" means the actual compliance deadline might not be met with sufficient buffer.\n- The \"turning regulatory burden into scientific contribution\" framing is appealing but potentially misleading from a resource perspective. Scientific contributions take time and don't produce immediate compliance artifacts. We need working logs NOW (or at least before the system goes live), not in 3 years when the research produces papers.\n- However, there IS a legitimate kernel: if we're building logging infrastructure anyway, designing it with extensibility for richer metadata could be efficient use of engineering time. But that's an engineering design choice, not a \"research project.\"\n- The scope is very broad: \"causal tracing and attribution methods\" + \"new metadata schemas for high-dimensional state logging\" - this sounds like multiple research programs, not a focused implementation task.\n- From effective use of existing R&D infrastructure perspective: does the lab already have the computational infrastructure to log high-dimensional internal activations? That's expensive in storage and compute. For large models, logging every activation is prohibitively resource-intensive.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has some legitimate overlap with compliance needs (better logging design), but it overreaches significantly into exploratory research territory that goes well beyond what Article 12 requires. The regulatory text asks for specific things: automatic recording of events, period of use, reference databases, 