ce perspective, it's actually safer to over-capture than under-capture, especially since classification of \"high-risk\" can shift as systems evolve.\n- The proposal mentions Article 12(3) requirements (timestamps, reference databases, input data matches) which specifically apply to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). For other high-risk categories, the logging requirements under Art 12(2)(a)-(c) are broader — they need to capture events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The proposal doesn't explicitly address these broader logging needs for non-Annex III-1(a) systems.\n- \"Eliminates human error\" is an overstatement but the direction is correct.\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, access controls, data protection (GDPR intersection with logging personal data), or log integrity/auditability — all important compliance considerations.\n- Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5)) — the proposal doesn't explicitly mention this.\n\nHowever, from a pure compliance officer's vantage point focused on institutional survival and regulatory compliance:\n- This is a core operational requirement. Without it, we literally cannot comply with Article 12(1) which uses \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" This is not optional.\n- A unified approach reduces the risk of gaps in compliance coverage.\n- The proposal addresses the specific minimum requirements enumerated in Art 12(3).\n- It's a foundational infrastructure piece that enables all other compliance activities (post-market monitoring, traceability, responding to regulator inquiries).\n\nThe overbreadth (\"all R&D projects\") is a minor operational concern but not a compliance failure — if anything, it's safer. The missing elements (retention, GDPR intersection, Art 12(3)(d) person identification) are implementation details that would be 