ewer). This means you can potentially attract external funding or grant support to offset internal costs.\n4. **Internal cost savings**: If the lab develops its own efficient logging infrastructure, it avoids paying premium for third-party compliance tooling. Over multiple AI systems and their lifetimes (the Act requires logs \"over the lifetime of the system\"), this compounds significantly.\n\nConcerns:\n1. **Scope risk**: \"Core R&D track\" is ambitious. If you dedicate significant researcher time to a research project on compressed log formats, that's time not spent on your primary AI development work. The question is whether the efficiency gains justify the research investment. However, the proposal frames it as meeting legal standards — so there IS a mandatory floor of work required anyway. The incremental research beyond the minimum compliance requirement is where the resource concern lies.\n2. **Timing**: Article 12 requires automatic logging \"over the lifetime of the system.\" If you're going to build this in, doing it now (while designing systems) is far more efficient than retrofitting later. This argues for early investment.\n3. **\"Selective sampling\" risk**: I need to be careful here — selective sampling could conflict with the regulatory requirement if done improperly. Article 12(2) requires logs \"relevant for\" specific purposes, and Article 12(3) lists minimum requirements for certain systems. You can't sample away required data points. However, the proposal says \"maintain full traceability,\" which suggests they understand this constraint. The research question would be about efficient encoding of that mandatory data, not omitting it.\n4. **Funding**: As a core R&D track, this needs dedicated personnel and compute for experimentation. In a lab with funding constraints, is this the highest-value use of those researchers? But then again, you MUST implement logging regardless — so the question is whether to do minimal compliance (buy off-the-shelf