ry for Article 12 compliance — what's really needed is that logs are recorded and documentation is kept current, not necessarily that everything flows in real-time. The proposal conflates several technical challenges without clear scoping.\n\nFrom my perspective:\n- It's technically feasible in its core (Git-based doc linking). ✓\n- It addresses a real requirement from the Act. ✓\n- But it's somewhat over-scoped and vague on the hardest part (the metrics-to-regulation mapping). \n- Prototyping speed would be good for the Git portion but uncertain for the \"real-time synchronization\" portion.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this because:\n1. The core pattern is technically sound and prototable quickly.\n2. It addresses a genuine requirement (keeping docs current per Recital 71).\n3. Even if we scope it down to just the Git-linking pipeline, that's valuable infrastructure.\n\nBut my promise score should reflect some uncertainty about whether the full \"real-time data streams\" vision is the right level of engineering investment vs. a simpler \"regenerate docs on each release + log events\" approach being sufficient for compliance.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-good promise score, reflecting that the core is prototable and viable but the full scope needs decomposition before we commit resources.\n",
