earch project aimed at publishing at conferences has no defined timeline, no clear acceptance criteria tied to regulatory compliance, and introduces significant schedule risk.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, I could build a compliant logging layer (structured event capture, timestamps, data references per Article 12(3)) in weeks using existing libraries and patterns. This research project would take months or years with uncertain outcomes.\n- The \"findings will be submitted to top-tier AI conferences\" framing makes the deliverable academic publication rather than a working compliance feature. That's misaligned with what leadership needs: a working system that meets Article 12.\n- There's no mention of integrating this into actual product systems, defining which events need logging for specific high-risk use cases, or meeting the minimum requirements in Article 12(3)(a)-(d).\n\n**My Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is:\n1. Over-engineered relative to what the regulation actually requires. The regulatory requirement is implementable with standard engineering practices today.\n2. Open-ended research without clear acceptance criteria tied to compliance deliverables.\n3. Focused on academic contribution (conference publications) rather than shipping a working logging system.\n4. Introduces significant time-to-delivery risk for a problem that has known solutions.\n\nThis doesn't meet my bar for approving. It's an interesting academic direction, but it's not what we need to meet Article 12 in a timely way with quality engineering deliverables. I would reject this and instead fund direct implementation of logging infrastructure that meets the regulatory requirements using proven patterns.\n\nPromise score: The underlying technical problem (efficient state capture) has some merit, and compression/visualization could be useful long-term. But as framed - a formal research project for conference publication - it's poorly aligned with our engineering needs