        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1991) from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\":\n\nThe proposal describes an automated system that:\n- Links code commits and dataset changes directly to regulatory technical documentation\n- Replaces static reports with a living record of development\n- Implements version-control integration that automatically triggers updates to compliance files when algorithms or training sets are modified\n- Aims to eliminate risk of outdated documentation (Recital 71 requirement)\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is essentially CI/CD pipeline automation applied to compliance documentation. This is technically well-understood. Git webhooks, change detection in version control (code + datasets), triggering documentation generation scripts — these are all standard DevOps practices.\n- The challenge is the \"compliance file\" side: what exactly needs to be updated and how? Recital 71 mentions general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system. Some of this CAN be auto-generated (e.g., model architecture descriptions from code, dataset metadata from version control), but much requires human judgment and narrative explanation. The \"living document\" concept is sound but the automation boundary needs careful scoping.\n- Dataset versioning is a real technical challenge — datasets are large, changes can be subtle (relabeling, augmentation changes), and detecting meaningful vs. trivial changes in a dataset is non-trivial.\n- The trigger mechanism is straightforward: git hooks or CI/CD pipeline stages that detect changes to specific paths/patterns.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype could be built relatively quickly for the code-commit-to-doc-update part (maybe 2-4 weeks for a basic version).\n- The dataset change detection part is harder and would take longer to get right.\n- This doesn't directly impede prototyping speed — in fact, it could help by making compliance documentation generation automated rather than a manual bottleneck after each prototype iteration.\n- However, adding this pipeline overhead early in development could slow down rapid prototyping cycles if not carefully designed (e.g., requiring doc updates before merging).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe idea is technically sound and addresses a real gap (documentation staying current). The core mechanism (version control triggers → doc update) is well-understood engineering. The main technical risks are:\n1. Defining what \"auto-updates\" means for compliance-relevant documentation — much of it requires human narrative, not just code diffs.\n2. Dataset change detection at meaningful granularity.\n3. Not over-engineering this into a bottleneck that slows prototyping.\n\nBut as a concept and initial prototype direction, it's viable. The \"living record\" framing is good because Recital 71 explicitly says documentation should be \"kept up to date... throughout the lifetime.\" This isn't science fiction; it's applying existing CI/CD patterns to a compliance context.\n\nI'd rate this moderately promising. It's technically feasible, addresses a real regulatory need, and can be prototyped incrementally. But there are non-trivial scoping challenges around what can truly be automated vs. what needs human review, and the dataset versioning piece is harder than the code side. From a pure \"can we build a working prototype that demonstrates value\" standpoint, yes, this is doable in reasonable time. It doesn't introduce exotic technology or unproven architecture.\n\nI'll approve it with a promise score around 70-75. It's solid, technically tractable, addresses the regulation well, but has scoping challenges that mean the \"fully automated\" vision is more aspirational than immediately deliverable. The prototype potential is good though — you can show value quickly on the code-side triggers even if the dataset side takes longer.\n\nLet me settle on 72.\n",
