arch matures.\n\n2. **Scope creep / misalignment**: Article 12 is a specific, bounded technical requirement about logging capabilities. The proposal reframes this as \"creating AI architectures natively designed to be interpretable and auditable\" — this is a vastly different (and much more ambitious) undertaking. It conflates compliance logging with general interpretability research. The Act doesn't require self-explaining models; it requires specific event logs.\n\n3. **Theoretical vs. implementable**: A \"theoretical research track\" producing \"self-explaining models\" is not a compliance deliverable. Compliance needs concrete, verifiable logging mechanisms that meet the minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III systems. This proposal doesn't produce anything I can present to a regulator as evidence of compliance.\n\n4. **Resource commitment risk**: Approving this means committing scarce implementation resources to speculative research rather than building the specific logging infrastructure our high-risk systems need NOW. From institutional survival perspective, we need working compliance solutions, not theoretical frameworks that may or may not materialize.\n\n5. **\"Primary functional output\" framing is concerning**: Making logs a \"primary functional output\" of the model could introduce performance trade-offs, add complexity, and potentially create new failure modes — all of which would be compliance risks in themselves. The Act requires logging to work; it doesn't require the AI to \"explain itself.\"\n\n6. **Market reputation angle**: While positioning research as responding to regulation is fine for PR, a Compliance Officer needs assurance that our systems will actually meet Article 12 requirements on schedule. A theoretical track doesn't provide that assurance. It could even be seen by regulators as an avoidance strategy — \"we're still researching how to comply\" rather than demonstrating compliance.\n\n7. **The proposal says \"new theoretic