        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2786\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal conflates a bounded regulatory logging requirement (structured event capture per Art. 12(1)–(3)) with a full data-governance/provenance platform; building a 'middleware layer + versioned data lake integrated with training scripts' to achieve 'absolute traceability of the knowledge frontier's evolution' is a multi-quarter infrastructure build that would stall prototyping velocity, and the claim of zero researcher intervention for lineage mapping through opaque ML pipelines is not technically realistic—Article 12 actually only requires automatic recording of specific event fields (timestamps, reference DB, input matches, verifier IDs), which a lightweight structured-logging shim could deliver in weeks.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 30\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is P2786: \"Automated Provenance Engine\" - a middleware layer that automatically maps data lineages from raw input to final output for every high-risk system run, using a versioned data lake integrated with training scripts to generate Article 12 compliant logs without manual researcher intervention.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The idea of a middleware layer for automatic logging is technically sound in principle. Data lineage tracking is a well-established concept (Apache Atlas, OpenLineage, etc.).\n- However, \"automatically maps data lineages from raw input to final output\" is an extremely ambitious claim. In practice, AI systems—especially deep learning models—have opaque internal transformations. Mapping the exact lineage from a raw input pixel through 100+ layers of neural network computation to a specific output feature is not trivially \"automatic.\" You can track data provenance at the dataset/batch level, but fine-grained per-inference lineage through model internals is far more complex.\n- A \"versioned data lake\" is feasible (tools like Delta Lake, Iceberg exist), but integrating it with training scripts to generate Article 12 compliant logs requires deep understanding of what Article 12 actually demands. The regulation asks for automatic recording of events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring—not necessarily full data lineage in the provenance-tracking sense.\n- \"Without manual researcher intervention\" is a red flag from an engineering standpoint. In practice, every new model architecture, every new pipeline topology will require at least initial configuration. Claiming zero intervention is overpromising.\n- The scope creep here is significant: this isn't just logging—it's building an entire data governance/provenance infrastructure. That's a multi-quarter, multi-team effort, not a prototyping project.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This proposal would consume enormous engineering resources before producing any shippable artifact. The \"middleware layer\" + \"versioned data lake\" + \"integration with training scripts\" is essentially building a platform, not a prototype.\n- For Article 12 compliance specifically, what's actually needed is structured event logging (timestamps, inputs, outputs, model versions, decision records). A full provenance engine is overkill for the regulatory requirement as stated in the text. The regulation asks for automatic recording of events—not for building a complete data lineage system.\n- A much lighter-weight approach (structured logging middleware that captures the specific fields Article 12(3) requires: start/end times, reference database, input data matches, verifier IDs) could be prototyped in weeks rather than months.\n- The \"absolute traceability of the knowledge frontier's evolution\" language is marketing-speak that obscures the actual engineering scope.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal conflates a relatively bounded regulatory requirement (automatic event logging per Article 12) with an extremely ambitious data governance platform. From a prototyping speed perspective, this would tie up significant engineering resources on infrastructure that far exceeds what the regulation actually demands for compliance. The technical claim of \"without manual researcher intervention\" is not realistic for evolving ML systems. A slimmer, more targeted logging middleware would achieve Article 12 compliance much faster with less risk of scope explosion.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge there IS value in some degree of automation—the principle of reducing manual logging burden is correct. And a versioned data store is useful infrastructure. But the packaging here makes it sound like a massive platform build rather than a targeted compliance feature.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n1. The scope massively exceeds what Article 12 technically requires\n2. The \"without manual intervention\" claim is not technically realistic for evolving systems\n3. From a prototyping speed perspective, this would be a multi-month infrastructure project when the actual compliance need can be met with a much lighter-weight solution\n4. The technical feasibility of \"automatic mapping of data lineages from raw input to final output\" through complex ML models is significantly overpromised\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 30-35. There's a kernel of good idea (automate logging, use versioned storage), but the execution scope and technical claims make this unviable as described for our prototyping velocity.\n",
