bility. However, this is appropriate - it's a quality gate for whatever logging system gets built.\n- \"Stress-test our logging outputs against Article 12 requirements\" - this is good but the scope might need to be clearly defined to cover all three sub-points in 12(2) and potentially the Annex III point 1(a) minimums in 12(3) if applicable\n- Resource commitment: building a dedicated testing environment with automated test cases requires engineering resources. But from a compliance perspective, this is a one-time investment that prevents recurring compliance failures.\n- The proposal says \"before any model is deployed\" - this is correct for high-risk systems under the Act\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\n- This directly supports the institution's ability to demonstrate compliance with Article 12 during market surveillance by notified bodies or authorities\n- Creates an internal audit trail of verification (meta-compliance: we can show we tested our logging)\n- Reduces risk of enforcement action under Articles 99-101 (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for non-compliant high-risk systems)\n- Protects market reputation by ensuring deployed systems meet traceability requirements\n- The \"technical gate\" concept means compliance is baked into the development process rather than being a post-hoc check\n\nThis is a strong proposal from a compliance standpoint. It directly operationalizes Article 12 verification, creates institutional safeguards against non-compliance, and protects both regulatory standing and market reputation. The early-detection aspect reduces financial risk of redesigns and potential penalties.\n\nI'd rate this highly. It's not a perfect solution (it doesn't implement logging itself), but as a compliance verification mechanism, it's well-targeted and high-value. I'd approve it with a high promise score. Maybe 85-90 range. It's very aligned with my function but it's one piece of a larger compliance puzzle - the actual logging implemen