iance work across all projects when only some may be in scope.\n\n**Arguments against or concerns:**\n- The audit itself consumes resources: compliance officer time, researcher interview time (pulling lead researchers away from active R&D). But this is a small, bounded cost compared to the benefit of correct scoping.\n- Risk of \"analysis paralysis\" — but the proposal frames it as a prerequisite step, not an open-ended exercise. It has a clear deliverable: categorization by risk tier.\n- The balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: this audit actually *protects* scientific ambition by ensuring compliance engineering doesn't get misdirected to non-applicable projects. Researchers on non-high-risk projects can continue their work without unnecessary logging overhead being built into their pipelines.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a classic \"do the reconnaissance before spending the budget\" move. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is almost unambiguously correct. You cannot allocate scarce engineering resources to compliance implementation if you don't first know which projects trigger those obligations. The cost of the audit (compliance officer time + researcher interviews) is small and bounded. The benefit (correct targeting of expensive engineering work, avoidance of regulatory penalties on missed systems, freeing non-high-risk researchers from unnecessary compliance overhead) is large.\n\nHowever, I should note: this is necessary but not sufficient. It's a scoping step. But the question is whether it clears my bar, not whether it solves everything. And it does clear the bar — it's the logical first step that makes subsequent resource allocation decisions rational rather than guesswork.\n\nThe one slight concern from my vantage: the proposal says \"Compliance officers will audit technical documentation and interview lead researchers.\" This pulls researcher time away from active projects. But this is a bounded, short-term co