ntiers.\n\n**Intellectual merit**: The proposal describes an engineering project — build a tool, train a model on logs, alert compliance officers. There's no clear research question, no hypothesis to test, no methodological novelty described. It says \"develop an anomaly detection model trained on our event logs\" — this is a standard ML pipeline. The intellectual merit from a pure research standpoint is low. It's applied engineering in service of regulatory compliance. There's no mention of contributing to understanding the general problem of AI system drift, or publishing findings, or developing new theoretical frameworks.\n\n**Broader scientific impact**: The proposal explicitly frames this as \"transforms a regulatory burden into a technical asset that improves overall system reliability and safety.\" This is an internal utility tool. Its broader scientific impact is limited — it's designed for our own compliance needs. Unless the research findings (e.g., what patterns indicate substantial modifications in AI systems) are generalized and published, the broader impact is minimal. The proposal doesn't mention any plan for generalization or external contribution to the field.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: there IS a seed of research interest here. The question of \"what constitutes a substantial modification to an AI system\" and how to detect it automatically is genuinely an open research problem at the intersection of ML monitoring and regulatory science. If pursued with genuine intellectual curiosity — not just as a compliance checkbox but as a way to understand the nature of AI system change — it could contribute to the field. But as written, the proposal is framed entirely as an internal engineering deliverable (\"alert compliance officers in real time\"), not as a research endeavor.\n\nMy assessment: From a pure research vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal reads primarily as an engineering/complianc