m, what architecture to use, or how to keep prototyping fast while adding traceability.\n- The \"catastrophic late-stage redesigns\" framing is technically valid — retrofitting observability/logging into ML systems (especially those with complex model pipelines) IS significantly harder than designing for it upfront. So the principle is sound.\n- But as a proposal for me to approve/reject from my technical prototyping vantage point: this doesn't give me actionable technical direction. It's a resource allocation policy that leadership/PMO would own, not something in my domain of concern (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping).\n\nMy assessment:\n- The underlying engineering principle is valid (build it in from the start)\n- But the proposal itself is a process/policy change, not a technical solution\n- It's vague on specifics (\"small percentage,\" \"mandatory compliance budget\" without numbers)\n- From my vantage point, I care about whether I can prototype quickly and build technically viable systems. This proposal doesn't directly advance that — it's an upstream organizational decision\n- However, if resources are guaranteed at the architectural phase, it does protect prototyping speed by preventing later disruptions\n- It's not harmful, but it's also not a technical proposal I would champion or need to approve from my seat\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The principle is sound and aligned with good engineering practice. But as a \"proposal\" that I'm screening for whether to commit scarce implementation resources to pursue — this isn't really an implementation project. It's a policy change. It doesn't require technical prototyping work per se.\n\nDecision: I'll reject it because from my specific vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), this is not a technical proposal that would consume or direct my implementation resources in any meaningful way. It's an organizational/policy suggestion better handled by project ma