l (traceability) which aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on traceability.\n- It addresses the \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" aspect by linking docs to those artifacts.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal is narrow in scope. Article 12(1)-(3) requires automatic recording of events (logs) — this is a technical capability of the system itself, not just documentation management. The proposal doesn't address logging infrastructure (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, identification of natural persons for verification under Art 14(5)).\n- However, my job here is to judge THIS proposal on its own merits, not whether it covers all of Article 12. It's a specific component that addresses one aspect: documentation currency and traceability.\n- The CI/CD approach is practical and aligns with modern software development practices. It creates an immutable record (via version control) which supports audit readiness.\n- \"Hard-linked to code commits\" — this ensures every model change has corresponding documentation, reducing the risk of a gap between what's deployed and what's documented.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is directly relevant to Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation throughout the system lifetime.\n- It reduces regulatory audit risk (stale docs = non-conformity finding).\n- It supports market reputation by demonstrating proactive compliance posture.\n- It's a concrete, implementable engineering solution with clear triggers and outputs.\n- The institutional survival angle: if an auditor finds our technical documentation is stale relative to deployed models, that's a non-conformity under Article 43/Article 16 (provider obligations). This directly mitigates that risk.\n\nWeaknesses from compliance view:\n- It doesn't address the logging requirements of Art 12(1)-(3) per se — those are about the system recording events at runtime, not about documentation management. But 