        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P1619 suggests creating a dedicated internal funding stream to offset increased compute and storage costs associated with record-keeping under Article 12. Let me evaluate this from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me think through this carefully:\n\n**Strengths from my perspective:**\n- It directly addresses the core tension I care about: regulatory compliance costs eating into research budgets. Article 12 mandates automatic logging over the lifetime of high-risk systems, which does have real storage and compute implications (continuous log generation, retention periods, querying capabilities).\n- Ring-fencing a specific percentage prevents \"death by a thousand cuts\" where every project slowly bleeds resources to compliance without anyone noticing.\n- Tying applications to risk classification is smart — it proportionates the allocation rather than spreading thin across all projects uniformly. A low-risk system's logging needs are far less than a high-risk biometric one (Annex III point 1(a) has specific minimum requirements).\n- It prevents the situation where a project lead, facing a budget shortfall from compliance overhead, quietly cuts corners on logging or defers it — which would be a compliance violation.\n\n**Weaknesses / concerns:**\n- \"A specific percentage of the annual infrastructure budget\" is vague. Without a mechanism to right-size it, you could end up with an underfunded fund (forcing projects back into research budgets anyway) or an overfunded one (wasted resources that could go elsewhere). The proposal doesn't specify how that percentage is determined or adjusted.\n- It assumes the cost is primarily compute and storage. Article 12's requirements also include engineering effort to build logging capabilities, maintain them across system updates, manage data retention policies, and ensure logs are actually structured for traceability (not just dumped into a bucket). A fund that only covers \"compute and storage\" might miss the human/infrastructure-engineering costs.\n- \"Project leads can apply for these credits based on their system's risk classification\" — this introduces an application/approval layer. In my experience, internal grant processes add overhead. If the process is heavy, project leads will avoid applying (underreporting compliance needs) or the administration cost of running the fund offsets its benefit.\n- The proposal frames this as a new funding stream (\"dedicated internal funding stream,\" \"central fund\"). From an infrastructure-efficiency standpoint, I'd first ask: can't we simply reclassify existing budget lines? Adding a new administrative layer (application process, tracking, reporting) has its own overhead. Is the marginal cost of compliance data actually large enough to justify a new governance structure?\n- However, I should also consider: if you DON'T ring-fence it, what happens? Project leads absorb compliance costs into research budgets, which means they either cut research scope or quietly under-invest in logging (compliance risk). The status quo is arguably worse for both scientific ambition AND regulatory compliance.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe core insight is sound and directly addresses my domain of concern. The proportionality mechanism (risk classification) is well-designed. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level — it's more of a principle (\"set aside money for compliance so research isn't throttled\") than an actionable implementation plan. It doesn't specify:\n- How the percentage is determined or recalibrated\n- What scope of costs it covers (just compute/storage? engineering time too?)\n- The administrative mechanism (how heavy is the application process?)\n- What happens if the fund runs out mid-year\n\nThat said, as a *proposal* to screen — I'm not asking for full implementation detail at this stage. The strategic direction is correct and addresses a real risk in my domain. It's a reasonable first step that could be refined. But it's not a fully-formed plan; it's a policy principle dressed up as a program.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. The direction is right, the proportionality logic is good, and it addresses a genuine resource-allocation tension I manage. But the vagueness on implementation details keeps it from being a 90+. Maybe around 62-68? It's promising but needs significant refinement before it becomes actionable. Actually, let me reconsider — as Lab Director, would I back this? The principle is correct and important. If I reject it, what's the alternative? Let compliance costs be absorbed piecemeal by projects? That's worse for both science and compliance. So yes, approve. But the promise score should reflect that it needs work: maybe 65.\n\nLet me settle on approve with a promise of about 64-66. The strategic logic is sound, directly in my wheelhouse, but the execution details are thin and there's a risk of administrative overhead creating its own inefficiency.\n",
