igh-risk AI systems. A pure research project on logging granularity, while intellectually interesting, diverts resources from actual compliance engineering.\n- \"Top-tier AI safety conferences\" and \"establishing our lab as a leader in the intellectual merit of AI governance research\" — this is positioning/branding language. From my resource-allocation perspective, prestige at conferences doesn't pay for infrastructure or reduce implementation costs directly.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does this leverage what we already have? The comparative experiments would require setting up multiple logging configurations across test systems. That's additional engineering work that doesn't directly serve our compliance deadline. We could extract some insights from our own implementation process without a formal \"research project\" with publication goals.\n- An open-source provenance schema is a nice-to-have for the community, but it's not a requirement of the Act, and building one is an additional scope beyond what we need to comply.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The proposal confuses compliance implementation with research exploration. We don't need to \"investigate\" what minimum granularity is — Article 12(3) tells us for Annex III point 1(a) systems, and Articles 12(1)-(2) give the general framework. We need to BUILD the logging, not study whether less might suffice.\n2. The framing around conference publication and intellectual leadership is a scientific ambition goal that doesn't align with resource-constrained compliance work.\n3. \"Minimum necessary logging granularity\" could be read as trying to find ways to log less than what's required, which is risky from a compliance standpoint. Or it could mean finding the right level of detail so we're not over-logging (which has storage/compute costs). The latter interpretation is more useful but still somewhat tangential.\n4. The open-source schema component adds scope and community-ma