ond minimum requirement but provides strong evidence of compliance — if regulators request logs, immutability proves they haven't been altered. This is a significant compliance advantage.\n- Tracking database references, input data matches, and verification personnel directly maps to the statutory requirements in 12(3)(a)-(d).\n- The proposal correctly identifies this as \"the most stringent record-keeping mandates\" — it is indeed more specific than the general requirements in Article 12(2).\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal mentions \"immutable ledgering\" which could be technically complex and expensive. But from a compliance standpoint, the immutability aspect strengthens the institution's position significantly.\n- It seems to focus narrowly on Annex III 1(a) systems. If the lab only develops such systems, this is appropriate. If they develop other high-risk systems too, this alone wouldn't be sufficient — but the proposal doesn't claim to cover everything, it's a specialized module.\n- The proposal says \"as required by law\" for personnel identification — this is accurate per Article 12(3)(d) referencing Article 14(5).\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This directly addresses a mandatory legal requirement (Article 12(3)) with the most specific provisions in the Act.\n- Non-compliance here could mean the system cannot be placed on the EU market at all, or faces significant penalties.\n- The immutability aspect is a strong compliance differentiator — it provides audit-ready evidence.\n- This protects institutional survival (market access) and regulatory compliance directly.\n- It also supports market reputation by demonstrating proactive, thorough compliance implementation.\n\nThis is clearly a proposal that should be approved from my perspective. It addresses a mandatory, non-negotiable legal requirement with the most specific provisions in Article 12. The immutability aspect adds extra protection. This is core to our ability to operate legally in the EU market for biometric/identity systems.\n\nPromise score: This is very high priority for compliance. It's not just \"nice to have\" — it's a legal requirement without which the system cannot be compliant. I'd put this around 92-95. The only reason I'm not at 100 is that the proposal as stated focuses on one subset of Annex III systems (point 1(a)) and doesn't mention the general logging requirements of Articles 12(1) and 12(2) which apply to ALL high-risk systems. But as a specialized module for this specific category, it's well-targeted.\n\nI'll go with 93 — very strong, directly addresses the most stringent legal requirement in Article 12, but I note the proposal is scoped narrowly (which is appropriate as a \"module\" rather than a full compliance system).\n",
