ailored to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification type). But Article 12 applies to ALL high-risk AI systems, including those in Annex I. A truly unified framework needs to be flexible enough to handle the varying logging requirements of different categories of high-risk systems.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification — as a separate logging category beyond just \"system use and input matches.\"\n- It doesn't address retention periods, log integrity (tamper-evidence), accessibility for regulators, or the interplay with technical documentation requirements under Article 18.\n- However, these are implementation details that would presumably be in the \"mandatory data schemas\" and protocol. The proposal is at a framework/protocol level, not an implementation spec level.\n- \"Mandatory\" integration during design phase — from a compliance standpoint, this is exactly what I want. Voluntary adoption creates gaps. Mandating it ensures no system ships without logging capability.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:** A unified framework reduces the risk that one team's non-compliant system becomes an enforcement action against the whole institution. Standardization makes audits more predictable and defensible.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:** Demonstrating a structured, engineering-driven approach to compliance (rather than ad-hoc patching) signals maturity to regulators, customers, and business partners. It shows we take traceability seriously as an architectural principle, not an afterthought.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It addresses the core requirement of Article 12 in a systematic way that reduces compliance risk across the institution. The design-phase mandate is particularly important because it prevents the most common failure mode: building systems without logging and trying to bolt it o