eed of prototyping because you're investing in infrastructure that pays dividends across all projects.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- For a dev engineer, this is attractive: if documentation generation is automated, I don't spend weeks manually writing compliance docs after each prototype iteration. I can iterate faster because the \"compliance paperwork\" keeps pace with code changes automatically.\n- However, there's a risk: over-engineering the metadata schema upfront could slow down initial prototyping. If you need to fill in 50 fields of structured metadata before you can even run your first experiment, that's friction.\n- The proposal says \"during R&D\" which suggests integrating with the dev workflow rather than bolting on after the fact. This is good for speed.\n- The tool itself (the generator) is a relatively bounded engineering problem: ingest structured data → apply templates → output documents. Not a research project, more of an infrastructure/tooling project. Feasible within reasonable timelines.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Comprehensive technical documentation\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting. The automated portion might cover 60-70% but the risk management narrative, the qualitative assessment of limitations, etc., still need human input. So it's more of an \"auto-draft with human review\" than full automation.\n- The proposal frames it as reducing \"administrative toil\" which is valid, but from a prototyping speed perspective, the real value is that you don't have to stop prototyping to write docs. The doc generation can happen in CI/CD or on-demand.\n- Version control integration is smart: if every commit carries metadata annotations, the documentation is always current (addressing Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" requirement).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped tooling project that aligns with how dev engineers already work (structured data → generated artifacts). The main risk is scope cree