        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2945\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core pipeline (Git webhooks + MLflow API → structured compliance documents) is technically straightforward to prototype with existing, well-supported tooling, and the modular architecture allows incremental builds in weeks rather than months; however, the proposal primarily addresses the 'up-to-date technical documentation' dimension of Recital 71 while under-addressing Article 12(1)'s core requirement that the deployed system itself technically allow automatic event logging at runtime, and the 'mapping R&D changes to specific regulatory requirements' step will require non-trivial domain annotation or AI classification whose audit-grade accuracy is uncertain in early iterations.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 70\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2945 is about a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" that:\n- Synchronizes technical specs and training data logs with version control systems\n- Uses automated tooling to generate compliance reports from Git history and MLflow experiment tracking\n- Maps R&D changes to specific requirements in Recital 71\n- Aims to maintain up-to-date records without slowing research pace\n\nLet me assess this from a technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is sound: connecting version control (Git) and experiment tracking (MLflow) to generate documentation artifacts. This is technically feasible — both Git and MLflow have well-documented APIs, webhooks, and export capabilities.\n- Generating \"compliance reports\" from Git history is doable but has significant complexity: you need to parse commit messages, map changes to specific regulatory requirements (which Recital 71 item), handle branching/merging semantics, correlate with experiment runs.\n- The claim that this \"maintains up-to-date records required for regulatory audits without slowing down the research pace\" is somewhat optimistic. The mapping of arbitrary R&D commits to specific regulatory requirements in Recital 71 (general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing/validation processes, risk-management system) is non-trivial and will require significant domain-specific annotation or AI-assisted classification that may not be reliable enough for audit purposes.\n- However, the *prototyping* path here is clear: you can build a working prototype relatively quickly by hooking into Git webhooks + MLflow API to generate a structured document that tracks changes. The MVP is achievable in weeks, not months.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The proposal identifies concrete, available tooling (Git, MLflow) which means you don't need to build infrastructure from scratch. This accelerates prototyping significantly.\n- The \"mapping R&D changes to specific requirements\" step is the hard part and where quality will be uncertain in early prototypes. But as a prototype, even a semi-automated mapping with human-in-the-loop validation would demonstrate viability.\n- The architecture is modular: you can prototype the Git→document pipeline first, then MLflow integration, then the requirement-mapping layer incrementally. This fits well with iterative prototyping.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Transforms static reports into a real-time audit trail\" — this framing overpromises. A real audit trail requires immutability guarantees (tamper-evidence), which Git alone doesn't provide in the regulatory sense. You'd need additional infrastructure (e.g., signed commits, append-only storage, or blockchain-like hashing). This is an implementation detail that adds complexity but doesn't kill the idea.\n- The proposal conflates \"technical documentation\" (Article 12/Annex IV) with \"automatic recording of events/logs\" (the actual Article 12(1)-(3)). Recital 71 covers both, but Article 12 is specifically about *logging capabilities built into the system*. A documentation pipeline that generates reports from Git history addresses the \"up-to-date technical documentation\" part but doesn't address the core requirement of Article 12(1): that the high-risk AI system itself must technically allow for automatic recording of events. The proposal somewhat misses this critical distinction — it's solving the documentation problem, not the logging-in-the-system problem.\n- However, from a prototyping standpoint, even addressing just the documentation side is valuable and buildable.\n\n**Decision:**\nThe core technical approach (Git + MLflow → structured compliance documents) is viable to prototype quickly with existing tooling. The modular architecture allows incremental development. The main risk is that it addresses the \"up-to-date records\" aspect of Recital 71 but under-addresses Article 12(1)'s requirement for the system itself to log events automatically. However, as a component of a broader compliance strategy, this pipeline is technically feasible and prototypable in a reasonable timeframe. I'd approve it as a viable prototype direction, noting that it's one piece of the puzzle rather than the whole solution.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"maintains the up-to-date records required for regulatory audits.\" Article 12(1) requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This is about instrumentation *in* the AI system's runtime, not about generating documentation from R&D tooling. The proposal addresses the documentation side (which is more Annex IV / Recital 71 territory) but the core of Article 12 is runtime logging capability in the deployed system.\n\nFrom my vantage: Is this technically viable to prototype? Yes — building a pipeline that pulls from Git and MLflow and generates structured documents is straightforward with existing tools. The hard part (mapping commits to regulatory requirements) can be prototyped with rules + LLM classification. Speed of prototyping: good, because the tooling exists. Quality: moderate in early stages due to the mapping problem, but improvable.\n\nI'll approve it as a technically viable and reasonably fast-to-prototype component, even though it doesn't solve all of Article 12 by itself. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not a complete solution — maybe around 70.\n",
