ally, which have the most specific logging requirements under Article 12(3). This makes it a focused, achievable project.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The module is specialized and scoped to biometric verification systems. This means it doesn't need to be a general-purpose logging framework - it can be purpose-built for the specific data fields required by Article 12(3).\n- The core requirements are: start/end timestamps, reference database ID, input data matching records, verifier identity. These are all structured data that's relatively straightforward to capture and store.\n- \"Our systems team will implement\" - this suggests it's within existing team capabilities, not requiring new hires or exotic technologies.\n- The immutability requirement adds some complexity (hash chaining, append-only storage) but is a well-solved problem in software engineering.\n- \"Without disrupting general AI R&D\" - this is a good constraint; it's modular and doesn't impose overhead on other projects.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Immutable ledger\" might be over-engineering for the actual requirement. The regulation says \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" - it doesn't mandate blockchain or distributed ledgers. A simple append-only log with integrity verification would satisfy this. However, if they're already planning to build a more robust solution, that's fine - it exceeds requirements but is still technically viable.\n- The proposal doesn't mention the \"period of each use\" (start and end date/time) explicitly in its description, though it mentions \"high-granularity auditing\" which could encompass this.\n- No mention of log retention strategy, storage architecture, or how logs integrate with existing system telemetry - but these are implementation details that don't affect the viability assessment at proposal level.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, technically feasible module. The requirements are clearly defined 