nce and systematic compliance culture.\n- From a market reputation standpoint: consistent, auditable logging infrastructure signals to customers and partners that we take compliance seriously as an institutional capability, not a per-project afterthought.\n- The \"plug-and-play API\" framing is practical — researchers don't want to become logging engineers, and if the integration friction is low, adoption will be high, meaning actual compliance coverage rather than theoretical.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal mentions capturing \"timestamps, input data, and user IDs\" — this is a good baseline but doesn't explicitly mention all Article 12(3) minimum requirements (e.g., reference database for biometric systems, identification of natural persons involved in verification per Art 14(5)). However, the proposal frames it as a starting library that can be extended.\n- \"Over the lifetime\" is critical — the library needs to address retention and archival, not just capture. The proposal doesn't explicitly address this but implies it through \"over the lifetime.\"\n- There's a risk of over-centralization: if the library has a bug or security vulnerability, all projects are affected simultaneously. But this is an engineering concern more than a compliance one — actually, from a compliance perspective, having one well-audited system is better than many unaudited ones.\n\nOverall, this is strongly aligned with my function's concerns. It directly operationalizes Article 12(1), provides the infrastructure for meeting 12(2) and potentially 12(3), supports Recital 71's traceability goals, and strengthens our institutional compliance posture. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with what I need. It's not a perfect solution (needs to be extended to cover all Annex III specific requirements, retention policies, etc.), but the core idea — standardize the logging infrastructure so we don't have 20 different half-baked implementations — is exactly right fro