\" helps with traceability but the proposal is somewhat thin on what specific post-market monitoring events are captured.\n- For (c): Monitoring operation per Article 26(5) relates to the deployer's obligations regarding logging - this would be more about the operational context.\n\n**Article 12(3):** For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), specific minimum requirements:\n(a) recording of period of each use (start/end date and time)\n(b) reference database against which input data was checked\n(c) input data for which search led to a match\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification\n\nThe proposal captures timestamps (partially addressing 3a), input queries (partially addressing 3c), and operator IDs (partially addressing 3d). But it doesn't explicitly mention the reference database (3b) or distinguishing \"match\" results from non-matches. However, this is a general architecture proposal - specific Annex III requirements would need to be configured per system type.\n\n**Compliance Officer Assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1)'s core requirement for automatic logging\n- Standardized approach reduces compliance risk of individual projects missing requirements\n- \"Without requiring individual researchers to manually build logging\" - this is critical from a compliance perspective because relying on individual researchers to remember to implement logging is a systemic risk\n- Immutable database supports audit trail and traceability (Recital 71)\n- Centralized approach means the Compliance function can oversee all logging in one place\n- \"Baseline regulatory compliance\" language is appropriately scoped - it doesn't overpromise\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n- The proposal describes a baseline architecture but Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for certain system types that may need additional configuration\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input queries, and operator IDs\" but doesn't explicitly address output data, model versioning, or the full range of events needed for post-market monitoring\n- However, this is a structural/architectural proposal - it creates the framework. Specific event definitions would be configured per system type, which is appropriate.\n- \"Secure, immutable database\" addresses the audit and traceability requirements well\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is directly aligned with a core legal obligation (Article 12(1))\n- It de-risks the institution by creating a systemic solution rather than project-by-project compliance\n- It supports the \"comprehensive\" documentation requirement of Recital 71\n- The centralized approach means if a regulator audits, we have a single point of truth\n- Market reputation: showing regulators and partners that we've built logging into our infrastructure demonstrates maturity\n\nThis is clearly a proposal that should be approved. It addresses a mandatory legal requirement in a structurally sound way. The promise score should be high - it's not 100 because the proposal as stated is somewhat generic and would need significant specification work (what exactly constitutes an \"event,\" how to handle Annex III specific requirements, retention periods per GDPR interplay, etc.), but from a compliance architecture standpoint, this is foundational and necessary.\n\nI'd rate this around 82-88. It's essential, directly on-point for Article 12(1), creates institutional de-risking through standardization, and the centralized immutable audit trail supports both regulatory inspections and market reputation. The deduction from perfect score is because the proposal as written doesn't fully address the specificity required by Article 12(3) or the full breadth of Article 12(2)(a)-(c), and there's a risk that \"baseline\" becomes the ceiling rather than the floor.\n",
