        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1186 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution, whose domain of concern is **technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"Unified High-Risk Event Logging Framework. A standardized software library providing consistent automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects in the lab. We will develop a centralized API that captures timestamps, system states, and user identities into a secure, immutable database for every execution. This tool will be integrated into our core development environment as a mandatory dependency for any system classified as high-risk. It guarantees technical compliance with Article 12 while reducing the repetitive engineering burden on individual research teams.\"\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point: **technical viability** and **speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\nTechnical Viability:\n- A centralized logging library/API is a well-understood software pattern. This is technically very feasible — it's essentially building a structured logging framework with specific fields (timestamps, system states, user identities) backed by an immutable store.\n- \"Immutable database\" — this is achievable (append-only stores, WORM storage, hash-chained logs, etc.). Not cutting-edge but well-proven technology.\n- The API approach for capturing events is standard engineering practice.\n- Integrating as a \"mandatory dependency in the core development environment\" is a reasonable architectural choice that ensures uniformity.\n- However, there are technical challenges: different AI systems have very different architectures (some might be real-time inference pipelines, some batch processing, some interactive). A single unified API needs to be flexible enough to capture relevant events across heterogeneous systems without becoming bloated or slow.\n- \"System states\" is vague — for a complex ML system, what constitutes the relevant state? This could get complicated depending on the model architecture.\n- The proposal mentions it covers Article 12 requirements (timestamps, user identities) but doesn't explicitly address the more specific requirements in Art. 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (reference databases, input data matches, verification of natural persons). That's a gap but not necessarily a deal-breaker — it could be an extension.\n\nSpeed/Quality of Prototyping:\n- This is where I need to think carefully from my vantage point. As a Development Engineer concerned with prototyping speed and quality:\n  - PRO: A shared library means teams don't each reinvent logging infrastructure. That's a real efficiency gain for prototyping — you get compliance \"for free\" by importing one dependency.\n  - PRO: Consistency across projects means fewer integration bugs, easier code review, predictable behavior.\n  - CON: Making it a \"mandatory dependency\" in the core development environment could slow down early prototyping. If I'm building a quick proof-of-concept and need to wire up this logging framework before my prototype even works, that adds friction to the prototyping cycle.\n  - CON: A centralized API means one team owns it, which creates a bottleneck. If there's a bug or if I need a custom event type for my specific architecture, I'm waiting on another team's sprint cycle.\n  - CON: \"Secure, immutable database\" adds infrastructure overhead that might be overkill during early prototyping stages where you're just validating feasibility.\n  - However, the counter-argument is strong: if the alternative is every team building their own ad-hoc logging (which they inevitably will for compliance), then a shared framework is actually faster in aggregate and produces higher quality code because it's tested once rather than N times.\n\nOverall assessment: This is technically very viable — it's standard software infrastructure. The main concern from my