n initial version.\n\n**Decision from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — treat logging as a testable functional requirement rather than a passive feature — is technically sound and aligns well with how I'd want to approach prototyping compliance features. It's a bounded, implementable engineering task if properly scoped. The \"adversarial\" framing (chaos-engineering-style fault injection on the log pipeline) is a proven technique.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated has some vagueness that could lead to scope issues, and it slightly overreaches by implying it can fully \"convert compliance into a measurable engineering metric\" — that requires regulatory input beyond pure engineering. But the core technical contribution (a test harness for log completeness under failure conditions) is viable and useful.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically feasible, relatively quick to prototype an initial version, and addresses a real gap in how compliance logging is typically implemented. The risk of scope creep exists but is manageable with good engineering scoping. I'd approve it but note the dependency on regulatory domain knowledge for defining \"sufficient\" log content.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, technically sound, and aligned with how I work. The deduction is for the slight overreach in framing (implying pure engineering solves a problem that also needs legal/regulatory input) and the potential scope ambiguity. But it's genuinely useful and implementable.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
