rototyping:**\n- The core challenge isn't really technical novelty — it's integration and ensuring completeness. Making sure ALL relevant data flows through the pipeline without gaps.\n- The \"trigger updates every time a model is retrained or modified\" requires good instrumentation hooks, which we likely already have in our ML infrastructure.\n- Risk: over-engineering. If we try to make this too comprehensive from day one (covering all Annex III logging requirements, etc.), we slow down prototyping. A focused MVP on the documentation pipeline itself is very achievable.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Mandatory version control for ALL datasets and models\" — enforcement across the lab can be a political/cultural challenge more than technical. But technically feasible with CI gates.\n- The scope says \"automatically extracts training hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results\" — this is actually a well-scoped subset of what Recital 71 asks for (which also includes algorithms, data descriptions, risk-management system documentation). So the proposal covers maybe 60-70% of Recital 71's requirements technically. But it's still a solid technical foundation.\n- The \"EU-compliant templates\" part is somewhat vague — we'd need regulatory input on what exactly goes in those templates. But that's not a blocker for prototyping the pipeline architecture.\n\n**My assessment:** This is highly technically viable, well-scoped enough to prototype quickly with existing tooling patterns, and directly addresses a core requirement of Article 12/Recital 71 (the \"kept up to date\" living documentation aspect). The engineering risk is low. The main uncertainty is the regulatory template specifics, but that doesn't block prototyping the pipeline architecture. This clears my bar for technical viability and prototyping speed easily.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly — maybe 82-85. It's technically straightforward (which is good), well-aligned with what we need to build, and a prototype c