rs. It's not a massive system build. The \"at every model checkpoint\" requirement is ambitious but aligns with what the Act demands (recital 71 says documentation should be kept up to date throughout lifetime). However, generating full technical dossiers at every single checkpoint could generate enormous volumes of documentation that may need curation — there's a risk this creates storage/management overhead. But as an automated pipeline, it's more efficient than manual entry.\n\n5. The \"living documentation\" concept is smart for the Act's requirement that documentation be kept current. A static document that goes stale at checkpoint 3 of 20 is non-compliant; a living doc that auto-updates is inherently compliant.\n\n6. Risk: If we build this, we're committing engineering time to build/maintain a pipeline tool. But the alternative (manual documentation) consumes researcher time continuously and scales linearly with number of models/experiments. The automation investment amortizes over many use cases. This is clearly better from an efficiency standpoint.\n\n7. One concern: \"at every model checkpoint\" — in rapid iteration, you might have hundreds of checkpoints per experiment. Do we really need a full dossier at each? The Act requires documentation of the system's characteristics, but not necessarily a separate document per checkpoint. However, having the data available and auto-compiled is different from manually writing 200 documents. The pipeline could aggregate intelligently.\n\nOverall: This is a high-value proposal from my vantage point. It leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow/W&B), reduces ongoing administrative burden (improving resource efficiency), scales with research output rather than requiring proportional additional headcount, and directly addresses a compliance requirement that would otherwise be a continuous tax on R&D throughput. The scope is bounded enough to be achievable within reasonable funding constraints.\n\nI'd approve this. Promise