ithmic contribution, no new theoretical insight, no methodological innovation beyond what already exists in the ML experimentation ecosystem.\n- The proposal explicitly frames its value as \"maintaining high prototyping speed by eliminating manual documentation\" — this is a productivity/efficiency argument, not an intellectual merit one.\n- From a research perspective, this is infrastructure plumbing. It doesn't advance understanding of how AI systems work, don't contribute to the scientific literature, and doesn't enable new classes of experiments that weren't previously possible.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- Minimal direct scientific impact. The output is compliance documentation, not publishable findings.\n- One could argue indirectly: if engineers spend less time on manual documentation and more on actual research, there's a small productivity gain for the lab's research output. But this is a weak argument — it's an indirect, second-order effect.\n- It doesn't advance the state of AI science, doesn't create new benchmarks or datasets for the community, doesn't open new research directions.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\nMy domain is knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact. This proposal is fundamentally an engineering/compliance infrastructure project. It has very little to offer in terms of:\n- Pushing what's known about AI systems\n- Contributing intellectually novel ideas or methods\n- Having broader scientific impact beyond this lab\n\nThe proposal is well-motivated from a compliance/efficiency standpoint (which would be a different function's concern), but from MY vantage point, it doesn't clear the bar. It's not research. It doesn't advance knowledge. It's automation of bookkeeping.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does it have ANY research merit? \n- The structured metadata could theoretically be repurposed for meta-analysis or ablation studies at scale. But that's a stretch from what's propose