ling scientific logic from regulatory logging is smart - it means compliance isn't an afterthought bolted onto research code, which reduces the risk of non-compliant deployments\n- CI/CD integration means logging won't be accidentally omitted in production deployments\n- \"Without requiring manual instrumentation by researchers\" reduces human error in logging, which is a major compliance risk (people forget to add logs)\n- Standardized approach across all projects ensures consistency - different teams won't interpret \"appropriate traceability\" differently\n- This directly supports my ability to demonstrate compliance to regulators and during post-market monitoring\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It says \"standardized software library\" but doesn't specify how it handles the specific requirements in Art 12(3) for biometric systems, or how it ensures logging is \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" (Art 12(2)) which varies by system\n- \"Telemetry and metadata automatically during runtime\" - does this capture the full lifetime? What about training data provenance, model version tracking? Recital 71 mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used\"\n- The proposal focuses on runtime logging but doesn't explicitly address the broader technical documentation requirements from Recital 71 (though that's more Article 11/Annex IV territory)\n- No mention of log retention periods, immutability, or security of logs themselves - which are practically important for compliance\n- \"Minimizes the engineering tax on individual scientists\" is a good operational point but from my compliance view, I need to ensure that minimizing the burden doesn't mean minimal logging\n\nOverall assessment: This is fundamentally the right approach. Article 12(1) uses \"shall\" - it's mandatory. Without automated logging infrastructure, we cannot comply. Building this as a unified framework rather than having each project team figure