        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2749 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is about creating an \"Automated Regulatory Monitoring Research Project\" - an AI system that analyzes logs and detects potential risk situations or substantial modifications automatically using anomaly detection models monitoring high-risk system logs in real-time against safety benchmarks.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime - this is a prerequisite, not what this proposal directly addresses. The proposal assumes logs exist and then builds monitoring on top.\n- Article 12(2)(a) requires logging capabilities to enable identifying situations that may present a risk or substantial modification. This proposal directly targets this by building anomaly detection to identify such situations from logs.\n- Article 12(2)(b) facilitates post-market monitoring under Article 72 - the real-time monitoring aspect supports this.\n- The proposal says \"shifts our approach from passive recording to active monitoring\" - this is important because Article 12 requires logging capabilities that ENABLE these functions, but doesn't mandate automated detection itself. However, having proactive detection strengthens compliance posture.\n\n**From a compliance officer's perspective:**\n\nPositives:\n1. Directly supports the traceability requirement in Recital 71 and Article 12(2)(a) - identifying risk situations and substantial modifications automatically is stronger than merely recording events passively.\n2. Supports post-market monitoring (Article 72) which is a compliance obligation for deployers/providers.\n3. Having an automated system to detect anomalies in logs would demonstrate due diligence to