ere's uncertainty about when/whether it delivers.\n- Market reputation: A proactive monitoring capability is a positive signal to regulators and market partners.\n- Institutional survival: Having automated detection of substantial modifications could prevent us from missing reporting obligations under the Act (e.g., notifying authorities of significant changes).\n\nThe proposal aligns well with the spirit and letter of Article 12(2)(a) - it's about identifying risk situations and substantial modifications. The real-time aspect supports post-market monitoring. The fact that it's a \"research project\" introduces some uncertainty, but the direction is correct.\n\nHowever, I have some reservations:\n- It's positioned as research rather than an implementation plan for compliance. My bar is: does committing resources to this help ensure regulatory compliance? A research project might not deliver in time for compliance deadlines.\n- The \"anomaly detection models\" language suggests ML-based approaches whose own compliance (if they become part of the high-risk system's monitoring) would need to be addressed - meta-compliance concern.\n- But it could also be a standalone tool outside the regulated system, which is cleaner.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal moves in the right direction for compliance - active detection vs. passive recording strengthens our Article 12 posture and supports post-market monitoring. The research framing introduces uncertainty but doesn't negate the value. From my vantage point, I'd approve it because it directly enhances the capabilities required by Article 12(2)(a) and (b), and demonstrates proactive compliance to regulators. But I'm not at 100% because of the \"research\" uncertainty and the need for clear regulatory alignment of the benchmarks used.\n\nI'll give it a promise score around 62-70. It's directionally correct, supports key Article 12 obligations, but has research-phase uncertainty that keeps me from full enthusiasm. I'll approve it because it clears my bar - it enhances compliance posture without creating obvious new risks.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise = 65.\n",
