ently or incompletely, which would be a compliance gap.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - a middleware framework is more likely to persist across project transitions than ad-hoc scripts.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"diverse AI projects\" but Article 12 applies specifically to HIGH-RISK AI systems. Not all projects will be high-risk. This isn't a flaw per se (having infrastructure ready is good), but the compliance obligation is specific.\n- The framing emphasizes \"preserving research velocity and resource efficiency\" - this is an engineering/productivity framing rather than a compliance one. From my perspective, the primary driver should be regulatory obligation, not convenience for researchers. However, if it achieves both, that's fine.\n- A single middleware framework might be rigid. Different high-risk AI systems may have very different logging needs (a biometric system under Annex III 1(a) vs. a credit scoring system). The proposal says \"plug-and-play APIs\" which suggests modularity, but I'd need to verify it can handle the specific minimums in Article 12(3).\n- Who is responsible for ensuring the middleware actually captures what's required? If researchers \"integrate into their models,\" there's still a human-in-the-loop configuration step. The compliance risk shifts from \"no logging\" to \"misconfigured logging.\" This needs governance oversight.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Article 12(3)'s specific minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems, or the traceability requirements in Article 12(2)(a)-(c). It's framed as a general solution.\n\n**Decision assessment:**\nFrom my compliance perspective, this is a strong structural investment. A standardized logging framework:\n- Directly addresses the \"technically allow for automatic recording\" requirement of Art 12(1)\n- Creates auditability and consistency across the institution\n- Reduces the risk of non-compliance from ad-hoc implementations\n- Makes post-market monitoring (Art 72, referenced in 12(2)(b)) more feasible\n- Supports the \"lifetime\" requirement since it's infrastructure, not a one-off script\n\nThe main risk is that it might be over-engineered or under-configured for specific high-risk use cases. But as an institutional investment in compliance infrastructure, this is well-aligned with my mandate.\n\nI would approve this. It's a foundational compliance enabler. The promise score: I'd give it maybe 78-82. It's clearly aligned and important, but the proposal's framing as a research-efficiency tool rather than a compliance obligation creates some concern about whether the right governance will be applied to ensure it actually meets all Article 12 requirements (especially the specific minimums in 12(3)). Also, \"diverse AI projects\" is broader than what's legally required - we need to ensure high-risk systems get the full treatment. But overall, this is a strong proposal from my vantage point.\n\nI'll go with approve and a promise of around 80.\n",
