        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is \"P2074: Unified Compliance Logging Middleware.\" Let me assess it:\n\n**What it proposes:** A standardized software library (middleware) that automates event recording, timestamping, input/output interception, and structured storage for all high-risk AI prototypes. It would be integrated into base project templates so researchers get compliance logging \"for free\" without manually implementing it in each new project.\n\n**Technical viability assessment from my vantage point:**\n\nPros:\n- This is a well-understood software engineering pattern (middleware/interceptor/AOP - aspect-oriented programming). The technical concept of intercepting inputs, outputs, and operational events to log them is straightforward and has many existing precedents (e.g., OpenTelemetry, logging frameworks in production systems).\n- It directly addresses Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events/logs over the lifetime) and supports 12(2)(a)-(c) by providing a consistent mechanism.\n- The \"wrapper\" approach is technically sound — intercepting at the API/service layer is feasible for most ML inference pipelines.\n- Integrating into base project templates is a smart adoption strategy that reduces friction.\n\nCons / Technical concerns:\n- \"Unified\" across \"different AI models\" is where it gets tricky. Different model architectures (transformers, traditional ML, RAG systems, multi-agent systems) have very different internal event structures. A single middleware may not capture the granular events needed for all model types without becoming overly complex or requiring model-specific adapters anyway.\n- The scope in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) requires specific data points: reference database references, input data that led to matches, identification