in practice, you'll likely need model-type-specific adapters or plugins. But that's a design detail, not a fundamental blocker.\n\nThe claim about maintaining prototyping speed is credible IF the middleware is lightweight, has minimal configuration overhead, and allows pass-through for experimental/non-compliance-critical runs during early prototyping phases (with compliance logging kicking in at a later stage). If it forces full compliance logging from day one of every experiment, it could actually slow down exploratory work.\n\nI'd say this is technically viable with moderate design risk. The promise score should reflect that it's a solid, well-motivated engineering project but has the \"unified across all models\" challenge and the need to balance rigor vs. prototyping speed. I'd put this around 72-78. It's genuinely useful and the pattern works, but the execution challenges (model diversity, performance overhead, not becoming a prototyping bottleneck) keep it from being a slam dunk at 90+.\n\nLet me settle on my assessment:\n- Rationale: The interceptor/middleware pattern is technically proven and directly maps to Article 12(1)'s automatic logging requirement; the main technical risk is generalizing across heterogeneous model architectures without per-domain adapters, but this is a design challenge rather than a viability blocker. From a prototyping-speed standpoint, template integration is smart for adoption, though the middleware must remain lightweight enough not to impose latency overhead on inference or rigidity that slows exploratory work.\n- Decision: approve — it's technically feasible, well-scoped, and addresses a real compliance need in a way that (if executed properly) reduces rather than increases prototyping friction.\n- Promise: 74\n",
