ely throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" This is a dual-purpose investment: one pipeline serves both logging and documentation needs.\n5. From a funding perspective, the upfront cost (implementing MLflow integration + template generator) is amortized across every future high-risk system we develop. The marginal cost per additional system approaches zero for the documentation layer.\n\n**Concerns / limitations:**\n1. Scope: The proposal focuses on training parameters, dataset versions, validation metrics — which covers a significant chunk of Recital 71's requirements but may not cover everything (risk-management system documentation, capabilities and limitations narratives, etc.). However, this is a reasonable first implementation; it doesn't claim to be complete.\n2. \"Real time\" might be aspirational or over-engineered for some use cases — but from an efficiency standpoint, real-time generation is strictly better than batch updates because it eliminates the risk of stale documentation and the labor cost of periodic updates.\n3. The proposal is well-scoped: it's not trying to build a whole compliance platform, just the documentation pipeline layer. This is appropriate for resource constraints.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my perspective. It directly addresses a resource-inefficiency problem (manual documentation), leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow, MLOps), has clear ROI that scales with portfolio size, and maps directly to the regulatory requirements in Article 12 and Recital 71. The implementation cost is moderate (it's an integration layer, not a new system from scratch). It frees up researcher time for scientific work. It's well-scoped and doesn't overreach.\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear efficiency play that also satisfies regulatory requirements — a rare alignment where compliance investment simultaneously improves operational efficiency rather than purely being a cost center.\n\nPromise score: I'd rate this quite hi