cle 12(3) logging (recording every use session with start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verification person IDs) on systems that don't require it would be costly in both development time and ongoing computational overhead (storage, processing).\n- This is a prerequisite to efficient resource allocation — you can't allocate well without knowing the classification. It's foundational.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- \"Comprehensive review\" could balloon in scope. Need clear scoping to keep it bounded.\n- Cross-functional audits between legal and scientists can be slow due to scheduling conflicts, especially if lead scientists are deeply embedded in active research.\n- The risk classification isn't static — as systems evolve (substantial modifications), the mapping needs updating. But that's a maintenance concern, not a reason to reject the initial project.\n- There's an assumption that some of our systems won't be high-risk. If ALL our systems turn out to be high-risk, this audit still has value (it confirms the classification and documents the rationale) but the \"prevent over-engineering\" benefit is reduced.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:** This proposal is almost perfectly aligned with my domain concerns. Resource-allocation efficiency is literally what it's designed to achieve. It prevents waste of scarce implementation resources (funding, engineering time, computational infrastructure) by ensuring we only build the logging capabilities each system actually needs. It also serves \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" because you need to know which systems need enhanced logging before you can plan capacity allocation on shared compute clusters or storage.\n\nThe balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is served here: scientists can focus their ambition on the research itself rather than being bogged down with compliance engineering that isn't required for their particular system's risk class.\n\nThis clears 