        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2919 is an \"Automated Provenance Framework for Model Interpretability\" that:\n- Captures internal state activations, weight shifts, and decision paths alongside mandatory Article 12 event markers\n- Develops a standardized telemetry API across all high-risk projects\n- Feeds data into a centralized research lake for retrospective analysis\n- Frames compliance as a \"goldmine of empirical data for studying AI failure modes\"\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability concerns:**\n1. Capturing \"internal state activations, weight shifts, and decision paths\" is extraordinarily complex. For many modern architectures (especially large transformer models), capturing meaningful internal state activations at scale is not a solved engineering problem. It's computationally expensive, storage-intensive, and the interpretability of these signals is itself an open research question. This goes far beyond what Article 12 actually requires.\n\n2. A \"standardized telemetry API across all high-risk projects\" sounds reasonable in principle, but in practice, different AI systems have wildly different architectures. Building a one-size-fits-all telemetry layer that works across diverse model types (CNNs, transformers, hybrid systems, classical ML pipelines) while capturing \"internal state activations\" is an enormous engineering challenge with unclear feasibility.\n\n3. The \"centralized research lake for retrospective analysis\" adds significant infrastructure complexity - data pipeline, storage, access controls, retention policies, etc. This is a major engineering investment.\n\n4. The proposal conflates regulatory compliance (Article 12's logging requirements) with interpretability research. Article 12 requires specific