k. Also, it conflates the logging infrastructure (the actual Article 12 requirement) with a downstream analytics layer.\n\nI'll approve it because the direction is technically sound and prototypically feasible in principle - anomaly detection in logs is well-trodden territory. But my promise score will be moderate because the proposal lacks the technical specificity I'd need to hit the ground running, and the ML-first framing may slow initial prototyping compared to simpler baselines.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. The key question: does this clear my bar? My bar is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\" \n\n- Technical viability: YES, anomaly detection in logs is viable. But the specific approach (train an ML model) introduces dependencies (labeled data, training pipeline, inference serving) that add risk to the timeline.\n- Speed of prototyping: MODERATE. I could prototype a rule-based version quickly. The ML version requires more setup. The proposal doesn't acknowledge this distinction.\n- Quality: Uncertain without more specificity on what \"anomalies\" look like in our specific system context.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate promise score around 55-60. It's a viable direction but the proposal needs more technical grounding before I'd commit full prototyping resources to it as stated. Actually, since there's no quota and I should judge strictly on merits - is this technically viable? Yes. Can I prototype it reasonably well? Yes, with some rework of the approach (start statistical, add ML if needed). I'll approve at maybe 58-62.\n",
