ting for the shared component. But the proposal frames it as a \"plug-in\" module, suggesting minimal disruption to existing workflows.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This directly leverages and builds upon existing frameworks (\"our primary frameworks\"). It's not asking for new infrastructure but a layer on top of what exists.\n- By standardizing, it also makes the lab's overall AI system portfolio more auditable - easier to verify compliance across systems without re-engineering each one.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Who maintains this library over time? As frameworks evolve (new versions, new models), the library needs updates. This is an ongoing cost that should be factored in. But even so, maintaining one shared library is cheaper than maintaining N bespoke ones.\n- \"Primary frameworks\" - what about teams working outside those primary frameworks? The proposal doesn't address this, but it's scoped reasonably by acknowledging it covers the main use cases.\n- The scope is reasonable: timestamps and metadata during training and inference. This maps well to Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events over lifetime) and partially addresses Article 12(3)(a) (recording period of each use).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that multiplies value across many teams, reduces per-team compliance burden, leverages existing frameworks rather than building new ones, and frees research funding for actual science. The ROI story is clear: build once, use N times. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by eliminating redundancy.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high - it's squarely in my wheelhouse of concerns (efficiency, infrastructure reuse, funding constraints). I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because there's inherent risk in the maintenance burden over time and the assumption that \"primary frameworks\" covers enough use cas