 of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is where the proposal really shines from my perspective. It repurposes existing CI/CD infrastructure (GitHub Actions, Jenkins, etc.) that the lab already maintains for model deployment. Rather than building a parallel documentation system, it hooks into the existing pipeline.\n- Version-controlled repositories are standard in R&D labs - we already use Git. Extending this to include regulatory documentation metadata is incremental, not revolutionary.\n- The \"automatic linking\" aspect means scientists don't need to manually update compliance docs - their normal workflow (committing code, pushing dataset changes) triggers the doc updates. This reduces friction and doesn't divert researcher time.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The scope of \"automatically links model architecture changes and dataset updates to the required regulatory documentation\" is actually quite complex in practice. Mapping a specific layer change in a neural network to a paragraph in technical documentation isn't trivially automatable. Some judgment will still be needed.\n- \"Replaces static PDFs with a living record\" - this is good in principle, but regulators may expect certain document formats. We'd need to ensure the output format meets expectations.\n- The CI/CD trigger approach works well for discrete changes (retraining, new dataset) but might struggle with more continuous or ambiguous modifications.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal that leverages existing infrastructure efficiently. It addresses a genuine compliance requirement (Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" mandate) through automation rather than manual labor, which is the most resource-efficient approach available. The use of CI/CD tools means we're not building new infrastructure but extending what we have. The upfront engineering cost is moderate and well-understood. It directly addresses a regulatory risk (audit failure due to stale documentation) that would be far more expensive than the implementation cost.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, has clear ROI through audit-prevention, and doesn't significantly divert research resources once operational. I'd approve it with a high promise score because it hits all three of my concerns: resource efficiency (automation), funding balance (uses existing tools, prevents expensive non-compliance), and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure (CI/CD extension).\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's strong but not perfect - the \"automatic linking\" is somewhat optimistic about how cleanly you can map code changes to regulatory text without human review, and there may be edge cases where the CI/CD trigger model doesn't capture all relevant modifications. But it's clearly worth pursuing from a resource-efficiency standpoint.\n",
