lidated against Article 12 requirements. There's a risk that auto-generated documents miss nuances or are insufficient in depth (e.g., risk-management system documentation, which is more narrative).\n- \"Real-time reports on system capabilities, limitations\" — capabilities and limitations aren't always trivially extractable from hyperparameters and code commits. Some of these require expert judgment. The pipeline might produce technically accurate but substantively thin documentation that doesn't truly satisfy the regulator's expectations for \"clear and comprehensive form.\"\n- Integration with MLflow is practical, but what about systems not tracked in MLflow? What about multi-model pipelines? Coverage gaps could create compliance blind spots.\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71) but Article 12(1) also requires automatic logging capabilities built into the system itself. This pipeline generates documents FROM logs, which is complementary but doesn't address whether the system has proper logging architecture.\n- \"Guaranteeing that documentation remains current\" — this is a strong claim. In practice, there will be gaps (e.g., changes to intended purpose, new risk assessments) that don't trigger a model version tag but still require documentation updates.\n\n**Compliance Assessment:**\nThis proposal directly addresses a core compliance obligation under Article 12 and Recital 71. The lifetime maintenance of technical documentation is explicitly required. An automated pipeline reduces the risk of non-compliance due to human error or neglect. This protects institutional survival (fines for non-compliance can be up to €35M or 7% of global turnover), regulatory compliance (directly addresses Article 12), and market reputation (demonstrates robust compliance infrastructure).\n\nThe main risk is over-reliance on automation producing documentation that looks complete but lacks the qualitative depth a regulator might expect. However, as a tool tha