ated gradients.\" The regulation asks for event recording, not interpretability research.\n- The scope is extremely ambitious: developing new interpretability tools (probing classifiers, integrated gradients applied to production systems) is a multi-year, high-risk research endeavor with uncertain outcomes\n- Resource allocation concern: This would consume significant R&D resources on a project that goes well beyond what the regulation requires. The lab needs to meet Article 12's requirements efficiently — building compliant logging infrastructure — not embark on frontier interpretability research\n- \"Elevates regulatory logging into a scientific exploration\" — this framing reveals it's more of an academic ambition than a compliance necessity\n- The deliverable (published benchmarks) is not what regulators need; they need working logging systems in production\n- Risk: if the research doesn't pan out (which is likely for novel interpretability methods on complex systems), resources are wasted with no regulatory benefit gained\n- The lab already has infrastructure concerns — diverting teams to exploratory interpretability work when they need to build straightforward, compliant logging pipelines is poor resource allocation\n- \"Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — this proposal doesn't leverage existing infrastructure; it requires new capabilities (probing classifiers on latent spaces, integrated gradients tooling)\n\nThe key issue from my vantage point: This is a scientific ambition dressed as regulatory response. Article 12 asks for event logging with specific fields. This proposal wants to build an interpretability research program. That's a fundamentally different resource commitment with much higher risk and uncertainty. The lab has limited resources and needs to be efficient in meeting compliance requirements while preserving capacity for its core R&D mission.\n\nI would reject this. It's well-meaning but misaligned with the actual regulatory requirement, extremely resource-intensive, high-risk (research may not yield useful results), and doesn't efficiently use existing infrastructure. The promise score should reflect that it has some intellectual appeal but is a poor fit for resource-efficient compliance work. Maybe around 25-30? It's not zero because the underlying question of understanding risk triggers is legitimate, but from my specific vantage point of resource efficiency and meeting requirements without overcommitting, this is a clear reject.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise ~22.\n",
