        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1755 from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P1755: \"Formal Methods for Algorithmic Auditability\"**\n\nThe proposal is about developing new mathematical frameworks that translate raw logs into human-comprehensible proofs of system behavior. It involves:\n- A dedicated research project (new, not leveraging existing)\n- New mathematical frameworks (greenfield R&D)\n- Intersection of causal inference and event logging\n- Creating audit trails that explain why specific risks occurred\n- Publishing in top-tier AI safety venues\n- Elevating scientific impact\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:** This is a \"dedicated research project\" to develop \"new mathematical frameworks.\" This is pure exploratory R&D. Article 12 requires logging capabilities - it doesn't require formal proofs or mathematical frameworks that explain *why* risks occurred. The regulation asks for automatic recording of events (logs), traceability appropriate to intended purpose, and specific data points for Annex III 1(a) systems. It does not mandate \"human-comprehensible proofs of system behavior.\" This is a significant scope expansion beyond what the regulation requires.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:** The proposal explicitly frames itself as elevating \"scientific impact\" and contributing \"new knowledge to the field of AI transparency.\" While scientifically interesting, this is not driven by regulatory compliance need. It's an academic aspiration dressed in regulatory language. The lab has scarce implementation resources - committing them to a greenfield mathematical research project when Article 12 only requires functional logging capabilities is a poor allocation.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** This proposal creates a \"dedicated research project\" rather than leveraging existing teams or infrastructure that would be building the actual logging systems required by Article 12. It's additive resource consumption, not efficient reuse.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The regulatory requirement is functional (automatic recording of events, specific data fields), not epistemological (proofs of behavior). This proposal solves a problem that doesn't exist in the regulation.\n2. \"Dedicated research project\" signals new resource commitment - people, compute, time - for exploratory work with uncertain timelines and outcomes.\n3. The output is publications, not compliance artifacts. The lab's obligation under Article 12 is to implement logging, not to publish mathematical proofs.\n4. The causal inference angle is interesting academically but adds enormous complexity and uncertainty to what should be a straightforward engineering implementation task.\n5. No clear path from \"formal methods\" output to the specific Article 12 requirements (start/end dates, reference databases, input data matching, natural person identification).\n\nThis is scientifically ambitious work that has little direct bearing on meeting Article 12's actual requirements. From a resource-allocation perspective, it would divert scarce implementation capacity toward open-ended research with no guaranteed regulatory payoff. I should reject this.\n\nPromise score: It's not worthless - the ideas about causal inference and audit trails are intellectually interesting, and there might be marginal value in better understanding log patterns. But from my specific vantage point of resource efficiency and compliance delivery, it scores very low. I'd say maybe 8-12. The \"explain why\" angle is beyond scope, the formal methods approach is high-risk/high-cost for what the regulation actually needs, and it's framed as a scientific contribution rather than an implementation deliverable.\n\nI'll give it an 8. It has some intellectual merit but fundamentally misaligns with my mandate of efficient resource allocation toward regulatory compliance.\n",
