tandardized API\" — this suggests a thin wrapper approach rather than deep coupling, which is good for preserving scientific agility.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A centralized logging system can leverage existing data storage, monitoring, and IT infrastructure already in the lab rather than each project building their own. This is efficient use of what we have.\n- It also creates a single point of truth for compliance, which reduces audit burden on the lab as an organization — less scattered documentation to maintain across projects.\n- The \"up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement in Recital 71 suggests ongoing maintenance costs, but centralizing that maintenance is cheaper than distributing it.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Who maintains this framework? It becomes a shared service with its own resource needs (staff time for updates when the Act's implementing regulations come out, bug fixes, etc.)\n- If the lab only has 1-2 high-risk projects, the overhead of building and maintaining a centralized platform might not be justified vs. each project handling their own logging minimally.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what happens if a researcher's pipeline architecture is very different from what the \"standardized API\" assumes — flexibility concerns.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective, this is a strong proposal. It addresses the core efficiency problem of N projects each solving the same compliance engineering problem independently. The upfront investment in a shared layer amortizes well across multiple projects and reduces total engineering spend. It also protects researcher time (our most expensive resource) from being diverted to compliance boilerplate. The risk of over-engineering or rigidity is moderate but manageable with good API design.\n\nI think this clears the bar for approval from my vantage point. It's a clear efficiency play that aligns with how R&D labs should operate: shared infrastru