ns, storing every single run immutably could become prohibitively expensive in terms of storage infrastructure and maintenance. This conflicts with funding constraints.\n- The proposal frames it as ensuring \"seamless legal compliance\" — but Article 12 is specifically about high-risk AI systems, not all R&D work. If we build this for the entire research lifecycle indiscriminately, we're over-investing relative to what's legally required. We'd be spending resources on logging non-high-risk experiments too, which is wasteful from a resource-allocation standpoint.\n- \"Black box for AI experimentation\" — this framing suggests it wraps around everything without researchers needing to think about it. In practice, integration into core codebase will require significant engineering effort upfront and ongoing maintenance. The \"seamless\" claim may be optimistic.\n- It doesn't address the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which need very specific logging fields (reference database, input data matches, natural person verification). A generic MLflow integration won't handle those without substantial custom work.\n\nOverall assessment: The direction is right — automated logging via existing tools like MLflow is the sensible approach and avoids building from scratch. However, the scope (\"entire research lifecycle\") and the immutability requirement are over-engineered for what Article 12 actually requires. A more targeted implementation scoped to high-risk systems only would be far more resource-efficient. The proposal as stated would commit significant engineering resources broadly when a narrower, phased approach would achieve compliance with less spend.\n\nDecision: This is partially aligned but over-scoped. From my vantage point, I'd want to approve the core concept (MLflow integration for automated logging) but reject the broad scope and immutability requirements as stated. Since I must judge it strictly 