entific work. If too low, the team is under-resourced.\n2. Creating a new dedicated personnel pool may be over-engineering if the volume of Annex III systems in the lab's portfolio is small. The overhead of maintaining a dedicated team might exceed the savings from sharing.\n3. It assumes the lab has enough \"general R&D budget\" to carve out. In many R&D institutions, budgets are project-specific and there isn't much discretionary general budget to reallocate.\n4. The proposal conflates two things: (a) a funding mechanism (budget carve-out) and (b) an organizational structure (shared service team). These could be decoupled. You might want the shared service approach without needing a new budget line if you can reallocate within existing structures.\n5. It's somewhat narrow — it only addresses Annex III systems, but Article 12 applies to ALL high-risk AI systems including those under Annex I. However, from a resource perspective, focusing on the most complex logging requirements (Annex III) where specialized implementation is needed makes sense as a priority.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: The shared service model IS efficient if utilization is high enough. But creating a new pool may be premature without knowing the actual volume of Annex III projects. A more flexible approach might be to designate 2-3 existing staff as \"logging specialists\" who support multiple teams, rather than building an entirely new team structure.\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding: The intent is good — protecting grant funds for science. But carving out a fixed percentage from general R&D budget could itself reduce the pool available for new projects. It's trading one form of constraint for another.\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: This actually argues AGAINST the proposal slightly. Rather than building new infrastructure (a new team, a new budget line), why not embed logging expertise into existing project teams or create a lightweight cross-functional group? The proposal creates new structure when existing structures might be sufficient with minor reassignment.\n\nOverall assessment: The instinct is correct — compliance costs should be ring-fenced from scientific grant budgets to protect research ambition. The shared service model has merit for economies of scale. However, the specific mechanism (dedicated budget carve-out + new personnel pool) may be heavier than necessary and creates new structural overhead. It's a reasonable direction but over-specified in its implementation approach. I'd give it moderate promise — the principle is sound but the execution as described adds bureaucracy that could itself become a resource drain.\n\nI think this lands around a 55-65 range. The core insight (protect scientific funding from compliance overhead via shared services) is valuable, but the specific mechanism of creating a new budget line and dedicated team pool is somewhat rigid and may not be the most efficient use of existing infrastructure. I'll say approve with a moderate promise score, because the direction is right and it addresses a genuine resource-allocation concern that would otherwise manifest as fragmented, inefficient per-project compliance spending. Actually, let me reconsider — does this \"clear my bar\"? \n\nThe proposal addresses a real problem (compliance costs eating into scientific budgets) with a reasonable structural solution (shared services). The inefficiency risk of the specific mechanism is manageable and could be refined during implementation. I think it clears the bar for approval because the core resource-allocation logic is sound, even if the packaging needs refinement. But my promise score should reflect that it's directionally right but not optimally structured.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 62.\n",
