nce between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is infrastructure that enables compliance rather than directly advancing science. It's necessary (Article 12 requires it), so it can't be skipped. But the question is whether the proposed solution is proportionate to what's actually required.\n- Recital 71 says documentation should be \"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" Article 12(1) requires automatic logging. The proposal addresses both — the living doc addresses Recital 71's update requirement, and the commit synchronization addresses Article 12(1)'s automatic recording.\n- However, is a full \"dynamic repository... in real-time\" necessary? Or would periodic (e.g., per-release) documentation updates with an automated changelog suffice? The regulation says \"up to date\" but doesn't necessarily mandate real-time for all systems. For high-risk systems, the bar is higher, but \"real-time\" might be over-engineering depending on our system categories.\n- From a funding perspective, this is a one-time build cost plus ongoing maintenance. If we're already paying engineers who could be doing other R&D work, there's an opportunity cost.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Most modern R&D labs already have: version control (Git), CI/CD pipelines, project management tools, documentation platforms (Confluence, Notion, MkDocs, etc.), and logging frameworks. The question is whether this proposal leverages these or builds parallel systems.\n- \"Linked directly to our codebase\" — good, suggests integration rather than a separate silo.\n- However, the phrasing \"implement a version-controlled documentation tool\" could mean building new software, which would duplicate existing capabilities. If we already have Git + MkDocs/Sphinx + CI hooks, we might need much less than what's being proposed.\n- The proposal doesn't acknowledge existing infrastructure or propose to build on it explicitly. It sounds lik