on of natural persons\" seems to defeat the purpose. The research would need to carefully delineate which log fields can be privacy-protected and which cannot.\n3. The proposal is somewhat generic in its framing — \"pushing the knowledge frontier of trustworthy AI\" is vague.\n4. It's a theoretical research stream, not an implementation project. From my vantage point, this is appropriate — it's about exploring ideas, not building products.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable research direction with genuine open questions at the intersection of privacy and regulatory compliance. However, as stated, it's somewhat underdeveloped — it identifies an area but doesn't articulate specific open problems or hypotheses that would make it intellectually compelling. The tension between log fidelity (required by regulation) and privacy preservation is real but the proposal glosses over how severe this tension actually is for certain required log fields.\n\nI'd say this clears a moderate bar. It's a legitimate research question with potential impact, but it's not at the cutting edge of intellectual contribution in its current formulation. The specific application to EU AI Act logging requirements does add novelty compared to generic \"privacy-preserving data sharing\" literature. I'll approve it but give a moderate promise score — it's a viable research direction but needs more specificity to be truly frontier-pushing.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise score around 58-62. It's a legitimate and timely research question with real intellectual tension at its core, but the proposal as stated is somewhat generic and doesn't fully grapple with the fundamental incompatibility between some required log fields (exact identification of persons, specific input data) and privacy-noising approaches. The broader scientific impact is moderate — useful for regulatory compliance engineering but not a paradigm shift in AI or privacy science.\n\nI'll go with 60.\n",
