uires, and overstates how automatable the documentation portion actually is in an R&D context.\n\nFor prototyping speed: It's a build-once-use-many tool, but it's not on the critical path for prototyping a new model or system. It's more of a compliance infrastructure project. It might help marginally by reducing post-prototype documentation time, but it doesn't accelerate the actual prototyping cycle.\n\nDecision: I'll reject it. The technical scope is overblown relative to what's actually buildable near-term in an R&D lab, it conflates documentation generation with runtime logging (which are different engineering problems), and it's not on the critical path for prototyping speed. The parts that ARE viable (auto-populating some metadata fields from CI/CD) could be done as small incremental features without needing to commit to a full \"pipeline\" project.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 35-40. There's a valid kernel of an idea, but the framing and scope make it less attractive as a committed implementation resource allocation.\n",
