ious and currently at a very early research stage. We don't have reliable methods for doing this in production LLMs or complex systems. This is genuinely frontier science.\n\n3. However, there's a mismatch: the regulation asks for logging of specific events (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, human verification). The proposal seems to be over-engineering the solution by trying to use mechanistic interpretability to trigger logs, when most of what Article 12 requires is straightforward event recording.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- The research question IS scientifically interesting: can we detect anomalous behavior in latent space? Can we identify decision boundaries in high-dimensional activation spaces? These are legitimate open questions.\n- However, the framing as \"Article 12 compliance\" somewhat undermines the intellectual ambition. The proposal seems to be wrapping a frontier research program in regulatory language to justify its existence within the lab's compliance mandate.\n- The scope is very broad and underspecified: \"latent space monitoring techniques\" could mean many things — activation patching, probing classifiers, causal tracing, etc. Without more specificity, it's hard to assess whether this is a well-designed research program or an unfocused exploration.\n- There's a risk of solutionism here: using the most sophisticated available tool (mechanistic interpretability) for what might be a much simpler engineering problem.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, mechanistic interpretability techniques for anomaly detection could have broad applications beyond AI regulation — in safety research, adversarial robustness, model debugging, etc.\n- The contribution to the broader field would be: new methods for detecting when models operate outside their training distribution or near decision boundaries, which is valuable regardless of regulatory context.\n- However, if the primary motivation is compliance, the results 