a engineering teams and computing infrastructure. This proposal leverages existing capabilities rather than requiring new hardware or facilities.\n\nArguments AGAINST approval (or concerns):\n- Risk that this becomes a research rabbit hole. \"Explore sparse logging and differentially private record-keeping methods\" is broad. A small team could spend 2-3 years publishing papers without delivering the actual compliance capability we need NOW for our current systems. The EU AI Act has timelines — we can't be \"exploring\" when we need working log infrastructure in production.\n- There's a tension: the proposal says it will \"satisfy Article 12,\" but differential privacy applied to logs could actually make them LESS useful for traceability, post-market monitoring, and compliance verification (Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information\"). If we anonymize or sparsify too aggressively, we may fail to meet the substantive requirements of Articles 12(2)(a)-(c) which require identifying specific situations, facilitating monitoring, etc.\n- The \"publishable scientific contribution\" framing is a double-edged sword from my perspective. As Lab Director, I care about whether the work actually solves our problem efficiently. If the primary driver is publication rather than operational deployment, we might get elegant but impractical solutions. However, the proposal does say it's meant to reduce infrastructure storage overhead, so there IS an operational aim.\n- Is this really a \"research initiative\" or should it be part of the engineering/compliance team's work? If our compliance engineers can implement basic compression and structured logging with off-the-shelf tools (columnar storage, tiered storage, log rotation), do we need a dedicated research project? The marginal value of \"differentially private record-keeping\" over standard encryption + access control might be low for the immediate compliance requirement.\n\n**Balancing:**\nThe core idea — making regulatory