re projects.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- The quality of the generated documentation depends on how well researchers use their experiment tracking tools. If they're already logging to MLflow/WandB (which good labs do), the data should be there.\n- Risk: if experiment tracking is inconsistent or incomplete, the auto-generated docs will have gaps — but that's exactly what the CI/CD flagging catches.\n- The \"standardized template\" approach ensures consistency, which is important for regulatory review.\n\n**Limitations/concerns:**\n- This addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation) well, but Article 12(1)-(3) about automatic logging of events at runtime is a different challenge. This proposal doesn't seem to address the runtime logging requirements (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, natural person identification for Annex III point 1(a)). However, it's not trying to — it's scoped to documentation generation from dev-time artifacts.\n- The scope is reasonable: it handles the \"documentation\" side (Recital 71) but not the \"runtime logging\" side (Article 12(1)-(3)). That's fine for a single proposal.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a very practical, technically sound, and well-scoped engineering project. It leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow/WandB/Git), requires no novel research, has clear technical feasibility, and directly serves my function's priority of maintaining prototyping speed while meeting compliance requirements. The CI/CD integration pattern is well-established. This is the kind of \"boring but essential\" engineering that unblocks teams.\n\nI'd rate this highly — it's technically very viable, quick to prototype (weeks, not months), and directly addresses a real friction point in my domain. It's not 100 because there are some assumptions about data quality in existing tracking systems and the scope doesn't cover all of Article 12's runtime logging requirements. But from a technical viability and prototyping spe