nt. The \"before development begins\" aspiration is noble but in practice R&D projects evolve — a project that starts as low-risk could drift into high-risk territory during prototyping.\n\n4. Technical viability: This isn't really a technical solution at all. It's an organizational process. There's no engineering challenge here to assess for viability. The \"standardized rubric\" and \"multidisciplinary committee\" are administrative mechanisms, not technical architectures I can prototype or evaluate.\n\n5. Speed of prototyping: A quarterly gate introduces delay. If my project is mid-prototype and the classification changes, I'm stuck. This doesn't accelerate anything from an engineering standpoint. It might actually slow things down by adding a mandatory checkpoint.\n\n6. Quality: Arguably yes — knowing your requirements upfront helps build quality logging into the system rather than bolting it on later. But this is more of a PM/governance benefit than an engineering one.\n\n7. The proposal says \"prevents wasteful application of expensive logging requirements to low-risk research projects\" — from an engineering perspective, this is actually somewhat valid. If I'm building a low-risk tool and someone mandates full Article 12 logging, that's wasted engineering effort. Knowing the classification helps me scope my technical work appropriately.\n\n**Decision:** This is a governance process proposal, not a technical solution. From my pure \"technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping\" vantage point, it doesn't directly contribute to how I build things. It's more relevant to legal compliance functions or program management. However, the scoping benefit (knowing which logging minimums apply) has some indirect value for engineering planning. But it's not something I would \"back\" with my scarce implementation resources — this is a process/committee that doesn't produce technical artifacts.\n\nI think I should reject this from my vantage point. It's not technically viable as an \"engineering solution\" because it isn't one. It's a governance mechanism. My domain is about whether solutions are technically buildable and how they affect prototyping speed. A quarterly committee with legal experts doesn't fit that frame.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe 15-20. There's marginal indirect benefit for engineering scoping, but it's not in my wheelhouse and the quarterly cadence is actually anti-speed for prototyping.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise ~18.\n",
