ut-output metadata is vague — does it capture the specific fields needed? The proposal seems to address a generic case but doesn't explicitly map to the article-specific minimums.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" (Article 12(1)) — this is a long-term storage and availability concern. A logging library that captures events is one thing; ensuring those logs are retained, immutable, and queryable over years is another engineering challenge entirely. The proposal doesn't address retention architecture.\n- \"Hooks into existing model training and inference wrappers\" — technically feasible, but the implementation complexity of making this work across all existing wrapper versions without breaking them could be non-trivial. Prototyping speed might actually slow during the transition period.\n- The scope is ambitious (\"all R&D pipelines\") which means a large upfront investment before any single project benefits. From a prototyping-speed perspective, this is a significant lead time risk.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — a standardized logging library that auto-captures events at the wrapper level — is technically viable and well-aligned with how we'd want to build it. It's the right architectural pattern: intercept at the abstraction layer rather than requiring each team to implement compliance separately. This directly serves prototyping speed by removing per-project logging boilerplate.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat shallow on the specific technical requirements (especially Annex III 1(a) specifics), and the \"lifetime\" retention aspect isn't addressed. The scope (\"all R&D pipelines\") could slow initial delivery. But fundamentally, the approach is sound and would be something I'd want to build or contribute to.\n\nI think this clears my bar for approval. It's technically viable, it addresses a real compliance need in a way that actually helps prototyping speed (removes manual logging burden), and the architectural pattern is correct. The gaps are implementation details that can be addressed during scoping rather than fundamental flaws in the approach. I'd approve it with moderate-to-high confidence.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's a solid, viable approach that serves my domain well, but the vagueness on specific Article 12(3) requirements and the lifetime-retention architecture keeps it from being a 90+. The scope is large enough to create delivery risk.\n\nLet me settle on 75.\n",
