fective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: It hooks into *existing* training and deployment pipelines rather than requiring new infrastructure. It's a middleware layer, not a ground-up rebuild. This respects what we already have and extends it minimally.\n\n4. **Scalability**: As the EU AI Act applies to high-risk systems, and as our portfolio grows, this framework scales without linearly increasing compliance engineering costs.\n\n5. **The \"immutable database\" aspect** aligns well with the traceability requirements in Recital 71 and Article 12. Centralized storage also simplifies audit readiness — one place to go when a regulator asks for records.\n\nArguments AGAINST or concerns:\n- There's an upfront investment cost (developing the library, the middleware, the database). But this is amortized across all projects.\n- \"Centralized\" could become a bottleneck or single point of failure. However, from a resource-allocation standpoint, one centralized system is still cheaper than N distributed ones.\n- The proposal says \"without requiring custom code for every new model\" — in practice there may be some customization needed for edge cases, but the 80/20 principle applies: covering the common case well is where most of the resource savings are.\n- Risk of over-engineering if we build a framework that's too rigid and can't adapt to novel research methodologies. But this is manageable with good API design.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point: This proposal is highly aligned with my core concerns. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by eliminating duplication, it protects scientific ambition by offloading compliance from research teams, and it leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new builds. The upfront cost is justified by the per-project savings that compound as our portfolio grows. This is one of those \"obviously correct\" investments for a lab with multiple AI projects facing the same regulatory requirements.\n\nI'd 