esearch. This protects scientific throughput.\n\n**Arguments for caution/rejection:**\n1. Scope risk: \"Parsing unified telemetry logs into human-readable reports\" could balloon. What does \"unified telemetry\" mean? Do we have that already, or is this proposal implicitly assuming a data unification project as well? If the underlying infrastructure isn't in place, the real cost is much higher than stated.\n2. The proposal says it focuses on Article 12 specifically (risk triggers and verification steps). This is appropriately scoped — not trying to build a full compliance platform, just addressing one article's logging requirements.\n3. \"Read-only\" is good from an infrastructure perspective — doesn't add write-path complexity or risk to production systems.\n4. The audit frequency question: How often do external audits actually happen? If it's once every 2-3 years, is a dedicated dashboard tool the best use of resources vs. a well-documented SOP for manual extraction? However, internal compliance reviews could be more frequent.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my Lab Director perspective focused on resource efficiency: This proposal is well-scoped and directly addresses a real inefficiency (engineers doing manual log work during audits). It's a read-only frontend layer — low infrastructure risk, bounded scope. The key assumption is that \"unified telemetry logs\" already exist; if they don't, the real project is much larger. But even as stated, it's a reasonable allocation of a small engineering team for a quarter or two to protect ongoing research throughput from compliance disruption.\n\nThe fact that it's read-only and focuses on one specific article (12) makes it proportionate. It doesn't compete with scientific ambition — it protects it by removing friction during audits. The resource commitment is modest relative to the recurring cost of engineer-hours lost during every audit cycle.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's a solid, efficient