d/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is relatively straightforward to prototype. You can stand up a log collection layer (structured logging from the AI system), pipe it into a stream processor, apply simple threshold-based or statistical anomaly detection, and display results on a dashboard. A competent team could have a working prototype in 2-4 weeks.\n- The \"anomaly detection layer\" is the trickiest part to get right quickly, but even a v1 with rule-based alerts (e.g., inference latency spikes, output distribution shifts, error rate changes) would be achievable fast.\n- It's well-scoped enough to be prototyped incrementally.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal conflates \"monitoring\" with \"logging capability.\" Article 12(1) requires the SYSTEM to technically allow automatic recording of events (logs). This is about the system itself generating logs, not about building a dashboard on top of them. A dashboard is downstream consumption, not the logging mechanism itself.\n- However, the proposal does address Article 12(2)'s requirement for traceability and monitoring — it's about what you DO with the logs to ensure the required events are captured and actionable.\n- \"Anomaly detection\" is a somewhat vague technical term here. What kind? Statistical (z-score, EWMA)? ML-based? Rule-based? For prototyping speed, I'd want rule-based first, then iterate. The proposal doesn't specify, which could slow things down if the team over-engineers the v1.\n- The \"centralized dashboard for engineers\" is good — it keeps the scope tight (internal use) rather than trying to build a compliance-reporting tool for regulators immediately.\n\n**Verdict from my angle:**\nThis is a technically viable, reasonably well-scoped proposal that can be prototyped quickly. The core components (log streaming, anomaly detection, dashboard visualization) are all established patterns. The main risk is scope creep in the \"anomaly detection\" layer — if the team tries to build a sophi