        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1220\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A standardized, institution-wide automatic logging framework directly operationalises Article 12(1)'s core mandate and provides a single auditable architecture that eliminates the compliance risk of inconsistent or missing per-project implementations; the immutable ledger supports the traceability expectations in Recital 71, and Annex III-specific hooks address the minimum data-capture floor in Article 12(3). The principal gap is that the proposal emphasises usage-period and input-match telemetry but does not yet articulate how it will capture events relevant to risk-identification under Art. 12(2)(a) (situations presenting a risk or substantial modification) and post-market monitoring under Art. 12(2)(b)-(c), which are equally mandatory.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 82\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1220 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Unified High-Risk Logging Framework\" - A standardized software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects. It ensures consistent data capture for usage periods and input matches. The engineering team will build a modular API that developers integrate into their models to push telemetry to a secure, immutable ledger. This will include specific hooks for Annex III requirements. It guarantees regulatory compliance while minimizing the individual development burden on researchers.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified framework directly addresses this requirement by standardizing the logging capability across all projects.\n- Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems: usage periods, reference databases, input data matches, and identification of natural persons involved in verification. The proposal mentions \"specific hooks for Annex III requirements\" which aligns with this.\n- A standardized library ensures consistency - no project can be overlooked or implement logging incompletely. This is critical for compliance because if one system misses a log requirement, the entire institution faces regulatory exposure.\n- \"Secure, immutable ledger\" supports traceability (Recital 71) and protects against tampering of records, which is important for demonstrating compliance to regulators during audits.\n- Reducing individual development burden means researchers are more likely to actually implement logging correctly rather than cutting corners or forgetting it - this reduces compliance risk.\n- \"Modular API\" that developers integrate suggests the framework can evolve as regulations change (e.g., if Article 12 is amended or guidance clarifies requirements).\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal says \"guarantees regulatory compliance\" - no single technical solution can \"guarantee\" compliance. Compliance also requires organizational processes, human oversight, documentation, and governance. However, this might just be aspirational language in the ideation document.\n- The proposal mentions \"usage periods and input matches\" but doesn't explicitly mention all four elements of Article 12(3): (a) period of each use, (b) reference database, (c) input data for search matches, (d) identification of natural persons involved in verification. It says \"specific hooks for Annex III requirements\" which could cover this, but it's a bit vague on the human verification identification aspect.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging to enable recording of events relevant for: (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, (c) monitoring operation per Article 26(5). The proposal focuses more on data capture but doesn't explicitly address the risk identification and post-market monitoring aspects. However, a \"unified framework\" could be designed to include these.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" - this is good for integrity but needs to be balanced with GDPR/data minimization considerations (though that's another compliance domain). From my vantage point, immutability supports the traceability requirement well.\n- The proposal is about building a library/framework. From a compliance perspective, I need to ensure it actually captures what Article 12 requires and that there's governance around its use. But as an ideation-level proposal, it's directionally correct.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a strong compliance-aligned proposal. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic recording over lifetime. The standardization aspect reduces the risk of inconsistent or missing implementations across projects. The mention of Annex III hooks shows awareness of specific minimum requirements. From my perspective as Compliance Officer:\n\n- Institutional survival: Having a standardized, institution-wide logging framework means we won't have one project that's non-compliant while others are fine. It creates a single point of accountability and audit trail.\n- Regulatory compliance: Directly maps to Article 12 requirements. The immutable ledger supports traceability as emphasized in Recital 71.\n- Market reputation: Being able to demonstrate a systematic, standardized approach to logging across all high-risk systems shows regulatory maturity to clients, partners, and regulators.\n\nThe main gap I see is that the proposal focuses somewhat narrowly on data capture (usage periods, input matches) without explicitly addressing Article 12(2)(a) - identifying risk situations and substantial modifications. But at an ideation level, this could be addressed in design. The \"modular\" nature suggests extensibility.\n\nI would approve this. It's directionally very strong for compliance. My promise score should reflect that it's a high-value proposal from my vantage point but with some areas needing more detail to fully satisfy all of Article 12. I'd say around 78-85. Let me settle on 82 - it's clearly aligned and valuable, but the vagueness on the full scope of Article 12(2) requirements (particularly risk identification and substantial modification logging) keeps it from being a perfect score.\n",
