eport writing) away from actual research.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- MLflow and Weights & Biases are tools that many labs already have in their stack or can adopt at moderate cost. The \"custom generator\" is the novel part, but it's essentially a mapping layer over metadata that already exists (hyperparameters, dataset versions, training logs). This means you're not building new data collection infrastructure — you're reorganizing and formatting data you already capture.\n- The fact that it produces records \"during the training phase\" means it leverages existing compute resources without adding significant overhead.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The \"custom generator that maps metadata to specific legal requirements\" is where complexity could creep in. Legal requirement mapping requires ongoing maintenance as regulations evolve or interpretations shift. This is a non-trivial engineering and legal-intersection effort.\n- However, for the core documentation (general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes), the metadata largely exists in modern ML experiment tracking systems. The mapping layer, while requiring initial development, is relatively straightforward compared to building the entire pipeline from scratch.\n- There's a question of whether \"automated\" documentation truly satisfies the legal requirement for \"comprehensible information... drawn in a clear and comprehensive form.\" Pure auto-generated output might need human review/curation to meet that standard. But even with that caveat, it dramatically reduces manual effort.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal. It addresses a mandatory compliance requirement (you have to do SOMETHING for documentation), it does so by leveraging existing infrastructure and workflows rather than creating parallel processes, it frees researcher time back for actual scientific work, and the core technical a