l. It's technically viable (easy to build) but doesn't address the core technical challenge of Article 12 (automatic runtime logging). The \"mandatory\" framing and emphasis on legal liability rather than system architecture suggests it would add process overhead without delivering the actual technical capability needed. From a prototyping speed perspective, it could slow things down if treated as a gate.\n\nI'll lean toward rejecting it because:\n1. It misidentifies the core technical requirement (automatic logging vs. documentation repos)\n2. The \"mandatory administrative workflow\" framing threatens prototyping velocity\n3. What it proposes is relatively basic MLOps hygiene, not a novel or impactful solution\n4. It doesn't provide a path to actually building the runtime telemetry infrastructure Article 12 demands\n\nPromise score: Maybe 25-30. It's not useless (docs-as-code is good practice), but it's misdirected for this regulatory requirement and could impede prototyping speed if treated as mandatory overhead.\n",
