cation of natural persons). There's tension here.\n  - \"Ledger\" suggests blockchain or append-only storage. For high-frequency telemetry from AI systems, this could be a performance bottleneck and add complexity to prototyping cycles. During rapid iteration/prototyping, you want flexible, queryable logs, not necessarily immutable ones.\n  - The immutability requirement isn't explicitly in Article 12 — it says \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime.\" It doesn't mandate immutability specifically. This adds engineering complexity that may be unnecessary.\n\n- \"Guarantees regulatory compliance\" — no software library can *guarantee* compliance. Compliance depends on correct usage, appropriate scope of what's being logged, and organizational processes. This is an overclaim. From a technical standpoint, the library captures data, but whether it meets all Article 12 requirements depends on implementation context.\n\n- \"Minimizing individual development burden\" — this is the right goal for prototyping speed. If researchers don't have to implement logging themselves, they can focus on model work. However, the integration still requires developers to understand where to place hooks (at inference time, at training time, at verification steps). The modular API helps but doesn't eliminate all burden.\n\n- Technical feasibility: Building a unified framework that works across \"all high-risk AI projects\" is ambitious. Different architectures (transformer-based LLMs, classical ML pipelines, computer vision systems) have very different event structures. A truly unified framework needs to be abstract enough to handle heterogeneity but specific enough for Annex III requirements. This is doable but non-trivial — it's a significant engineering investment before any single project benefits.\n\n- Speed of prototyping: The initial build is slow (you need to design the API, implement the ledger infrastructure, write the hooks). But once built, every subsequent high-