or every new project\" — this is the key claim. In practice, you'll still need per-project configuration of WHAT to log and WHERE in the pipeline. But a shared library reduces boilerplate significantly.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where it shines from my perspective. If I'm spinning up a new high-risk AI prototype, having a pre-built logging layer means I don't have to spend days figuring out compliance logging before I can iterate on the actual model. That's a real speed win.\n- \"Protects prototyping speed by removing the burden of manual record-keeping\" — yes, this is accurate. Without it, every new project has to solve the same logging problem from scratch, which is pure overhead for R&D velocity.\n- However, there's a risk: if the middleware is too rigid or if integrating it slows down prototyping (e.g., if you have to configure many parameters before running), it could actually HURT speed in early exploration phases. But as described (decorators/wrappers that are opt-in and auto-capture basic events), this risk is manageable.\n- The centralized repository adds a dependency — if the logging infrastructure goes down, does prototyping halt? Good design would make logging async/non-blocking, but that's an implementation detail not specified here. Still, it's a standard concern.\n\n**Alignment with Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1): \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" — directly addressed by this middleware.\n- Article 12(2)(a)-(c): The middleware captures start/end times and input references, which partially addresses these. For full compliance on Annex III 1(a), you'd need more specific fields (reference database, matched input data, person identification). But as a *base layer*, it provides the infrastructure to extend.\n- Recital 71: \"high-risk AI systems should technically allow for the automatic recording of events, by means of logs\" — directly aligned.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a strong, technically sound proposal that directly addresses Article 12(1) and supports (2). From my vantage point, it's a high-value investment because:\n1. It's technically well-understood (decorators, middleware, log streaming are all standard patterns)\n2. It has clear ROI for prototyping speed — every new project benefits immediately\n3. It reduces the compliance burden per-project from \"figure out logging\" to \"configure which events to capture\"\n4. The scope is appropriately bounded — it's a library/middleware, not a full platform\n\nConcerns (minor):\n- \"Without manual coding for every new project\" is slightly overblown — there will always be some per-project configuration\n- The proposal doesn't address retention policies, access controls in detail, or how to handle the specific Annex III 1(a) fields beyond basic timestamps\n- But these are implementation details that can be addressed during development\n\nOverall: This is a clear approve from my perspective. It's technically viable, directly reduces prototyping overhead, and aligns well with the regulatory requirement for automatic logging. The promise score should reflect high confidence — I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 100 because the proposal is somewhat high-level and doesn't address edge cases (complex ML pipelines where \"intercepting system calls\" isn't straightforward, or the specific Annex III fields), but the core idea is sound and high-value for my function.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
