ng infrastructure.\n- Does it go beyond Article 12's requirements? The proposal mentions \"every major milestone\" which could be broader than strictly required. However, Article 12 requires logs \"over the lifetime of the system,\" and Recital 71 emphasizes documentation throughout development (training, testing, validation processes). So requiring verification at milestones is well within scope.\n- The \"mandatory Article 12 Audit before any high-risk system moves from training to validation\" - this is a reasonable internal control. It's not over-engineering; it's ensuring the technical capability exists before you're in a position where you can't easily add it.\n- From market reputation: demonstrating that compliance is built into the development process (not bolted on) signals maturity to regulators and market partners.\n- Institutional survival: catching logging gaps early prevents situations where a deployed system can't produce required logs for post-market monitoring or incident investigation, which could lead to enforcement action.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Every major milestone\" is somewhat vague - but as an operational guideline, some flexibility in defining milestones is appropriate and the compliance function would define what those are.\n- The proposal is essentially a process/governance change rather than a technical build. It's relatively low-cost to implement (checklist updates, gate creation) compared to the risk it mitigates.\n- It doesn't specify who performs the audit or what the pass/fail criteria are in detail, but as a high-level protocol proposal, that's expected - implementation details would follow.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a strong, well-scoped compliance integration measure. It directly serves Article 12 by creating structural enforcement of logging requirements at development gates. It's proportionate to the regulatory requirement. It protects institutional survival by preventing non-compliance from reaching production