entification (Art 12(2)(a)) specifically\n- \"Without adding significant manual overhead\" - from a compliance standpoint, I need assurance that the automated capture is actually sufficient and complete. Automation without proper scope definition can create false confidence.\n- The proposal focuses on model lifecycle but Article 12 also requires logging over the \"lifetime of the system\" which includes operational/deployment phase - though it does mention inference pipelines\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-aligned proposal with the core requirements of Article 12. It addresses the fundamental obligation (automatic recording) and does so in a scalable, standardized way that reduces compliance risk across all models. The \"compliant by design\" framing is exactly what I want. While it could be more specific about Annex III(1)(a) requirements, as a foundational framework it provides the architecture upon which those specifics can be layered. From an institutional survival perspective, having a standardized logging layer that automates compliance reduces the risk of gaps that could trigger regulatory enforcement. For market reputation, being able to demonstrate \"compliant by design\" is a strong signal.\n\nI would approve this. It's fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires and represents the right architectural approach. My promise score: This is a high-value proposal for compliance. The main reservation is that it's somewhat generic and I'd want to see specific mapping to each sub-requirement, but as a foundational commitment of resources, it's the right direction. I'd say around 82-85. It's strong but not perfect - it needs more specificity on Annex III requirements and explicit post-market monitoring facilitation.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
