e immutable database infrastructure). This is a large resource commitment from an R&D budget that would otherwise fund experiments, model development, data acquisition, etc.\n- \"Mandatory middleware\" could create a bottleneck — if the framework has issues, all projects are affected. Single point of failure risk.\n- The proposal says \"the engineering team will develop modular plugins\" — this implies dedicated engineering resources pulled from other work. In an R&D lab, engineering bandwidth is scarce and often shared across projects.\n- Risk of over-engineering: the framework might be built to a level of generality that doesn't match the actual diversity of our systems. We might spend months building flexibility we don't need.\n- \"Secure, immutable database\" has infrastructure costs (storage, security certifications, backup) that are ongoing and non-trivial.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic in its architecture — it doesn't specify how the plugins will integrate with heterogeneous ML pipelines (different frameworks, different data modalities). This could lead to scope creep during implementation.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a classic \"build vs. buy\" and \"centralize vs. distribute\" decision. The centralization argument is strong IF we have multiple high-risk AI projects running concurrently or in pipeline. If we only have 1-2, the upfront cost may not be justified versus per-project logging. But as an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act with a portfolio of systems, the scaling argument likely holds.\n\nHowever, the proposal doesn't mention:\n- What happens if a project's needs are genuinely different from what the framework supports?\n- How the \"modular plugins\" will be maintained over time as ML pipelines evolve (and they evolve rapidly in R&D)\n- The ongoing maintenance cost of the framework itself\n- Whether this duplicates any existing logging/telemetry infrastructure we already have\n\nThe proposal is well-motivated and addresses a real compliance requirement (Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events). The efficiency argument is sound. But it's a significant resource commitment, and the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level without addressing the engineering realities of integrating with heterogeneous R&D pipelines.\n\nFrom my vantage point: I'd lean toward approving this in principle because the resource-efficiency case for centralization is strong when you have multiple projects. It protects scientific time by making compliance \"boring infrastructure\" rather than a per-project research task. But I'm not giving it a 100 because of the upfront cost, the risk of over-engineering, and the lack of specificity about integration with our actual heterogeneous pipeline landscape.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. The resource-allocation argument for centralization is compelling for an institution running multiple high-risk systems. It frees researchers to focus on science. The main concern is execution risk (will the plugins actually integrate smoothly?), but that's a project-management challenge, not a reason to reject the direction.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. Strong directional fit with my mandate, but significant execution risk and upfront cost. Not a 90+ because it commits substantial engineering resources up front and the integration complexity is underestimated in the proposal text.\n\nLet me settle on 75.\n",
