        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2201\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal directly addresses Recital 71's explicit requirement that technical documentation be 'kept up to date…throughout the lifetime of the AI system,' and version-controlled, auto-generated records provide the audit trail a regulator or notified body would expect when verifying ongoing compliance. However, as framed it targets the *documentation* layer (metadata from commits and model weights) rather than the operational *logging capability* that Article 12(1)–(3) mandates in the system itself—automatic event recording for traceability, risk identification, and post-market monitoring—which is a distinct engineering obligation; additionally, reliance on automated metadata extraction introduces an accuracy/integrity risk if the pipeline silently misses updates to risk-management documentation or validation records. It is a necessary and high-value enabler for the record-keeping regime but not a self-contained solution to Article 12.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2201 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically focused on Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is for a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation System\" — a live, version-controlled repository that auto-generates compliance documentation from GitHub commits and model weights, linked to a compliance dashboard with automated metadata extraction. It aims to replace static PDF reports with real-time compliance snapshots.\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12:\n\n**Recital 71 states:** \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" This directly supports the proposal's core premise that documentation must evolve with the system. The recital also says information should include \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system and drawn in a clear and comprehensive form.\"\n\n**Article 12 itself** focuses more on:\n- Para 1: Technical capability for automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime\n- Para 2: Logging capabilities for traceability appropriate to intended purpose\n- Para 3: Specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems\n\nNow, the proposal is about *technical documentation* rather than *logging*. Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" — that's operational logging. The technical documentation requirement comes from Recital 71 and more explicitly from Articles 11 and 18 (which deal with technical documentation). However, the recital is part of the interpretive framework for Article 12 as well, since it specifically references \"keeping records\" in context of traceability.\n\nFrom my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the Recital 71 requirement that documentation be \"kept up to date throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" — this is a genuine compliance gap many organizations face with static documents\n- Version control and audit trail are essential for demonstrating compliance to regulators (traceability)\n- Reducing reliance on static PDFs aligns with the spirit of continuous compliance monitoring\n- A compliance dashboard makes it easier to demonstrate readiness during regulator inspections or notified body audits\n- The \"real-time compliance snapshots\" concept supports post-market monitoring requirements\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal conflates technical documentation (Articles 11, 18) with the specific logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3). Logging is about automatic event recording during operation; documentation is about describing the system. These are related but distinct obligations. A dynamic doc system doesn't automatically satisfy the logging requirement of Art 12(1) that systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\"\n- The proposal focuses on metadata extraction from GitHub commits and model weights — this captures code changes but may miss operational data, risk-management documentation updates, or validation test results. Recital 71 requires documentation of \"risk-management system\" and \"testing and validation processes\" which may not be captured by git commits alone.\n- \"Automated metadata extraction\" is a significant technical assumption. If the automation fails or produces incomplete/inaccurate metadata, the compliance record could be compromised. A regulator examining records would need to see that the documentation accurately reflects the system state.\n- The proposal doesn't address retention periods for logs/documentation, which regulators will expect.\n- It's a tooling/infrastructure investment — from my perspective, it addresses a real compliance gap but the scope as described may be narrower than what Article 12 + Recital 71 actually require.\n\n**From institutional survival and regulatory compliance standpoint:**\nThis is a valuable investment because:\n1. Static documentation that becomes outdated is a genuine compliance risk — if a regulator inspects and finds your technical documentation doesn't match the deployed system, you have a non-compliance issue.\n2. Version control provides an audit trail that regulators increasingly expect.\n3. It reduces the risk of human error in document management.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful: the proposal as framed is primarily about *documentation* infrastructure, not about the *logging capability* that Article 12(1) specifically mandates. The logging requirement is a technical feature of the AI system itself (the system must record its own events). A documentation repository doesn't implement that. But it does support the broader traceability and record-keeping ecosystem that Article 12 sits within.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this clears my bar because:\n- It directly addresses a real compliance obligation (up-to-date technical documentation per Recital 71)\n- It reduces institutional risk of non-compliance found during inspections\n- The audit trail / version control supports traceability requirements\n- It's a reasonable and proportionate investment\n\nBut it doesn't fully satisfy Article 12 on its own — the logging capability (Art 12(1)-(3)) is a separate engineering requirement. Still, as one component of a broader compliance architecture, it's valuable.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score. It addresses a genuine compliance gap but I want to note that it's necessary but not sufficient for full Article 12 compliance. The proposal doesn't address the automatic event logging requirement itself. From my perspective, it's a strong enabler but not a complete solution. I'd give it around 72-78. It's clearly va