        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2897 describes a \"Unified Traceability Framework\" — a standardized lab-wide API and database architecture for automatic event logging across all high-risk AI projects. It proposes modular middleware that captures system events and timestamps automatically so researchers don't write custom logging code per model.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) requires that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified framework directly addresses this requirement at an architectural level.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes traceability, verifying compliance, monitoring operations and post-market monitoring. A standardized approach ensures consistency across all projects, which is critical for demonstrating systematic compliance to regulators.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities that enable recording of events relevant for identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. A unified framework with modular design could be configured to capture these event types consistently.\n- From a regulatory audit perspective, having ONE consistent system across all high-risk AI projects makes it far easier to demonstrate compliance during inspections. Regulators can see that the institution has a systematic approach rather than ad-hoc, researcher-dependent logging.\n- \"Minimal impact on research velocity\" is important — if researchers resist or work around the system, you end up with gaps in your logs, which is a compliance failure.\n- The modular design allows for project-specific configurations while maintaining a consistent backbone, which aligns with Article 12(3) where specific logging requirements differ by category (e.g., biometric identification systems need specific data points).\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"All high-risk AI projects\" — the framework needs to be flexible enough to handle different risk categories and the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems. If it's too rigid, it might not capture the specific data points required (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, natural person identification).\n- \"Researchers do not have to write custom logging code\" — this is good for compliance because it removes dependence on individual researcher diligence. However, some systems may genuinely need custom event definitions beyond what a standard middleware captures. The modular design should accommodate this.\n- Lifetime requirement: Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" The framework needs to ensure logs are preserved for the entire operational life of each AI system, not just during development. This is an infrastructure commitment that must be planned for.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a robust, auditable logging infrastructure signals institutional seriousness to customers and regulators — this supports commercial viability.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- A compliance failure under the EU AI Act carries significant penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Having a unified, demonstrable logging system reduces the risk of being caught with gaps.\n- If a regulator conducts an inspection and finds that each team has its own ad-hoc logging (or worse, no logging), that's a systemic compliance failure. A unified framework demonstrates organizational-level commitment to Article 12.\n\n**Decision:** This is clearly aligned with my function's mandate. It directly implements the core requirement of Article