ity of any solution\n- It consumes scheduling resources (interviews) that could pull engineers away\n- But without it, I'd be guessing at what needs building\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The audit is useful but not something a development engineer would champion as their primary contribution. However, the information it produces IS necessary for me to do good work. Without knowing which systems lack what specific logging capabilities per Article 12(3)(a)-(d), I can't design appropriate technical solutions.\n\nI'll lean toward approve with a moderate promise score. It's not my core domain but it's a reasonable prerequisite that unblocks technical work. Actually, let me reconsider — the instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\" My bar is: does this advance technical viability or speed/quality of prototyping?\n\nThe audit itself doesn't prototype anything or test any technical solution. It's a discovery step. But it's a necessary discovery step without which I can't work effectively. I think it clears the bar at a modest level — it unblocks downstream technical work by providing the input data I need. But it's not exciting from my perspective and it's someone else's (compliance team's) job to execute.\n\nI'll approve with a promise of maybe 50-58. It's useful but not core to my function, and its value is indirect.\n",
