lly calls for an \"AI-driven\" diagnostic model, which means training data requirements, model selection, tuning, validation — all of which slow down prototyping significantly.\n- The scope is vague: \"analyze event logs for signs of substantial modification or risk\" — what are we actually detecting? Without clear feature definitions and labeled examples, building a meaningful anomaly detector is very hard to prototype quickly.\n- The \"market reputation for safety\" framing is not an engineering concern but it signals that leadership expects this to be visible/polished, which adds pressure on quality.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has a legitimate regulatory hook (Article 12(2)(a) explicitly requires identifying situations of risk/substantial modification). However, from my vantage point:\n- The technical scope is too broad and underspecified for a viable prototype commitment. \"Detect substantial modifications via AI\" is not a well-posed engineering problem without much more definition.\n- Building a secondary AI model introduces its own regulatory concerns (is it itself an AI system that needs validation?) and multiplies maintenance burden.\n- A simpler rule-based or statistical approach could address the same regulatory need with far less prototyping risk.\n- The proposal doesn't mention what logging infrastructure already exists or what the log schema looks like — jumping to \"build a secondary diagnostic model\" assumes the logging layer is mature, which may not be the case.\n\nThat said, there IS a viable core here: if we define specific, measurable anomalies (e.g., input distribution shift, output confidence degradation, latency spikes, unexpected error patterns), a prototype anomaly detector on structured logs could be built in a reasonable sprint. The problem is the proposal as written is too ambitious and underspecified to commit resources to.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject with a moderate promise score — the underlying need is real, but the specific engineering commitment described (AI-driven secondary model for detecting \"substantial modification\") is not technically well-posed enough to prototype reliably on a timeline. It's more of a research direction than an implementable feature.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"flag anomalies in the primary system's behavior based on real-time log data\" and \"alert compliance officers when thresholds are exceeded.\" If I read this charitably as \"build a monitoring/alerting pipeline that uses statistical or ML-based anomaly detection on structured logs to flag potential issues,\" there IS a prototypable core. But the framing around \"substantial modification detection via AI\" is where it gets technically shaky, because substantial modification is defined by regulatory criteria (significant change in intended purpose, architecture, data sources, etc.) that aren't purely observable from runtime logs alone.\n\nI'll reject this. The technical viability concern is: you cannot reliably detect \"substantial modifications\" (a legal/regulatory classification) purely from runtime log anomaly patterns without extensive domain knowledge encoding and human-in-the-loop validation. And the speed of prototyping would be poor because the success criteria are fuzzy. A simpler version (rule-based alerting on specific metrics) could work, but that's not what this proposal describes.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 35-40. There's a real regulatory need and a partially viable technical direction, but as written it commits to an underspecified, high-complexity build with unclear success criteria.\n",
