        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1758\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal conflates a compliance obligation with an open-ended R&D programme: Article 12(3) specifies concrete minimum logging fields (timestamps, reference DB, matched inputs, verifier identity), yet this project commits scarce engineering capacity to designing and building a novel 'semantic' architecture that maps logs to theoretical hypotheses — a deliverable with no defined endpoint or acceptance criterion. Furthermore, embedding this in the core experimental framework creates lock-in risk on shared infrastructure for an unproven schema, while the actual regulatory floor (automatic event recording per Art 12(1)–(3)) could be met at a fraction of the cost using existing observability tooling already present in the lab.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is \"Research into Semantic Event-Logging Architectures\" — developing logging systems that capture internal state transitions and decision logic, moving beyond simple event recording toward deeper system observability, designing a metadata schema mapping automated logs to specific theoretical hypotheses being tested during R&D iterations, implementing in the core experimental framework.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This is framed as \"research into\" and \"a project to develop\" — it's an open-ended R&D effort, not a targeted implementation of what Article 12 requires. The regulation says high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" The minimum requirements in paragraph 3 are quite specific: start/end times, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons verifying results.\n- The proposal goes well beyond the regulatory requirement. It's proposing to build a \"semantic\" logging architecture that captures \"internal state transitions and decision logic\" — this is a research project, not compliance work.\n- It requires implementing in the \"core experimental framework,\" which means touching foundational infrastructure that other projects depend on. This has high opportunity cost — diverting engineers from their current workstreams to rebuild core infra for an unproven architectural concept.\n- The metadata schema mapping logs to \"theoretical hypotheses being tested during R&D iterations\" is a novel and ambitious undertaking with unclear deliverables.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal explicitly frames a legal compliance requirement as a vehicle for \"deeper scientific insight into model behavior.\" This is classic scope creep dressed up as dual-use value. While it's true that better observability can aid research, the regulation only requires what it states in paragraphs 1-3. Building a semantic architecture with hypothesis-mapping metadata is not required by Article 12.\n- The \"turns a legal requirement into a tool for deeper scientific insight\" framing is attractive but risky — it suggests we'd be spending compliance budget on research that may or may not yield the promised insights, while the actual compliance deliverable (basic logging) might get neglected or delayed.\n- From a funding perspective, this is an unfunded R&D project being proposed as if it's compliance work. The lab doesn't have dedicated \"compliance-as-research\" budget lines typically.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Implementing in the \"core experimental framework\" means modifying shared infrastructure. This creates lock-in risk — if the semantic architecture design doesn't pan out or proves too costly to maintain, we're stuck with a modified core system.\n- Existing logging solutions likely already capture timestamps and basic events (which is what Article 12(3) requires for Annex III systems). Building a fundamentally new \"semantic\" layer on top may be duplicative of existing observability tools we might already have.\n- The proposal doesn't reference what logging infrastructure currently exists, which makes me question whether the team has done adequate scoping before proposing a rebuild.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is an ambitious research idea that I find intellectually interesting but professionally problematic from my vantage point. It:\n1. Confuses compliance with research — Article 12 sets a floor, and this proposal builds a cathedral when a solid house is what's needed for compliance.\n2. Has unclear, open-ended deliverables (\"research into,\" \"develop logging systems that capture\") — no defined endpoint.\n3. Touches core infrastructure without clear justification over existing tools.\n4. Risks diverting scarce engineering resources from projects with defined outcomes.\n5. The \"dual-use\" framing (compliance + research) is appealing but muddies accountability for both.\n\nHowever, I should note: the idea of richer logging beyond bare timestamps does align with the spirit of Article 12(2)(a)-(c), which asks for events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation. So there's a kernel of legitimate need here that goes slightly beyond the absolute minimum in paragraph 3 (which only applies to Annex III point 1(a) systems).\n\nBut as written, this is a research project with no defined scope, no clear compliance deliverable timeline, and high infrastructure risk. From my resource-allocation standpoint, I'd reject it as proposed. The lab could extract the useful parts (e.g., \"ensure our logging captures state transitions relevant to risk identification per Art 12(2)(a)\") without committing to a full semantic architecture research project that modifies core infrastructure.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal as framed is an open-ended research program, not a targeted compliance or efficiency improvement with defined deliverables and resource bounds. It touches shared infrastructure for an unproven architectural concept while the actual regulatory requirement (basic automatic event logging) could be met much more cheaply with existing tools.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-35. The intellectual idea has merit, and the spirit aligns partially with Article 12(2), but as a resource commitment from my vantage point, it's too open-ended, touches shared infra without adequate justification, and conflates compliance with research in a way that muddies both. I'm not \"would never back it\" (0) because the underlying observability thinking is sound, but I definitely wouldn't approve it as written.\n",
