ence pipelines\" - this is a good sign. It's building on what we already have rather than creating parallel systems. A modular API approach means teams can opt in without major refactoring, which reduces disruption to ongoing research. This is efficient use of existing infrastructure.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Building an internal software library requires dedicated engineering time and ongoing maintenance. Who maintains it? If it falls to the same engineers doing research, it becomes a tax on scientific output.\n- \"All high-risk AI projects\" - if the lab only has a handful, the ROI might not be there yet. But if we anticipate growth (EU AI Act is new, more systems will become classified), this could be forward-looking.\n- The scope described seems reasonable: timestamps, input data capture - these are relatively straightforward to standardize. It's not proposing to solve every logging requirement in Article 12, just the core automatic recording piece.\n- A modular API that integrates with existing pipelines is a sensible architectural choice - it doesn't force teams to rewrite their systems.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a well-targeted proposal that addresses a real resource-allocation inefficiency (duplicate logging infrastructure) while leveraging existing infrastructure (inference pipelines). The scope is appropriately bounded - it's not trying to solve the entire compliance picture, just the automatic event recording layer. From my vantage point, this is high-value: it reduces per-project engineering cost for a mandatory requirement, scales with the number of teams, and doesn't significantly impede scientific ambition if well-scoped. The main risk is maintenance burden, but that's manageable.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my perspective - maybe 82-85. It's clearly aligned with resource efficiency and existing infrastructure use. I'm not giving it 90+ because there's some risk around long-term maintenance ownership and the assumption that multiple teams wi