ework reduces per-project compliance costs but adds a dependency: if the framework has bugs or lags behind regulatory changes, all projects are affected.\n- Funding constraints matter: building and maintaining a centralized logging platform is expensive. The proposal doesn't mention cost, staffing, or maintenance burden over time.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does the lab already have logging infrastructure? The proposal assumes building something new (\"we will develop\"). If there's already telemetry/logging in place for other purposes (e.g., ML experiment tracking like MLflow, W&B, etc.), this might be redundant or at least could leverage existing systems.\n- A \"centralized, secure archive\" implies new storage infrastructure, access controls, retention policies — all additional costs.\n- The modular API approach is sensible from an integration standpoint, reducing friction for researchers.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The upfront cost of building and maintaining this framework is significant and not detailed in the proposal.\n2. It's a \"build once\" solution that assumes the lab will have enough high-risk AI systems to justify the amortization. If the pipeline is thin, this is over-engineering.\n3. Maintenance burden: regulatory requirements evolve (the EU AI Act itself has implementing regulations coming). The framework needs continuous updates. Who maintains it? That's a recurring resource cost.\n4. Centralization creates a single point of failure and a bottleneck for compliance changes.\n5. However, the efficiency argument is real: if you have 10+ high-risk systems, per-project logging development is wasteful duplication.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a reasonable proposal in direction — standardizing compliance infrastructure reduces total engineering hours across projects. But from my resource-allocation perspective, I need to be careful:\n- The upfront investment is substantial and the ROI depends on volume of