ng the same regulatory challenge — but that's a stretch and not what the proposal describes (it says \"internal audits\" and is framed around protecting \"our market reputation\").\n\nThe proposal is well-structured as a compliance measure and would likely be approved by legal, operations, or management functions. But from the researcher's vantage point focused on knowledge advancement, intellectual merit, and scientific impact, this is essentially an administrative overhead process. It consumes resources (monthly audits, template maintenance, version control integration) that could otherwise be directed toward actual research.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does good documentation enable better science? There's a weak argument that rigorous documentation of training data, algorithms, and validation processes could improve reproducibility — which is a value in science. But the proposal frames this as compliance (protecting reputation with regulators), not as advancing scientific practice. The \"monthly internal audits\" are about checking boxes, not improving research methodology.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal has essentially zero connection to the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, or broader scientific impact. It's a pure compliance/administrative instrument. From my vantage point, it doesn't clear any meaningful bar. I would reject it.\n\nPromise score: Very low. Maybe 5-8 at most — there's a whisper of \"documenting algorithms and training data\" which touches on reproducibility values in science, but the proposal as framed is entirely about regulatory compliance and market reputation protection. I'll give it a 5.\n",
