. \"legitimate variation\" is non-trivial but well-studied.\n- The proposal mentions \"data scientists will build\" — from a prototyping speed perspective, this could be either good (they have the ML expertise) or bad (if the problem is more of an engineering/pipeline problem than a modeling problem).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A basic version of this (statistical thresholds on key metrics, rule-based anomaly flags) can be prototyped quickly — days to weeks.\n- A sophisticated ML-based anomaly detection system would take longer but is still very doable within existing tooling ecosystems.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level and could be decomposed into incrementally deliverable pieces, which is good for prototyping.\n- One concern: the proposal frames this as a \"suite\" — that's broad scope. A \"suite\" implies multiple tools, multiple modules. From a speed perspective, starting with a single focused detector would be faster to prototype and validate.\n- The connection to Article 12 is relevant but somewhat loose — the article mandates logging capability (the system must record events), while this proposal is about post-hoc analysis of those logs for risk detection. This is more aligned with Articles 72 (post-market monitoring) and 79 (serious incidents/substantial modification). It's a complementary tool, not directly implementing Article 12's requirements per se, but it leverages the data that Article 12 requires to be captured.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — \"anomaly detection monitors\" without specifying what signals are monitored, what the baseline looks like, what thresholds or models are used. This makes it hard to assess whether a prototype would be fast or slow.\n- \"Specialized toolset\" and \"suite\" suggest scope that could balloon. From a prototyping speed perspective, I'd want a narrow first cut.\n- The technical approach (anomaly detection on logs) is well-understood and has existing 