        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2127 as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2127: \"The Prototyping Sandbox Policy\"**\n- Creates a formal distinction between Exploratory Research and High-Risk Development\n- Proposes a tiered approach to record-keeping\n- Full Article 12 logging only triggered once a project reaches a specific TRL or risk profile\n- Early prototypes remain in low-friction environment\n\n**My compliance assessment:**\n\nThe critical issue here is whether this proposal creates a compliant framework or an exposure to regulatory non-compliance.\n\nArticle 12 states: \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The key phrase is \"over the lifetime of the system.\" This means logging must be present from the beginning of the system's operational life, not retrofitted when it \"matures\" into a high-risk classification.\n\nThe proposal essentially says: \"We'll delay full logging until we decide the project has become 'high-risk enough.'\" But this is problematic for several reasons:\n\n1. **Regulatory risk**: The EU AI Act doesn't grant institutions a grace period or a TRL-based exemption from Article 12. If a system is classified as high-risk, it must log \"over the lifetime of the system.\" A project that starts as a prototype but evolves into a high-risk system needs to have logging capabilities technically present from the point it becomes a high-risk AI system. The proposal's \"tiered approach\" based on internal TRL thresholds doesn't align with how the regulation actually works. The classification isn't based on our internal TRL; it's based on whether the system meets the criteria in Annex III or is intended for use as a safety component etc.\n\n2. **Institutional survival**: If regulators audit and find that we ran \"prototypes\" of what was effectively high-risk systems without proper logging, the institutional exposure is severe. Fines under the AI Act can reach €35 million or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, and up to €15 million or 3% for non-compliance with other requirements. The \"we were still in prototype mode\" defense would likely not hold if the system was being tested on real data or deployed in any capacity matching Annex III criteria.\n\n3. **Market reputation**: If it becomes public that an R&D institution deliberately delayed logging obligations to \"protect intellectual merit,\" this is a PR disaster. It signals to regulators and market partners that we prioritize convenience over compliance.\n\n4. **The \"classification system\" is dangerous**: Who decides when TRL X is reached? This creates internal ambiguity and potential for misclassification. Regulators won't accept our internal TRL thresholds as substitutes for the regulatory definition of a high-risk AI system.\n\n5. **However**, there IS a legitimate compliance concept here: the distinction between research/development activities and market deployment. Article 2(6) of the AI Act does exclude \"scientific research and development\" from scope in certain cases, and recital 37 mentions that AI systems developed specifically for scientific research purposes are excluded. But this is narrow — it applies to pure R&D, not to prototyping of products that will eventually be high-risk systems.\n\n6. **The practical concern**: If we build logging infrastructure late (after the \"trigger point\"), we may find it technically infeasible or prohibitively expensive to retrofit logging into an existing architecture. Article 12(1) says systems shall \"technically allow for\" automatic recording — this is a design requirement, not just an operational one. You can't add logging to a system that wasn't architected with it in mind.\n\n7. **The proposal's framing as \"protecting intellectual merit\"** is a red flag from a compliance perspective. It suggests the motivation is to avoid compliance overhead rather than to ensure genuine research freedom. Regulators and courts will not sympathize with an institution that argues its prototypes were too innovative for proper record-keeping.\n\n**My decision**: REJECT. The proposal, as framed, creates significant regulatory exposure by attempting to internalize a \"graduated\" approach to logging obligations that the regulation does not sanction. While there is a narrow legitimate research exemption under Article 2(6), the proposal's mechanism (TRL-based triggering) doesn't map onto the regulation's actual classification criteria and would likely be viewed as non-compliant if challenged. The risk of institutional liability, fines, and reputational damage far outweighs any perceived benefit of reducing early-stage friction.\n\nA compliance officer could potentially support a *narrowly scoped* version that maps to the actual Article 2(6) R&D exclusion, but this proposal as written is too broad, too self-referential (our TRL thresholds), and framed in a way that suggests deliberate avoidance of obligations rather than legitimate scope limitation.\n\n**Promise score**: I'd give this maybe an 8-12. There's a kernel of a valid compliance strategy buried here (the R&D exclusion does exist), but the proposal as written is dangerous, misaligned with the regulation's actual structure, and framed in a way that would not survive regulatory scrutiny. From my vantage point of institutional survival and compliance, this is more liability than protection.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 8.\n",
