zed repository rather than ephemeral project-level storage.\n\n6. Covers timestamps (start/end), which relates to Article 12(3)(a) for biometric systems specifically, but the general principle applies broadly.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input versions, and execution logs\" but doesn't explicitly mention all elements in Article 12(3) (reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons involved in verification). However, this might be a high-level framework description rather than an exhaustive spec.\n\n2. \"Centralized read-only repository\" - need to ensure this actually persists for the full lifetime and is accessible for regulatory audits. Read-only is good for integrity but must also allow authorized access.\n\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the distinction between different categories of high-risk systems (Annex I vs Annex III) or the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3). But as a framework, it could be designed to accommodate those specifics.\n\n4. \"All internal models\" - this is broad. The regulation applies to high-risk AI systems specifically. However, for compliance purposes, erring on the side of capturing logs for all models (or at least ensuring you can identify which are high-risk) is prudent. This avoids classification disputes later.\n\n**From my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, market reputation):**\n- This is a strong proposal that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1).\n- It shifts compliance from individual behavior to systemic design - this is exactly what regulators look for as evidence of organizational commitment.\n- The centralized approach reduces risk of gaps in records across projects.\n- Read-only storage protects against tampering allegations.\n- It's implementable and clear in scope.\n\nThe main gap I see is that it doesn't explicitly enumerate all the specific data points required under Article 12(3) for Annex II