f experiment tracking (MLflow, Weights & Biases, DVC). The proposal says \"build,\" which suggests creating something new rather than integrating with existing tools. This could be resource-intensive.\n- \"Automatically links runtime event logs to specific model weights, hyperparameters, and training data shards\" — this is technically feasible but the scope is broad. \"Training data shards\" tracking at that granularity can be computationally expensive and storage-heavy.\n- The proposal conflates two things: Article 12 compliance (which requires logging of *use* events in deployed high-risk systems) with research reproducibility (tracking development experiments). These are different phases of the lifecycle. Article 12 is about the *operational* lifetime of a deployed system, while experimental metadata is about the *development* phase. Merging them into one \"pipeline\" could over-engineer the solution or create confusion about what's actually required by the regulation vs. what's good research practice.\n- However, from a resource-efficiency standpoint, if you're building logging infrastructure anyway for compliance, extending it to capture development metadata is marginal cost. That's the key insight.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea — unify compliance logging with research reproducibility tracking so one infrastructure serves both purposes — is genuinely good from a resource-allocation perspective. It avoids duplicative spending and leverages the fact that we're already building logging for Article 12. The framing of turning regulatory overhead into scientific infrastructure value-add is also smart for securing internal buy-in and justifying budget.\n\nHowever, the word \"build\" is concerning. If this means constructing a new backend from scratch rather than extending our existing experiment-tracking and ML pipeline tools, it could be resource-heavy. Also, the scope (\"training data shards\") might overreach what Article 12 actually requires for operational logging vs. development reproducibility.\n\nBut overall, the strategic logic is sound: one system serving dual purposes (compliance + research rigor) is efficient use of scarce resources. It leverages existing R&D infrastructure (version control, experiment tracking) and extends it rather than building parallel systems. The scientific ambition element (enhancing intellectual merit of outputs) justifies the investment beyond pure compliance cost.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a well-conceived proposal from an efficiency standpoint. I'd approve but with moderate-to-good promise because the \"build\" language and scope breadth introduce some resource risk, while the strategic logic is strong.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 72 — Strong strategic fit for my mandate (dual-purpose infrastructure, efficient use of existing tools), but the \"build from scratch\" framing and broad scope around data shards introduce execution risk that keeps it from being a clear 80+.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The key question is: does this clear my bar? My bar is about resource-allocation efficiency, balancing ambition with constraints, and using existing infrastructure effectively. \n\nThe proposal's core insight — don't build compliance logging in isolation; integrate it with the research pipeline so one investment serves both regulatory and scientific needs — is exactly the kind of thinking that conserves resources. It also positions the lab as turning a cost center (compliance) into a value add (better reproducibility, stronger publication claims). That's good for funding narratives too.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep: \"training data shards\" at full granularity could balloon storage and compute costs. And \"build a version-controlled backend\" might mean reinventing what DVC/MLflow already do. But those are implementation details that can be managed in execution. The strategic direction is right.\n\nI'll approve with a promise of around 73-75.\n",
