        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P1702 is a \"Human-in-the-Loop Verification Audit System\" — a digital verification portal that tracks natural persons verifying AI results for Annex III systems under Article 12(3)(d).\n\nLet me assess this from my specific domain concerns:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This proposes building an entirely new \"verification portal\" as a bespoke system. The question is whether we can achieve the same compliance outcome by integrating logging capabilities into our existing R&D infrastructure rather than building a standalone product.\n- A dedicated \"portal\" implies a new software development effort with its own UI, authentication layer, timestamping infrastructure, and data linking mechanisms. This could be achieved more efficiently by extending our existing experiment management systems or LIMS (Laboratory Information Management Systems) to capture sign-off events alongside the logs already being generated.\n- However, Article 12(3)(d) specifically requires \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results\" — this is a concrete requirement that needs to be met. If our existing systems don't capture this, we need something to do it.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Building a full \"verification portal\" is a significant software engineering commitment. The proposal frames it as a product (\"We will build a verification portal\"), which suggests substantial development resources.\n- The requirement itself (Article 12(3)(d)) is relatively narrow: identify who verified, when, and link to logs. This doesn't require an elaborate portal — it could be a lightweight integration into existing workflows.\n- The proposal