iciency standpoint, this is suboptimal. But it does identify the right problem space. I think this is borderline — it's not wrong in what it addresses, but the implementation approach is inefficient.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"We will build a verification portal\" — that's committing scarce engineering resources to a new standalone system when we could extend existing ones. From my vantage point of resource efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, this is not well-conceived. It's solving the right problem with an unnecessarily expensive solution.\n\nBut I should also consider: maybe our existing systems genuinely can't do this. Without knowing more, I'll judge it on its stated merits. The proposal as written commits to building a new portal rather than leveraging existing infrastructure. That's a resource-inefficient approach.\n\nDecision: Reject — the implementation approach is over-engineered and doesn't leverage existing R&D infrastructure. The regulatory requirement can be met by extending current logging pipelines with authentication metadata, not by building a standalone portal.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 35-40. It identifies the right problem (Article 12(3)(d)) but proposes an inefficient solution. From my vantage point, it's not something I'd back without significant re-scoping to integrate into existing systems rather than building a new portal.\n",
