        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P2759 is about establishing an AI Risk Categorization Framework - a mandatory internal vetting process to determine which R&D projects fall under high-risk classification in Annex III, so that Article 12 requirements are only applied where legally mandated. It proposes a quarterly review board with legal counsel and principal investigators using a standardized rubric.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\n**Rationale:**\n\nThis proposal is directly aligned with my core concern about resource-allocation efficiency. The key insight here is that Article 12 applies specifically to \"high-risk AI systems\" - not all AI systems in the lab. If we apply full logging and record-keeping infrastructure to every single research project, we waste significant engineering resources, storage capacity, and developer time on projects that have no legal obligation to comply. This creates a misallocation of scarce implementation resources away from actual scientific work or from projects that DO need compliance investment.\n\nThe quarterly review board with legal counsel and PIs is a reasonable governance mechanism. It's not overly heavy - it's periodic (quarterly), involves the right stakeholders (legal for classification accuracy, PIs who know what their systems actually do), and uses a standardized rubric which reduces inconsistency.\n\nHowever, there are some concerns from my vantage point:\n- A \"mandatory internal vetting process\" adds an administrative layer to project initiation. Every new R&D project would need to go through this before resources are allocated. This could slow down the pace of scientific work and add friction to the research pipeline.\n- Quarterly review cadence - is that sufficient? AI systems evolve, a project might shift from low-risk to high-risk mid-cycle. But quarterly is a reasonable compromise between responsiveness and not overloading the board.\n- The standardized rubric \"based on the AI Act's risk criteria\" is sound but needs careful design to avoid being either too permissive (missing high-risk systems) or too conservative (flagging everything as high-risk, defeating the purpose).\n\nOn balance: This proposal directly serves my mandate of efficient resource allocation. Without a clear categorization gate, we face two failure modes: (1) over-compliance - spending engineering hours building logging infrastructure for projects that don't need it, diverting from scientific work; or (2) under-compliance - missing a high-risk system and facing regulatory penalties. The framework addresses both. It's a governance structure that is proportionate - quarterly, not daily; legal + PIs, not an army of compliance officers.\n\nThe risk is bureaucratic overhead slowing research, but the cost of NOT having this (wasted resources on unnecessary logging OR regulatory non-compliance) far outweighs the modest administrative cost of a quarterly review board. This is exactly the kind of structural efficiency measure that protects funding constraints and keeps infrastructure use aligned with actual legal requirements.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score, maybe 82-85. It's strongly aligned with my domain but I want to note the risk of it becoming a bottleneck if not well-designed. The quarterly cadence is reasonable but could need adjustment as the lab scales.\n",
