ates due diligence.\n\nFrom market reputation: being able to show customers, partners, and regulators that we have systematic validation of our logging capabilities builds trust and differentiates us from competitors who may not have such rigor.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar comfortably. It's a practical, focused, directly relevant compliance control. The limitations noted don't disqualify it — they just mean it shouldn't be the ONLY thing we do for Article 12 compliance. But as a standalone proposal to commit resources to, it's well-scoped and high-value.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 78-82. It's very strong but not perfect because of the lifetime/ongoing monitoring gap and the fact that structural field checking doesn't guarantee semantic adequacy of logged events. But it's clearly worth pursuing.\n",
