        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1124\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core mandate of Article 12(1) — that high-risk systems 'shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)' — by baking logging into shared infrastructure rather than leaving it to individual project teams. The specific fields captured (timestamps, input/database references, user identifiers) map neatly onto the minimum requirements in Article 12(3)(a)–(d), and a standardised middleware layer ensures uniformity across all high-risk projects, which is critical for audit readiness, regulator inspections, and demonstrating consistent compliance over each system's lifetime. From an institutional-survival standpoint, making logging the default architectural behaviour (rather than an opt-in per project) dramatically reduces the risk that any single deployment ships without the mandatory technical capability, and it lowers the political cost of compliance by removing the 'developer friction' excuse for non-conformity.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1124 describes a \"Unified Compliance Telemetry Middleware\" - a standardized internal API and middleware layer that automatically captures required event logs across all high-risk projects using decorators and interceptors for common frameworks to automatically log timestamps, input references, and user identifiers. The goal is to remove compliance friction from prototyping workflows.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) mandates that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A middleware approach that automatically captures logs aligns directly with this requirement. It makes compliance a default rather than an opt-in, which is exactly what the regulation demands - it's not optional, it's structural.\n- Article 12(3)(a) requires recording of start/end date and time of each use - timestamps are explicitly mentioned in the proposal.\n- Article 12(3)(b) requires reference database against which input data has been checked - \"input references\" covers this.\n- Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification - \"user identifiers\" covers this.\n- The \"automatic\" aspect is critical: the regulation says systems SHALL technically allow for AUTOMATIC recording. A middleware that does this by default across all projects ensures no project can accidentally or intentionally skip logging. This is a strong compliance posture.\n- Standardization reduces risk of inconsistent implementation across projects, which would be a nightmare during regulatory audit.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - a centralized middleware makes it easier to maintain logs throughout the system's lifetime rather than having scattered per-project implementations that might break or get abandoned.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal mentions \"common frameworks\" - what if a project uses a framework not covered by the middleware? There could be gaps. However, this is an implementation detail and doesn't invalidate the approach.\n- \"Without requiring per-project manual coding\" - from a compliance standpoint, I'd want to ensure there's still a mechanism for projects with unique needs to add additional logging. But the baseline being automatic is exactly what we want.\n- The proposal frames it as removing friction to maintain development speed. From my perspective, this is actually a positive: if compliance is built into the infrastructure, developers are less likely to bypass or work around logging requirements. Making it easy to comply is better than making compliance an afterthought that gets skipped under deadline pressure.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - the middleware approach suggests centralized management which supports long-term log retention and integrity.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis proposal directly addresses the core mandate of Article 12(1) - automatic recording as a technical capability built into the system architecture. It also covers key elements of Article 12(3). The standardized, middleware approach is superior to per-project solutions for compliance because:\n1. It ensures uniformity (no project accidentally misses a requirement)\n2. It's harder to circumvent than individual project implementations\n3. It scales with the number of high-risk systems\n4. It creates a single audit trail structure that regulators can understand\n5. It supports the \"lifetime\" requirement by centralizing log management\n\nThe only concern I have is whether \"common frameworks\" covers everything, but as an initial investment in compliance infrastructure, this is clearly aligned with our regulatory obligations. The fact that it reduces friction for developers is a bonus - less resistance means better actual compliance in practice.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly responsive to Article 12's requirements, it creates institutional resilience against non-compliance (by making the compliant path the default), and it supports audit readiness. From my vantage point of institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this is a high-value investment. The promise score should be high - maybe 85-90. It's not a perfect solution (edge cases with uncommon frameworks, need to ensure log integrity and retention policies are also addressed), but it's fundamentally the right architectural decision for Article 12 compliance.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 87.\n",
