- It accelerates prototyping because researchers don't have to think about compliance logging during iteration.\n- The \"modular API\" aspect means it can be built incrementally — start with timestamps and input capture (easy), then add more fields later.\n- Risk: building a \"unified\" layer across all high-risk projects could become a bottleneck if the team is small. But for a lab that has multiple high-risk projects, the amortized cost is favorable.\n- One concern: \"across different AI models\" — if we're talking about fundamentally different architectures (e.g., RAG pipelines vs. fine-tuned transformers vs. ensemble systems), a single wrapper might need significant adapters. But at the serving/inference API level, this should be manageable since most modern ML serving stacks expose HTTP/gRPC interfaces.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Consistent log formats are important for downstream compliance review and post-market monitoring (Article 72). This addresses that.\n- The proposal says \"baseline legal compliance\" which is appropriately scoped — it's not trying to solve every edge case of Article 12, just the core automatic logging requirement.\n- It explicitly removes manual burden from researchers, which directly improves prototyping speed.\n\n**Potential weaknesses:**\n- \"System states\" could be ambiguous in scope and potentially expensive to capture for large models. But this is an implementation detail that can be scoped down.\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention policies, storage architecture, or how logs are accessed by regulators — but those are separate concerns from the middleware itself.\n- No mention of performance overhead, which matters for real-time systems. But again, implementable.\n\nOverall: This is a technically sound, well-scoped infrastructure investment that directly serves my domain (speed/quality of prototyping) while addressing the core requirement of Article 12(1). The pattern is proven in software engineering (middleware, AP