irement in Article 12(2).\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- Lossless compression of logs is a well-trodden path (snappy, lz4, zstd, etc.). Prototyping this quickly is feasible and low-risk.\n- \"Selective sampling\" that maintains legal traceability is much harder to prototype in a way that actually works for compliance. You'd need to define what's \"selective\" while proving you haven't lost legally required events. That's not just an engineering problem — it's a regulatory interpretation problem. As a dev engineer, I can't easily prototype this without legal guidance on what the minimum viable log set is per use case.\n- The proposal frames this as a \"research initiative\" — which from my vantage point raises concerns about scope creep and unclear deliverables. \"Explore compressed log formats and selective sampling techniques\" is vague. What's the definition of done? What system are we optimizing for?\n\n**Risk assessment:**\n- If I approve this, I'm committing scarce implementation resources to an R&D project whose core premise (selective sampling while maintaining full legal traceability) may not be technically viable in a way that satisfies the regulator. The compression part is fine and straightforward but doesn't really need a \"research initiative\" — it's standard engineering.\n- The framing as \"transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific opportunity\" feels like it's dressing up what might be a compliance risk (under-logging) as innovation.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe compression angle is valid but trivial from an engineering standpoint — you'd just pick zstd or lz4 and be done, no research needed. The \"selective sampling\" angle is where the real technical risk lives: it may fundamentally conflict with Article 12's requirements for traceability. You can't sample away the events that a regulator later asks to see. This isn't something I can confidently prototype into a working solution without significant regulatory interpretation that isn't