odebase: provides audit trail and traceability\n- Automatic synchronization with commits reduces risk of documentation drift (a major compliance failure mode)\n- Tracks algorithm changes, training data sources, validation results — exactly what Recital 71 lists as necessary information\n- Replacing static PDFs addresses the practical problem that static documents go stale quickly in R&D environments\n- \"Synchronizes commits with compliance logs\" directly supports Article 12(1) automatic recording requirement\n- Preserves institutional knowledge (relevant to market reputation and continuity)\n\nPotential concerns:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on documentation/repository aspects but doesn't explicitly address the *system-level* logging capabilities required by Article 12(1)-(3). The \"compliance logs\" mentioned are somewhat vague — do they capture the specific event types in Art. 12(2)(a), (b), (c)?\n- It's a development-phase tool; does it extend to post-market operation? Recital 71 and Article 12 emphasize \"lifetime of the system,\" which includes deployment and operation, not just R&D.\n- The proposal says it \"satisfies Recital 71\" — but Recitals are interpretive guidance, not binding obligations themselves. The binding obligation is in Articles 11 (technical documentation) and 12 (record-keeping). The proposal seems to conflate these somewhat.\n- No mention of the specific Annex III logging minimums in Art. 12(3).\n\nHowever, from a compliance officer's perspective:\n- This is clearly aligned with the direction of the regulation\n- It addresses a real and critical compliance gap (documentation currency)\n- The version-control approach provides auditability\n- It's a practical, implementable solution rather than an aspirational one\n- The institutional survival angle: having outdated documentation during a regulatory inspection could be devastating; this directly mitigates that risk\n- Market reputation: demonstrating proactive, systematic compliance infrastructure is a reputational asset\n\nThe proposal doesn't fully cover all of Article 12's logging requirements (especially the system-level event recording in operation), but it does address a significant and necessary component. It's not a complete solution to Article 12 compliance on its own, but it's a strong, well-aligned building block.\n\nMy bar: Does this clear my threshold for committing scarce implementation resources from a compliance standpoint? Yes — it directly addresses a core obligation (documentation currency per Recital 71 and Article 11), reduces regulatory risk substantially, is practically implementable, and supports the broader compliance architecture. It's not the whole solution but it's a necessary and high-value component.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: I'd put it around 82-85. It's strongly aligned but has gaps (doesn't fully address Art. 12(2)-(3) operational logging, somewhat conflates recitals with binding articles). But the core value is real and high from a compliance perspective.\n\nLet me settle on 84 — strong alignment, practical, addresses critical documentation currency requirement, but incomplete coverage of all Article 12 obligations means it's not a 90+.\n",
