ect. This protocol gives visibility into whether the logging actually works as intended.\n\n**Arguments against / concerns:**\n1. \"Mandatory\" and \"immediate redesign\" language could be resource-intensive if gaps are found frequently early on — could pull engineers away from research.\n2. The compliance team conducting these drills requires their time; if they're already stretched thin, this adds overhead.\n3. However, the proposal is essentially a *process* (a drill protocol), not a new system build. It's low capital cost.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: This is a process-level intervention that costs primarily compliance team time and some engineer time for retrieval exercises. It doesn't require new tooling or infrastructure. The \"immediate redesign\" clause could be costly, but it prevents far larger costs later. Net resource-positive compared to the alternative (being caught by regulators).\n- Scientific ambition vs. funding: Avoiding fines protects the funding envelope available for actual R&D. A single fine under the EU AI Act can be up to €35M or 7% of global turnover. Even a \"small\" compliance failure found by regulators could trigger costly remediation under audit conditions that disrupt research timelines.\n- Effective use of existing infrastructure: The drill uses logs that must already exist per Article 12(1). It doesn't ask for new systems; it asks for verification that the mandated systems actually function as intended. This is essentially a quality-assurance step on infrastructure you're legally required to build anyway.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a low-cost, high-leverage process proposal. It uses existing staff (compliance team) and existing infrastructure (mandated logs) in a structured way to de-risk the lab's entire compliance posture. The \"immediate redesign\" language is slightly aggressive but proportionate given the stakes of non-compliance. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is one of the cheapest ways to ensure that the much more expensive logging systems you're already building actually work when it matters.\n\nI'll approve with a fairly high promise score — maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect proposal (the \"immediate redesign\" could be operationally disruptive if gaps are systemic, and it adds recurring time demands on both compliance and engineering), but from my resource-efficiency vantage point, it's a strong use of existing assets to prevent catastrophic resource drain.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
