onsume a significant fraction of engineering time.\n- However, if you don't do this centrally, every research team will end up spending time on ad-hoc logging solutions that may not comply anyway - so the total cost might actually be HIGHER without this centralized approach.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is where I'm more cautious. \"Integrated into all AI pipelines\" implies significant modification to existing research codebases and workflows. Research teams often use varied, evolving toolchains. Forcing a single middleware layer could conflict with experimental flexibility.\n- However, the proposal is specifically about high-risk models, not all models. So the scope is limited to those that need Article 12 compliance.\n- The concern about \"inconsistent discipline of individual researchers\" is valid - if logging depends on researcher behavior, it's unreliable. Automation removes that human variable.\n\n**Specific assessment:**\n- The proposal addresses a real and mandatory requirement (Article 12) in a way that scales. Without centralized automation, compliance becomes fragmented and error-prone.\n- From a resource efficiency standpoint, building one robust logging middleware is more efficient than N teams each building their own solution.\n- The risk: over-engineering. \"A standardized software library\" integrated into \"all AI pipelines\" could become a bottleneck if the research toolchains are heterogeneous. The proposal doesn't address integration complexity or timeline.\n- But fundamentally, this is not an optional nice-to-have - it's a legal requirement for high-risk systems. The question isn't whether to do logging, but HOW to do it efficiently. A centralized approach is the most resource-efficient path versus per-project implementations.\n\n**My decision:** This clears my bar from a resource-allocation efficiency perspective. It's a mandatory compliance requirement being addressed in the most scalable way possible. Buildin