onsistent logging across systems), reduced risk of missing an event category in one project while another handles it fine, clearer accountability for who built the logging layer, and easier updates when regulations or guidance change.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating to customers and regulators that we have a systematic, organization-wide compliance infrastructure is far more credible than patchwork per-project implementations.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal says \"automatically captures and stores event logs\" but Article 12(2) requires specific categories of events (risk situations, post-market monitoring, operational monitoring). A generic logging library might not capture all the domain-specific events needed unless it's designed with those use cases in mind. However, the proposal says \"consistent timestamps, input tracking, and database references\" which covers some but may not fully address risk identification events or substantial modification tracking.\n- The proposal is a framework/library — it provides infrastructure but doesn't necessarily ensure that each system actually logs the RIGHT events for its specific intended purpose (Art 12(2) says \"appropriate to the intended purpose\"). Each project still needs to configure what events matter.\n- It's an internal tool, so it doesn't address external compliance obligations like providing access to logs to notified bodies or regulators (though that might be a separate operational requirement).\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it describes the architecture but not the specific event taxonomy required by Art 12(2)(a)-(c) and Art 12(3)(a)-(d).\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. A unified logging framework is exactly the kind of institutional infrastructure that a compliance officer would want to mandate rather than having each project build its own ad-hoc solution. It reduces regulatory risk (fragme