        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1252\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal conflates two very different problems under one research banner: deterministic event-logging (Article 12(3) start/end times, reference DB pointers, input-data capture, verifier IDs), which is straightforward software engineering we can prototype in weeks with conventional tooling, and LLM-based generation of 'comprehensible technical documentation' from raw system behavior in real time, which is an open research problem where current models cannot reliably produce auditable, regulation-grade descriptions (hallucinated or misattributed capabilities/limitations would create a false-compliance risk far worse than missing documentation). As framed—a frontier R&D project with no near-term deliverable and heavy dependency on other teams' prototypes being available for testing—it does not clear my bar for technical viability on any realistic prototyping timeline, and it does not accelerate the speed or quality at which we ship working high-risk system prototypes.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1252) from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: **technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P1252**: \"Research on Autonomous Compliance Telemetry\" — developing AI systems (specifically LLMs) that automatically generate comprehensible technical documentation and logs from raw system behavior. It treats compliance as an ML problem, applies LLMs to analyze system evolution and synthesize general characteristics required by Recital 71 in real-time. They'd test on their own high-risk prototypes.\n\n**My vantage point: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.**\n\nLet me think about this critically:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- The proposal proposes using LLMs to \"automatically generate comprehensible technical documentation\" from raw system behavior. This is a fundamentally open research problem. LLMs are generative models — they can produce plausible-sounding text, but \"comprehensible technical documentation\" that satisfies regulatory requirements (clear, comprehensive, accurate) is an extremely high bar. The Act requires documentation that enables traceability, compliance verification, and post-market monitoring. An LLM-generated document that hallucinates or mischaracterizes system behavior would be worse than no document — it could create false confidence in compliance.\n- \"Treats compliance as a machine learning problem\" is a significant conceptual leap. Compliance documentation isn't just a pattern-matching task on raw logs. It requires domain understanding, causal reasoning about why events matter for risk, and legal/regulatory framing. Current LLMs are not reliable at this level of structured, auditable technical writing.\n- \"Real-time\" synthesis adds another layer of difficulty — you need the system to continuously understand its own evolution and produce up-to-date documentation (as Recital 71 requires: \"should be kept up to date... throughout the lifetime\"). This is an unsolved problem in AI self-monitoring.\n- The proposal frames this as a \"research\" project that \"pushes the knowledge frontier.\" From my vantage point, this means it's not a near-term viable solution for our compliance obligations. It's exploratory R&D with uncertain timelines and outcomes.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n- This is explicitly a research project (\"A new R&D project\"). It won't produce a deployable prototype quickly. The proposal doesn't have a clear path to a working prototype in the near term — it's about \"pushing the knowledge frontier.\"\n- Testing on \"our own high-risk prototypes\" suggests it depends on other projects' outputs first, creating dependencies.\n- The scope is enormous: you'd need to handle arbitrary system architectures, produce documentation that meets specific regulatory criteria (general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system), all in \"real-time.\" That's not a prototype you can iterate on quickly — it's a multi-year research effort.\n- However, there IS a kernel of something useful here: if even partially successful, automated log generation and documentation scaffolding could reduce the manual overhead we spend on compliance paperwork during prototyping cycles. The logging requirement (Article 12(1)) is about automatic recording of events — that part is more tractable engineering than the LLM-generated \"comprehensible technical documentation\" part.\n\n**What's actually viable vs. what's speculative:**\n- Automatic log generation (recording start/end times, input data, reference databases, etc.) — this is standard software engineering, not really an ML problem. Article 12(3) requirements are fairly specific and deterministic. We could build this with conventional engineering approaches.\n- LLM-generated \"comprehensible technical documentation\" from raw system behavior — this is the speculative, research-heavy part. It's where the proposal lives, but it's also where technical viability is weakest.\n- The conflation of these two different problems (deterministic logging vs. generative documentation synthesis) in one proposal muddies what we'd actually be building and when we'd get value.\n\n**Decision:**\nFrom my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n- The core \"LLM generates compliance documentation from raw behavior\" is not technically viable near-term as a reliable, auditable solution. It's genuinely open research with no clear path to the quality bar that EU regulation demands (the documentation must enable traceability and compliance verification — an LLM hallucination in that context is a regulatory failure).\n- The \"real-time\" aspect makes it even less tractable for prototyping.\n- However, there IS a useful seed here: if we carve out the deterministic logging requirements (Article 12(3) specifics) from the speculative generative documentation part, the logging side could be prototyped quickly and would genuinely reduce compliance overhead during development cycles.\n- But as PROPOSED — as a single R&D project treating \"compliance as an ML problem\" with LLMs generating real-time documentation — it's too speculative, too long-horizon, and the technical risk is very high for our prototyping timeline.\n\nI think I should reject this but give it a moderate promise score because there's a useful kernel (the logging/telemetry automation part) that could be extracted into something more tractable. The LLM-documentation-generation framing as proposed is not viable on any near-term timescale and the \"research\" framing means no clear prototype deliverable.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 35-40. There's a real problem being identified (compliance overhead during prototyping), but the proposed solution approach has significant technical viability issues, and as a research project it doesn't serve our need for speed in producing working prototypes. The deterministic logging part is viable but doesn't need an \"R&D project\" — it needs engineering.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 35.\n",
