        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1458\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises Article 12(2)(a)–(c) by converting the mandated logs into an active detection and escalation mechanism tied to our risk-management framework, with a clear notification path to the compliance office — precisely the evidence trail that distinguishes demonstrable due diligence from passive record-keeping in a regulatory inspection or incident investigation. From my vantage point (institutional survival, liability containment, market reputation), having documented real-time anomaly detection with defined triggers and escalation is the single most defensible posture we can present to notifiers, clients, and auditors when an Article 79(1) event does occur; the main caveat is that 'real-time' analytics on top of logging infrastructure represents a meaningful resource commitment beyond the bare minimum the Act mandates for record-keeping alone, but the compliance upside far outweighs that cost.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 89\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as the Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1458 describes a \"Post-Market Risk Detection System\" — an analytics layer that monitors system logs in real-time to identify patterns indicating potential risks or substantial modifications. It includes telemetry dashboards, automated alerts triggered by anomalies defined in the risk management framework, notifications to the compliance office of potential Article 79(1) events, and frames it as proactive liability mitigation and reputation safeguarding.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(2)(a): \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification\" — the proposal explicitly references Article 79(1) events.\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(b): facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72 — \"Post-Market Risk Detection System\" is literally about this.\n- The idea of turning passive logging into active detection aligns with the regulatory intent in Recital 71: \"monitoring of their operations and post market monitoring.\"\n- Automated alerts to the compliance office create a clear accountability chain — this is exactly what regulators expect.\n- Defining triggers in the risk management framework ties back to Article 9 (risk management system) requirements, creating internal coherence.\n- From a liability perspective, having documented evidence that you proactively monitored and detected anomalies is critical for demonstrating \"due diligence\" under the Act's governance requirements.\n\n**Concerns/Considerations:**\n- This goes beyond mere \"record-keeping\" — Article 12 requires technical capability to log; this proposal builds an analytics layer ON TOP of those logs. That's a scope expansion. However, from my vantage point (compliance and institutional survival), the ability to actually USE those logs for detection is what makes them meaningful for compliance. Without active monitoring, you have logs but no mechanism to act on them, which weakens your position in a regulatory inquiry.\n- The proposal says \"real-time\" — this is ambitious and may require significant resources. But from my perspective, the regulatory requirement doesn't mandate real-time; it mandates the capability to record. However, having near-real-time detection significantly strengthens our compliance posture.\n- \"Anomalies defined in our risk management framework\" — this is good because it ties back to Article 9's requirements and creates a defensible audit trail for what constitutes an anomaly.\n- The notification mechanism to the compliance office ensures I (as Compliance Officer) am in the loop, which is essential for my function.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:** If a high-risk AI system causes harm, having a documented system that was actively monitoring and detecting anomalies (and had clear escalation paths) dramatically reduces our legal exposure. It demonstrates we went beyond minimum compliance. In regulatory inspections, this shows maturity.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:** Clients and partners will increasingly ask about our post-market safety monitoring capabilities. Having this system is a competitive differentiator and trust signal.\n\n**Resource commitment concern:** This is a significant build — analytics layer, dashboards, automated alerting, anomaly detection tied to risk framework. It's not a trivial undertaking. But from my vantage point, the compliance value justifies it. The alternative — having logs but no active monitoring mechanism — leaves us vulnerable in any incident investigation.\n\n**Verdict:** This is strongly aligned with my function's priorities. It transforms Article 12 from a passive obligation into an active compliance asset. It directly serves Articles 79(1), 72, and 26(5) as referenced in the regulation itself. The notification chain to compliance ensures institutional control. I approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my function. I'd give it a high score — maybe 88-92. It's not perfect (the \"real-time\" ambition could be over-scoped, and the proposal doesn't explicitly address log retention periods or data protection concerns for the logs themselves), but the core value proposition is exactly what compliance needs. I'll go with 89.\n",
