        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2946\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12(1) imposes an unconditional mandate for automatic logging over the system's lifetime, and Article 12(3) specifies four minimum data points for Annex III biometric-ID systems; understanding the compute and latency costs of meeting those floors at production scale is a genuine enabler for institutional survival, because it lets us size infrastructure so that compliance doesn't quietly become unaffordable as we deploy more high-risk systems. The main residual risk from my vantage point is that 'optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency' could drift toward under-logging under operational pressure; the proposal must be explicitly scoped so that research optimises architecture, storage, and compression around a fixed regulatory floor—not the floor itself.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 64\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2946 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**P2946: \"Efficiency Research for Traceable AI Architectures\"**\n\nThis proposes a focused research project investigating how logging overhead impacts system latency and compute resource consumption. The goal is to benchmark different logging granularities against performance metrics to find the optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency.\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Arguments in favor (approve):**\n- Article 12(1) mandates that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a hard requirement, not optional. Understanding logging overhead helps ensure we can implement compliance without breaking our systems.\n- The proposal explicitly frames itself around meeting \"regulatory requirements\" — it's about ensuring we CAN comply while maintaining operational viability.\n- For institutional survival: if logging is so expensive that it makes running high-risk AI systems impractical, we face a business problem. Understanding the overhead helps us plan resources and ensure we can sustain compliance long-term.\n- Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)). If we're in that category, understanding what's feasible at scale is important.\n- It helps us demonstrate to regulators that our logging implementation is \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" (Article 12(2)) — if we can show we've thought through the tradeoffs.\n\n**Arguments against (reject):**\n- This is fundamentally an efficiency/optimization research project, not a compliance project per se. The regulation doesn't ask us to minimize logging overhead; it asks us to have logs. Optimizing for latency is more of an engineering/operations concern than a compliance one.\n- The risk here: if we're \"finding the optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency,\" there's a danger that the research could be used as justification to under-log — to log just enough to technically pass but not enough to be genuinely useful for traceability, post-market monitoring, or incident investigation. From a compliance standpoint, I'd rather err on the side of over-logging.\n- However, the proposal says \"optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency\" — it's framed as meeting requirements while being efficient, not cutting corners. That's reasonable.\n- The research is about understanding overhead to make compliance feasible at scale, which supports institutional survival (we need to be able to run these systems).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a legitimate support function for compliance. I don't need to approve every research project that touches on logging infrastructure — but this one has clear relevance: Article 12 mandates automatic logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which implies long-term, potentially massive log storage and processing. If we don't understand the cost implications, we risk either (a) under-investing in logging infrastructure and failing compliance at scale, or (b) over-logging to an extent that makes our systems uncompetitive.\n\nFrom a market reputation standpoint: if we can demonstrate we've thought through the engineering of our compliance infrastructure, it shows regulatory maturity. But this is secondary.\n\nThe main concern I have as Compliance Officer: the framing \"optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency\" could be misused. If researchers find that logging at full Article 12(3) granularity costs too much latency, the natural pressure will be to reduce below required levels. The compliance bar is not \"minimum viable logging\" — it's full compliance with Articles 12(1)-(3), including all four minimum data points for Annex III point 1(a) systems. Efficiency research should inform HOW we meet those requirements (architecture choices, storage solutions, compression, etc.), not WHETHER we meet them.\n\nHowever, the proposal as written doesn't suggest cutting below regulatory floors — it says \"between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency,\" implying the regulatory floor is fixed and the research finds efficient ways to meet it. That's acceptable.\n\nI'll approve this with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's useful for ensuring compliance is sustainable at scale (institutional survival), but it's not directly a compliance deliverable — it's an enabler. I'm not 100% because the primary risk I see (under-logging under guise of efficiency) isn't fully mitigated in the proposal text, and it's more engineering research than compliance work per se. But it clears my bar as something that supports institutional survival while maintaining compliance.\n\nI'll give it around 62-68. It's useful but not critical, and the primary compliance obligation (implementing logs) would exist regardless of this research.\n",
